🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is Clean Architecture?</p> <p>An architecture of concentric circles (entities, use cases, interface adapters, frameworks) where dependencies only point inward.</p> <p>Q2. What is the Dependency Rule?</p> <p>Inner circles must not know anything about outer circles: no imports, no names, no data formats.</p> <p>Q3. Entity vs. use case?</p> <p>Entities hold enterprise-wide rules; use cases hold application-specific workflows that use entities.</p> <p>Q4. What does a presenter do?</p> <p>Converts the use case's response model into a view model for a specific UI or API.</p> <p>Q5. Why is it testable?</p> <p>Business rules don't depend on frameworks, databases or UIs, so they can be tested with plain unit tests.</p> <p>Q1. How does the interactor call the presenter without depending on it?</p> <p>Through an output boundary interface defined in the use-case circle and implemented by the presenter (dependency inversion).</p> <p>Q2. Where do Spring annotations go?</p> <p>In the outer circles: controllers, configuration, gateway implementations. Use cases are wired as beans from configuration.</p> <p>Q3. Clean vs. Hexagonal?</p> <p>Same dependency principle; Clean names more circles and adds presenters and request/response models; Hexagonal focuses on ports/adapters.</p> <p>Q4. What is "screaming architecture"?</p> <p>The top-level structure shows what the system does (use cases, domain) rather than which framework it uses.</p> <p>Q5. How do you handle validation?</p> <p>Input format validation in controllers/request models; business invariants in entities; use-case preconditions in interactors.</p> <p>Q1. "Clean Architecture for a CRUD admin panel: yes or no?"</p> <p>No: the ceremony costs more than it saves. Use it where rules are complex and long-lived.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you avoid mapping fatigue?"</p> <p>Pragmatic shortcuts for reads (query services returning DTOs directly), MapStruct for mechanical mapping, and full rigour for complex write paths.</p> <p>Q3. "Where does the transaction boundary go?"</p> <p>Around the use case, applied from outside (decorator/AOP) so the interactor stays framework-free.</p> <p>Q4. "How do you introduce Clean Architecture into an existing system?"</p> <p>Pick a high-value, high-change area; extract entities and use cases with tests; move framework code outward; enforce with ArchUnit for that package; expand gradually.</p> <p>Q5. "Sealed results vs. exceptions for business outcomes?"</p> <p>Sealed results make expected outcomes explicit and compiler-checked; exceptions are for unexpected failures. Java 21 pattern matching makes results ergonomic.</p> <p>Problem: Add transactions, logging and role checks to use cases without putting any of that code inside the interactors. Use a generic <code>UseCase&lt;I, O&gt;</code> interface and decorators applied in the composition root.</p> Solution UseCaseDecorators.javaDecoratorsDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.clean.crosscutting;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

/**
 * Coding challenge: add transactions, logging and authorization to every use case WITHOUT putting
 * framework code in the interactors. Each concern is a decorator around a generic UseCase interface,
 * applied in the composition root (the outermost circle).
 */
public final class UseCaseDecorators {

    private UseCaseDecorators() {
    }

    @FunctionalInterface
    public interface UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; {
        O execute(I input);
    }

    /** Stands in for a database transaction manager. */
    public static final class TxManager {
        public final List&lt;String&gt; log = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

        void begin() {
            log.add("BEGIN");
        }

        void commit() {
            log.add("COMMIT");
        }

        void rollback() {
            log.add("ROLLBACK");
        }
    }

    public static &lt;I, O&gt; UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; transactional(UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; inner, TxManager tx) {
        return input -&gt; {
            tx.begin();
            try {
                O out = inner.execute(input);
                tx.commit();
                return out;
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                tx.rollback();
                throw e;
            }
        };
    }

    public static &lt;I, O&gt; UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; logged(String name, UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; inner) {
        return input -&gt; {
            System.out.println("    -&gt; " + name + "(" + input + ")");
            O out = inner.execute(input);
            System.out.println("    &lt;- " + name + " = " + out);
            return out;
        };
    }

    public static &lt;I, O&gt; UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; requiresRole(String role, Supplier&lt;String&gt; currentRole,
                                                   UseCase&lt;I, O&gt; inner) {
        return input -&gt; {
            if (!role.equals(currentRole.get())) {
                throw new SecurityException("role " + role + " required");
            }
            return inner.execute(input);
        };
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.clean.crosscutting;

import com.handbook.architecture.clean.crosscutting.UseCaseDecorators.TxManager;
import com.handbook.architecture.clean.crosscutting.UseCaseDecorators.UseCase;

public class DecoratorsDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // The interactor: pure business logic, no transactions, no logging, no security.
        UseCase&lt;Long, String&gt; issueRefund = paise -&gt; {
            if (paise &gt; 500_000) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("refunds over ₹5,000 need a manager");
            }
            return "refund of ₹" + paise / 100 + " issued";
        };

        TxManager tx = new TxManager();
        String[] role = {"SUPPORT_AGENT"};
        UseCase&lt;Long, String&gt; wired = UseCaseDecorators.logged("IssueRefund",
                UseCaseDecorators.requiresRole("SUPPORT_AGENT", () -&gt; role[0],
                        UseCaseDecorators.transactional(issueRefund, tx)));

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: cross-cutting concerns as decorators at the edge ===");
        wired.execute(29_900L);
        try {
            wired.execute(900_000L);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("    failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        role[0] = "WAREHOUSE";
        try {
            wired.execute(10_000L);
        } catch (SecurityException e) {
            System.out.println("    denied: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        System.out.println("transaction log: " + tx.log);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: cross-cutting concerns as decorators at the edge ===
    -&gt; IssueRefund(29900)
    &lt;- IssueRefund = refund of ₹299 issued
    -&gt; IssueRefund(900000)
    failed: refunds over ₹5,000 need a manager
    -&gt; IssueRefund(10000)
    denied: role SUPPORT_AGENT required
transaction log: [BEGIN, COMMIT, BEGIN, ROLLBACK]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: decorator order matters (authorization before opening a transaction; logging outermost); the failed refund rolls back, and the denied call never opens a transaction. Spring AOP (<code>@Transactional</code>, <code>@PreAuthorize</code>) does the same with proxies.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our lending app's eligibility rules were scattered across Angular, Spring controllers and stored procedures; customers saw different answers on web and mobile.</li> <li>T: One authoritative implementation of eligibility rules.</li> <li>A: Introduced entities (<code>Applicant</code>, <code>LoanProduct</code>) and use cases (<code>CheckEligibility</code>) as plain Java with 400 unit tests; channels became presenters; removed rule logic from the UI and procedures.</li> <li>R: Channel discrepancies dropped to zero; rule changes went from 3 weeks (three codebases) to 2 days.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is event-driven architecture?</p> <p>Components communicate by publishing and consuming events through a broker, instead of calling each other directly.</p> <p>Q2. What is an event?</p> <p>An immutable record of something that happened, named in the past tense (<code>OrderPlaced</code>).</p> <p>Q3. Name some message brokers.</p> <p>Apache Kafka, RabbitMQ, ActiveMQ, AWS SNS/SQS, Google Pub/Sub, Azure Service Bus.</p> <p>Q4. What is a consumer group in Kafka?</p> <p>A set of consumers sharing a topic's partitions; each group gets all events, and each partition is read by one member of the group.</p> <p>Q5. Main advantage?</p> <p>Loose coupling: producers don't know consumers, and new consumers can be added without changes.</p> <p>Q1. Event notification vs. event-carried state transfer?</p> <p>Notification carries an ID (consumers call back for details); state transfer carries the data consumers need.</p> <p>Q2. How is ordering guaranteed in Kafka?</p> <p>Only within a partition; events with the same key go to the same partition.</p> <p>Q3. What is a dead-letter queue?</p> <p>A topic/queue where messages go after failing processing repeatedly, so they don't block others and can be inspected or replayed.</p> <p>Q4. Event time vs. processing time?</p> <p>Event time is when it happened; processing time is when it's processed. Windows by event time handle late data correctly.</p> <p>Q5. How do you handle duplicate events?</p> <p>Idempotent consumers (dedupe by event ID, idempotent operations).</p> <p>Q1. "Design a real-time notification system for 10M users."</p> <p>Domain events → Kafka (keyed by user) → notification service consumer groups by channel (push/SMS/email), preference service lookup (cached), rate limits per user, idempotency per notification key, DLQs, provider adapters with retries, delivery status events back for analytics.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you evolve event schemas safely?"</p> <p>Schema registry with compatibility (backward/forward), additive changes, new event versions or topics for breaking changes, consumers as tolerant readers, and deprecation windows.</p> <p>Q3. "When would you NOT use event-driven?"</p> <p>When the user needs an immediate answer that depends on the result (a synchronous query/command), for simple CRUD, or when the team lacks the operational maturity for brokers.</p> <p>Q4. "Broker vs. mediator topology?"</p> <p>Broker: choreography, maximum decoupling, harder to see flows. Mediator: an orchestrator controls steps, easier to manage complex workflows and errors.</p> <p>Q5. "Consumer lag is growing. What do you check?"</p> <p>Throughput per partition vs. produce rate, slow downstream calls in handlers, rebalancing storms, poison messages, partition skew (hot keys), and whether more partitions/instances or batching are needed.</p> <p>Problem: A poison message must not block the stream. Retry each event up to 3 times; transient failures should succeed on retry, but an event that keeps failing goes to a dead-letter queue with its error, and processing continues.</p> Solution RetryingConsumer.javaDeadLetterDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.eventdriven.dlq;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

/**
 * Coding challenge: a poison message must not block the partition forever. Retry a few times; if
 * it still fails, park it on a dead-letter queue with the error, and move on to the next message.
 */
public final class RetryingConsumer&lt;E&gt; {

    public record DeadLetter&lt;E&gt;(E event, String error, int attempts) {
    }

    private final Consumer&lt;E&gt; handler;
    private final int maxAttempts;
    private final List&lt;DeadLetter&lt;E&gt;&gt; deadLetters = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    public RetryingConsumer(Consumer&lt;E&gt; handler, int maxAttempts) {
        this.handler = handler;
        this.maxAttempts = maxAttempts;
    }

    public void consume(List&lt;E&gt; partition) {
        for (E event : partition) {
            for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {
                try {
                    handler.accept(event);
                    break;
                } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                    if (attempt == maxAttempts) {
                        deadLetters.add(new DeadLetter&lt;&gt;(event, e.getMessage(), attempt));
                        System.out.println("    -&gt; DLQ after " + attempt + " attempts: " + event);
                        break;
                    }
                    System.out.println("    attempt " + attempt + " failed for " + event + ": " + e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public List&lt;DeadLetter&lt;E&gt;&gt; deadLetters() {
        return List.copyOf(deadLetters);
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.eventdriven.dlq;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class DeadLetterDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;String&gt; invoiced = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        int[] flakyFailures = {1};
        var consumer = new RetryingConsumer&lt;String&gt;(event -&gt; {
            if (event.contains("amount=abc")) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("cannot parse amount"); // poison: will never succeed
            }
            if (event.startsWith("ORD-3") &amp;&amp; flakyFailures[0]-- &gt; 0) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("invoice DB timeout"); // transient: succeeds on retry
            }
            invoiced.add(event.substring(0, 5));
        }, 3);

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: retries + dead-letter queue for poison messages ===");
        consumer.consume(List.of("ORD-1 amount=1447", "ORD-2 amount=abc", "ORD-3 amount=129", "ORD-4 amount=399"));
        System.out.println("invoiced: " + invoiced);
        System.out.println("dead letters: " + consumer.deadLetters());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: retries + dead-letter queue for poison messages ===
    attempt 1 failed for ORD-2 amount=abc: cannot parse amount
    attempt 2 failed for ORD-2 amount=abc: cannot parse amount
    -&gt; DLQ after 3 attempts: ORD-2 amount=abc
    attempt 1 failed for ORD-3 amount=129: invoice DB timeout
invoiced: [ORD-1, ORD-3, ORD-4]
dead letters: [DeadLetter[event=ORD-2 amount=abc, error=cannot parse amount, attempts=3]]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: distinguish transient (retry with backoff) from permanent (DLQ immediately) errors when you can; keep the original event and error in the DLQ for replay after a fix; Spring Kafka's <code>DefaultErrorHandler</code> + <code>DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer</code> implements exactly this.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Checkout called 7 downstream services synchronously; p99 latency was 2.8 s and any outage broke ordering.</li> <li>T: Make checkout fast and resilient while keeping all downstream reactions.</li> <li>A: Kept inventory reservation synchronous, moved loyalty, email, analytics and recommendations to <code>OrderPlaced</code> consumers via an outbox and Kafka; added DLQs, lag alerts and tracing.</li> <li>R: Checkout p99 went to 450 ms; three downstream outages over the next quarter had zero checkout impact.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is hexagonal architecture?</p> <p>An architecture where the business core is isolated and talks to the outside world only through ports (interfaces), with adapters implementing them for specific technologies.</p> <p>Q2. What is a port? What is an adapter?</p> <p>A port is an interface owned by the core (what it offers or needs); an adapter is a concrete implementation for a technology (REST, JPA, Kafka, a vendor API).</p> <p>Q3. Driving vs. driven adapters?</p> <p>Driving adapters call into the core (controllers, listeners, tests); driven adapters are called by the core (databases, external APIs).</p> <p>Q4. Main benefit?</p> <p>The core can be tested and changed without infrastructure, and technologies can be swapped by writing new adapters.</p> <p>Q5. Why "hexagon"?</p> <p>Just a drawing convention showing many sides (ports); the number six has no meaning.</p> <p>Q1. Where do JPA entities go?</p> <p>In the persistence adapter, mapped to domain objects, or pragmatically in the domain if the team accepts the coupling.</p> <p>Q2. How do you wire it in Spring?</p> <p>Keep core classes free of annotations and create them as beans in a <code>@Configuration</code> class, injecting adapters for the ports.</p> <p>Q3. How do you test each part?</p> <p>Core: unit tests with fakes. Adapters: integration tests (Testcontainers, WireMock). Plus contract tests per port and a few end-to-end tests.</p> <p>Q4. Hexagonal vs. layered?</p> <p>Layered has business depending on persistence; hexagonal inverts that so both depend on ports owned by the core.</p> <p>Q5. How do you enforce the rules?</p> <p>ArchUnit (<code>onionArchitecture()</code> or custom <code>noClasses()</code> rules), separate Maven/Gradle modules, or jMolecules.</p> <p>Q1. "When is hexagonal not worth it?"</p> <p>Small CRUD apps or short-lived prototypes, where mapping and extra interfaces cost more than they save.</p> <p>Q2. "How do transactions work across ports?"</p> <p>The use case is the transaction boundary; the transaction manager is applied around it (AOP/decorator), and repository adapters join it. Cross-system consistency needs sagas/outbox.</p> <p>Q3. "How do hexagons relate to microservices?"</p> <p>Each service can be a hexagon; inter-service clients are driven adapters and API endpoints/listeners are driving adapters. An ACL is a special driven adapter.</p> <p>Q4. "Hexagonal vs. Clean: real differences?"</p> <p>Clean adds explicit circles and the presenter/output-boundary idea; hexagonal is simpler and symmetric. Both enforce inward dependencies.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you prevent port explosion?"</p> <p>Group by capability, keep ports coarse-grained around use cases, and only introduce a port where you need substitution or isolation.</p> <p>Problem: Fakes make core tests fast, but only if they behave like the real adapter. Write a contract that any <code>PaymentPort</code> adapter must pass, and run it against the real PayFast adapter (with a stubbed HTTP API), a correct fake, and a buggy fake.</p> Solution PaymentPortContract.javaContractDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.hexagonal.contract;

import com.handbook.architecture.hexagonal.checkout.port.out.PaymentPort;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Coding challenge: a contract every PaymentPort adapter must satisfy, run against the real adapter
 * (with a stubbed HTTP API) and the in-memory test adapter. If both pass, tests of the core that use
 * the fake are trustworthy.
 */
public final class PaymentPortContract {

    private PaymentPortContract() {
    }

    /** Assumes the adapter's environment accepts amounts up to ₹5,000 and declines above. */
    public static List&lt;String&gt; verify(String name, PaymentPort adapter) {
        List&lt;String&gt; results = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        check(results, name, "small payment is captured", adapter.charge("C1", 10_000, "R1"));
        check(results, name, "payment above the limit is declined", !adapter.charge("C1", 900_000, "R2"));
        check(results, name, "boundary ₹5,000.00 is captured", adapter.charge("C1", 500_000, "R3"));
        return results;
    }

    private static void check(List&lt;String&gt; results, String adapter, String rule, boolean ok) {
        results.add((ok ? "PASS " : "FAIL ") + adapter + ": " + rule);
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.hexagonal.contract;

import com.handbook.architecture.hexagonal.checkout.adapter.out.PayFastPaymentAdapter;
import com.handbook.architecture.hexagonal.checkout.port.out.PaymentPort;

import java.util.Map;

public class ContractDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PaymentPort payFast = new PayFastPaymentAdapter(req -&gt;
                Map.of("status", Double.parseDouble(req.get("amount")) &lt;= 5_000 ? "CAPTURED" : "DECLINED_LIMIT"));
        PaymentPort fake = (customer, paise, ref) -&gt; paise &lt;= 500_000;
        PaymentPort buggyFake = (customer, paise, ref) -&gt; paise &lt; 500_000; // off-by-one at the boundary

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: one contract test suite for every PaymentPort adapter ===");
        PaymentPortContract.verify("PayFastAdapter", payFast).forEach(System.out::println);
        PaymentPortContract.verify("InMemoryFake", fake).forEach(System.out::println);
        PaymentPortContract.verify("BuggyFake", buggyFake).forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: one contract test suite for every PaymentPort adapter ===
PASS PayFastAdapter: small payment is captured
PASS PayFastAdapter: payment above the limit is declined
PASS PayFastAdapter: boundary ₹5,000.00 is captured
PASS InMemoryFake: small payment is captured
PASS InMemoryFake: payment above the limit is declined
PASS InMemoryFake: boundary ₹5,000.00 is captured
PASS BuggyFake: small payment is captured
PASS BuggyFake: payment above the limit is declined
FAIL BuggyFake: boundary ₹5,000.00 is captured
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the contract encodes the port's behavioural expectations (including boundaries); in JUnit, write it as an abstract test class with one subclass per adapter. The buggy fake fails the boundary case, which would otherwise let core tests pass incorrectly.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our order service's tests took 25 minutes because every test booted Spring and a database; developers stopped running them.</li> <li>T: Fast, reliable feedback on business logic.</li> <li>A: Refactored the core into a hexagon: ports for persistence, payments and notifications; moved JPA and vendor SDKs into adapters; added contract tests; kept a slim set of Testcontainers integration tests.</li> <li>R: 900 core tests ran in 8 seconds; the full suite dropped to 4 minutes; a later payment-provider switch took 2 weeks.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is layered architecture?</p> <p>Organizing an application into layers (presentation, business, persistence) where each layer depends only on layers below it.</p> <p>Q2. What does each Spring stereotype represent?</p> <p><code>@Controller</code>/<code>@RestController</code>: presentation; <code>@Service</code>: business logic; <code>@Repository</code>: data access (with exception translation).</p> <p>Q3. Why shouldn't controllers contain business logic?</p> <p>It can't be reused by other channels, is hard to test, and mixes HTTP concerns with rules.</p> <p>Q4. What is a DTO and why use one?</p> <p>A Data Transfer Object shapes data for the API, decoupling it from internal entities.</p> <p>Q5. Where does <code>@Transactional</code> usually go?</p> <p>On service-layer methods, because a use case is one unit of work.</p> <p>Q1. Closed vs. open layers?</p> <p>Closed layers must be passed through; open layers can be skipped. Opening read-only layers avoids pass-through services.</p> <p>Q2. What is the architecture sinkhole anti-pattern?</p> <p>Requests passing through layers that add no logic, just forwarding.</p> <p>Q3. Package by layer vs. package by feature?</p> <p>By layer groups all controllers together; by feature groups everything for "orders" together. Feature packaging scales better and keeps related code close.</p> <p>Q4. How do you enforce layering?</p> <p>ArchUnit tests in CI (or JPMS modules / Maven modules).</p> <p>Q5. What's wrong with returning JPA entities from controllers?</p> <p>Couples the API to the schema, risks lazy-loading exceptions and over-exposure of fields (and mass-assignment issues on input).</p> <p>Q1. "When would you choose layered over hexagonal?"</p> <p>Simple CRUD domains, small teams, short-lived apps, or where the framework's conventions give most value. Hexagonal pays off with complex logic and many integrations.</p> <p>Q2. "A change to add a field touches 7 files across layers. Problem?"</p> <p>It's typical of layered designs (shotgun surgery). Mitigate with package by feature, fewer mapping layers for simple reads, or generated mappers; question whether all layers add value.</p> <p>Q3. "How do layers relate to tiers?"</p> <p>Layers are logical code organization; tiers are physical deployment units. Several layers can run in one tier.</p> <p>Q4. "How would you migrate a big layered monolith toward modules?"</p> <p>Re-package by feature, identify module boundaries, introduce module APIs, forbid cross-module access to internals with ArchUnit, then split data ownership.</p> <p>Q5. "What's the dependency inversion step from layered to hexagonal?"</p> <p>The service defines repository/gateway interfaces it needs; persistence implements them. Now business code doesn't depend on persistence code.</p> <p>Problem: Given layer definitions (package prefixes, top to bottom) and a list of class dependencies, report every dependency that goes upward (lower layer depending on a higher one). Downward skips are allowed (relaxed layering).</p> Solution LayerRules.javaLayerRulesDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.layered.rules;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Coding challenge: a tiny version of what ArchUnit's layeredArchitecture() checks. Layers are
 * ordered top to bottom; a class may depend only on its own layer or the layers below it.
 */
public final class LayerRules {

    public record Dependency(String fromClass, String toClass) {
    }

    private final Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; layerRank = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;(); // package prefix → rank

    public LayerRules layer(String packagePrefix) {
        layerRank.put(packagePrefix, layerRank.size());
        return this;
    }

    public List&lt;String&gt; violations(List&lt;Dependency&gt; dependencies) {
        List&lt;String&gt; violations = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        for (Dependency d : dependencies) {
            Integer from = rank(d.fromClass());
            Integer to = rank(d.toClass());
            if (from != null &amp;&amp; to != null &amp;&amp; to &lt; from) {
                violations.add(simple(d.fromClass()) + " -&gt; " + simple(d.toClass())
                        + " (lower layer depends on a higher one)");
            }
        }
        return violations;
    }

    private Integer rank(String className) {
        return layerRank.entrySet().stream()
                .filter(e -&gt; className.startsWith(e.getKey()))
                .map(Map.Entry::getValue)
                .findFirst().orElse(null);
    }

    private static String simple(String className) {
        return className.substring(className.lastIndexOf('.', className.lastIndexOf('.') - 1) + 1);
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.layered.rules;

import com.handbook.architecture.layered.rules.LayerRules.Dependency;

import java.util.List;

public class LayerRulesDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LayerRules rules = new LayerRules().layer("app.web").layer("app.service").layer("app.repository");

        List&lt;Dependency&gt; deps = List.of(
                new Dependency("app.web.OrderController", "app.service.OrderService"),
                new Dependency("app.service.OrderService", "app.repository.OrderRepository"),
                new Dependency("app.web.OrderController", "app.repository.OrderRepository"), // skips a layer
                new Dependency("app.repository.OrderRepository", "app.web.OrderResponse"),   // upward!
                new Dependency("app.service.PricingService", "app.web.Pagination"));         // upward!

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: detect layer violations ===");
        List&lt;String&gt; violations = rules.violations(deps);
        violations.forEach(v -&gt; System.out.println("VIOLATION " + v));
        System.out.println(violations.size() + " violations; skipping a layer downward is allowed in relaxed layering.");
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: detect layer violations ===
VIOLATION repository.OrderRepository -&gt; web.OrderResponse (lower layer depends on a higher one)
VIOLATION service.PricingService -&gt; web.Pagination (lower layer depends on a higher one)
2 violations; skipping a layer downward is allowed in relaxed layering.
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: rank layers by order; a dependency is a violation if <code>rank(to) &lt; rank(from)</code>. Real tools (ArchUnit, jQAssistant) extract dependencies from bytecode; this exercise focuses on the rule.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A 6-year-old Spring app had business rules spread across controllers, services and SQL stored procedures; onboarding took months.</li> <li>T: Make the codebase predictable without a rewrite.</li> <li>A: Agreed layer rules with the team, moved rules into services feature by feature, introduced DTOs, and added ArchUnit tests that failed the build on violations (with a baseline of existing ones that shrank every sprint).</li> <li>R: Violations went from 340 to 0 in five months; new developers shipped their first feature in their second week.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a modular monolith?</p> <p>A single deployable application divided into well-defined business modules with enforced boundaries.</p> <p>Q2. How is it different from a normal monolith?</p> <p>A normal monolith often has no enforced boundaries (any code calls any code); a modular monolith restricts modules to each other's public APIs.</p> <p>Q3. Why not start with microservices?</p> <p>Microservices add network, deployment and data-consistency complexity; boundaries are hard to get right early. A modular monolith keeps things simple while boundaries are learned.</p> <p>Q4. How do modules communicate?</p> <p>Through public APIs (method calls) and in-process events.</p> <p>Q5. What is Spring Modulith?</p> <p>A Spring project that detects, verifies, tests and documents modules in a Spring Boot application, and supports reliable module events.</p> <p>Q1. How do you enforce module boundaries in Java?</p> <p>Package-private internals, ArchUnit/Spring Modulith verification, separate build modules, or JPMS.</p> <p>Q2. How do you handle data in a modular monolith?</p> <p>Each module owns its tables (ideally its own schema); no cross-module joins; other modules use its API or events.</p> <p>Q3. When do you extract a module into a service?</p> <p>When it needs independent scaling, deployment cadence, team ownership, technology or compliance isolation.</p> <p>Q4. How do module events differ from Kafka events?</p> <p>They're in-process (and can be transactional); with an event publication registry they're reliable. Semantically they should look like integration events so extraction is easy.</p> <p>Q5. What's a sign of a bad module boundary?</p> <p>Most changes touch several modules together, or modules call each other in long synchronous chains.</p> <p>Q1. "A CTO wants to move a 1M-line monolith to microservices. Your advice?"</p> <p>First modularize in place: map capabilities, enforce boundaries, split data ownership, then extract only modules with a clear reason, using the strangler approach. Measure benefits along the way.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you handle a cross-module transaction?"</p> <p>Within the monolith, a local transaction is allowed if both modules agree, but prefer events with eventual consistency to keep extraction possible.</p> <p>Q3. "How would you test module boundaries and behaviour?"</p> <p>Verification tests (Spring Modulith <code>verify()</code>), module-slice integration tests (<code>@ApplicationModuleTest</code>) that bootstrap one module with mocks for others, plus scenario tests that publish events.</p> <p>Q4. "How does Conway's law apply?"</p> <p>Module boundaries should match team boundaries; one team per module (or a few modules) reduces coordination.</p> <p>Q5. "What tooling would you put in CI?"</p> <p>Module verification, cycle checks, ArchUnit rules for internal packages, schema access checks, and generated module documentation (C4/PlantUML from Spring Modulith).</p> <p>Problem: Modules must form a DAG. Detect a dependency cycle and print it, or, if there's none, compute a valid build/initialization order with dependencies first.</p> Solution ModuleGraph.javaCycleDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.modular.cycles;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.TreeMap;
import java.util.TreeSet;

/**
 * Coding challenge: modules must form a DAG. Find a dependency cycle if there is one (DFS with
 * colours); otherwise return a valid build/initialization order (dependencies first).
 */
public final class ModuleGraph {

    private final Map&lt;String, TreeSet&lt;String&gt;&gt; edges = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();

    public ModuleGraph dependsOn(String module, String... dependencies) {
        edges.computeIfAbsent(module, m -&gt; new TreeSet&lt;&gt;()).addAll(List.of(dependencies));
        for (String d : dependencies) {
            edges.computeIfAbsent(d, m -&gt; new TreeSet&lt;&gt;());
        }
        return this;
    }

    public Optional&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; findCycle() {
        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; colour = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;(); // 0 = new, 1 = on stack, 2 = done
        Deque&lt;String&gt; path = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
        for (String start : edges.keySet()) {
            List&lt;String&gt; cycle = dfs(start, colour, path);
            if (cycle != null) {
                return Optional.of(cycle);
            }
        }
        return Optional.empty();
    }

    private List&lt;String&gt; dfs(String node, Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; colour, Deque&lt;String&gt; path) {
        int c = colour.getOrDefault(node, 0);
        if (c == 2) {
            return null;
        }
        if (c == 1) { // back edge: the path from node to here is a cycle
            List&lt;String&gt; cycle = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
            boolean in = false;
            for (var it = path.descendingIterator(); it.hasNext(); ) {
                String n = it.next();
                in |= n.equals(node);
                if (in) {
                    cycle.add(n);
                }
            }
            cycle.add(node);
            return cycle;
        }
        colour.put(node, 1);
        path.push(node);
        for (String next : edges.get(node)) {
            List&lt;String&gt; cycle = dfs(next, colour, path);
            if (cycle != null) {
                return cycle;
            }
        }
        path.pop();
        colour.put(node, 2);
        return null;
    }

    /** Dependencies before dependents (post-order DFS). Call only when there is no cycle. */
    public List&lt;String&gt; buildOrder() {
        List&lt;String&gt; order = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        Map&lt;String, Boolean&gt; seen = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();
        for (String m : edges.keySet()) {
            visit(m, seen, order);
        }
        return order;
    }

    private void visit(String m, Map&lt;String, Boolean&gt; seen, List&lt;String&gt; order) {
        if (seen.putIfAbsent(m, true) != null) {
            return;
        }
        edges.get(m).forEach(d -&gt; visit(d, seen, order));
        order.add(m);
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.architecture.modular.cycles;

public class CycleDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: detect module cycles, compute a build order ===");
        ModuleGraph healthy = new ModuleGraph()
                .dependsOn("ordering", "catalog", "customers")
                .dependsOn("billing", "customers")
                .dependsOn("shipping", "ordering");
        System.out.println("cycle: " + healthy.findCycle().map(Object::toString).orElse("none")
                + ", build order: " + healthy.buildOrder());

        ModuleGraph tangled = new ModuleGraph()
                .dependsOn("ordering", "catalog", "billing")
                .dependsOn("billing", "customers", "shipping")
                .dependsOn("shipping", "ordering");
        System.out.println("cycle: " + tangled.findCycle().map(c -&gt; String.join(" -&gt; ", c)).orElse("none")
                + "  (break it with an event or by moving the shared concept)");
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: detect module cycles, compute a build order ===
cycle: none, build order: [customers, billing, catalog, ordering, shipping]
cycle: billing -&gt; shipping -&gt; ordering -&gt; billing  (break it with an event or by moving the shared concept)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: DFS with three colours finds back edges (cycles) in O(V + E); post-order DFS gives a topological order. To break a cycle, invert one dependency with an event or move the shared concept into its own module.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our 600k-line monolith had 3 teams constantly breaking each other; leadership proposed a full microservices rewrite.</li> <li>T: Restore team autonomy with less risk than a rewrite.</li> <li>A: Proposed modularizing first: defined 7 modules with the teams, introduced module APIs, Spring Modulith verification with a shrinking violation baseline, and schema-per-module; extracted one module (search) that needed different scaling.</li> <li>R: Cross-team breakages fell by 80% in two quarters; the rewrite was cancelled, saving an estimated 18 months.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is Chain of Responsibility?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that passes a request along a chain of handlers; each handler handles it or passes it to the next. The sender doesn't know which handler will process it.</p> <p>Q2. Give real examples.</p> <p>Servlet filters, Spring Security's filter chain, logging levels, approval workflows, customer support escalation.</p> <p>Q3. What happens if no handler handles the request?</p> <p>It reaches the end of the chain. You should handle this explicitly with a default handler or an error, not silently ignore it.</p> <p>Q4. Advantages?</p> <p>Loose coupling between sender and receivers, easy to add or reorder handlers, single responsibility per handler.</p> <p>Q5. Write a simple chain for logging levels (INFO, DEBUG, ERROR).</p> <p>Abstract <code>Logger</code> with a <code>level</code> and <code>next</code>; <code>log(level, msg)</code> prints if <code>this.level &lt;= level</code>, then forwards to <code>next</code>. Chain: <code>info.setNext(debug).setNext(error)</code>.</p> <p>Q1. Pure chain vs. pipeline?</p> <p>Pure: exactly one handler processes the request (escalation). Pipeline/filter: every handler gets a turn and can stop it (validation, middleware).</p> <p>Q2. How do servlet filters implement this?</p> <p>Each <code>Filter.doFilter(req, res, chain)</code> does work, then calls <code>chain.doFilter()</code> to continue (or doesn't, to stop), and can do more work after it returns.</p> <p>Q3. How do you build chains in Spring?</p> <p>Inject <code>List&lt;Handler&gt;</code>; Spring orders beans by <code>@Order</code> / <code>Ordered</code>. Loop over the list, or link them at start-up.</p> <p>Q4. How do you test a chain?</p> <p>Each handler alone (accepts / rejects / forwards); then the assembled chain for order and short-circuit behavior.</p> <p>Q5. Chain vs. Decorator?</p> <p>Similar structure. Decorators always forward and add behavior; chain handlers may stop and their purpose is deciding who handles.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a configurable approval workflow engine (expenses, purchase orders) for many companies."</p> <p>Handlers as rules (amount limits, cost centres, roles) configured per tenant as data; versioned chains; escalation with timeouts (manager doesn't respond in 48 h, so escalate); parallel approvals (finance AND legal); a full audit trail; idempotent state transitions; and a UI to simulate a chain before activating it.</p> <p>Q2. "Design the request pipeline of an API gateway."</p> <p>Ordered stages: TLS termination, WAF/bot filtering, rate limiting (cheap, first), authentication (JWT validation, cached keys), authorization, request transformation, routing, retries/circuit breaking, response transformation, logging/metrics. Discuss per-route chains, fail-open vs. fail-closed per stage, and latency budgets.</p> <p>Q3. "A validation chain of 25 rules adds 400 ms. How do you speed it up?"</p> <p>Profile per handler; reorder by cost × rejection rate; cache external lookups; run independent expensive checks in parallel; move rarely failing checks to async post-processing where the business allows it.</p> <p>Q4. "Users want all validation errors at once, but payments want fail-fast. Same rules?"</p> <p>Yes. Keep handlers pure (return problems, don't throw); provide two traversals, collect-all and first-failure, chosen by the flow.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you make chains debuggable in production?"</p> <p>Structured logs or trace spans per handler with a decision field; a correlation ID; record the chain version and the handler that stopped the request; expose a "dry run" endpoint that shows each handler's verdict.</p> <p>Problem (customer support): Route tickets: the bot handles password resets; VIP customers go to the VIP desk; refunds go to L1 agents; crashes go to engineering. The first matching handler wins. Unmatched tickets go to L2 triage. Implement it without a class per handler.</p> Solution SupportDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.chain.support;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.function.Function;

/** Challenge solution: ticket routing as a list of functions; first non-empty answer wins. */
public class SupportDemo {

    record Ticket(String id, String text, boolean vip) {
    }

    static Optional&lt;String&gt; route(Ticket t, List&lt;Function&lt;Ticket, Optional&lt;String&gt;&gt;&gt; handlers) {
        return handlers.stream().map(h -&gt; h.apply(t)).flatMap(Optional::stream).findFirst();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;Function&lt;Ticket, Optional&lt;String&gt;&gt;&gt; handlers = List.of(
                t -&gt; t.text().contains("password") ? Optional.of("Bot: sent reset link") : Optional.empty(),
                t -&gt; t.vip() ? Optional.of("VIP desk: assigned to senior agent") : Optional.empty(),
                t -&gt; t.text().contains("refund") ? Optional.of("L1 agent: refund initiated") : Optional.empty(),
                t -&gt; t.text().contains("crash") ? Optional.of("Engineering: bug ticket created") : Optional.empty());

        List&lt;Ticket&gt; tickets = List.of(
                new Ticket("T1", "forgot password", false),
                new Ticket("T2", "app crash on checkout", true),
                new Ticket("T3", "want refund for damaged item", false),
                new Ticket("T4", "app crash on login", false),
                new Ticket("T5", "do you sell gift cards?", false));

        for (Ticket t : tickets) {
            System.out.println(t.id() + " -&gt; " + route(t, handlers).orElse("Unhandled: queued for L2 triage"));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>T1 -&gt; Bot: sent reset link
T2 -&gt; VIP desk: assigned to senior agent
T3 -&gt; L1 agent: refund initiated
T4 -&gt; Engineering: bug ticket created
T5 -&gt; Unhandled: queued for L2 triage
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: handlers are <code>Function&lt;Ticket, Optional&lt;String&gt;&gt;</code>; a lazy stream with <code>findFirst()</code> stops at the first match (T2 is a crash, but VIP wins because that handler is earlier, so order is policy); the end of the chain has an explicit default.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Order validation was one 600-line method; adding a fraud check broke COD orders.</li> <li>T: Make validation safe to extend and reuse across checkout, reorder and subscriptions.</li> <li>A: Split it into 11 validator classes, assembled into ordered chains per flow from Spring <code>@Order</code> beans; added per-validator metrics.</li> <li>R: New rules now ship in a day with isolated tests; metrics showed the slowest check, and reordering cut validation p99 by 60%.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Command pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that encapsulates a request as an object, so requests can be parameterized, queued, logged and undone.</p> <p>Q2. Name the participants.</p> <p>Command (interface), Concrete command (holds receiver + parameters), Receiver (does the work), Invoker (runs commands), Client (creates them).</p> <p>Q3. How do you implement undo?</p> <p>Each command implements <code>undo()</code> that reverses <code>execute()</code>; the invoker keeps a stack of executed commands and pops one to undo.</p> <p>Q4. Give Java examples.</p> <p><code>Runnable</code>/<code>Callable</code> in <code>ExecutorService</code>, Swing <code>Action</code>, and <code>UndoManager</code>.</p> <p>Q5. Advantage over calling methods directly?</p> <p>Decoupling (the invoker doesn't know the receiver), and requests become data you can store, delay, retry or undo.</p> <p>Q1. How would you implement redo?</p> <p>Two stacks: undo pops from the undo stack, calls <code>undo()</code>, pushes to the redo stack; redo does the reverse; a new command clears the redo stack.</p> <p>Q2. What is a macro command?</p> <p>A command made of other commands (Composite); <code>execute()</code> runs them in order and <code>undo()</code> undoes them in reverse order.</p> <p>Q3. How do you make queued commands safe to retry?</p> <p>Give each command a unique ID and make handlers idempotent (record processed IDs atomically with the effect).</p> <p>Q4. Command vs. Strategy?</p> <p>A command represents what to do (a request, often with its data); a strategy represents how to do something (an interchangeable algorithm).</p> <p>Q5. How do lambdas relate to Command?</p> <p>A lambda is a command with only <code>execute()</code>. Use lambdas for fire-and-forget tasks; use classes or records when you need undo, IDs, serialization or logging.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a job scheduling system (like a distributed cron) for millions of jobs."</p> <p>Jobs as persisted commands (type, payload, schedule, idempotency key); time-partitioned storage or a delay queue; leasing workers with heartbeats; at-least-once execution with idempotent handlers; retries with backoff; dead-letter queues; per-tenant fairness; observability per job type.</p> <p>Q2. "Commands vs. events in CQRS: walk me through a 'place order' flow."</p> <p><code>PlaceOrder</code> command goes to a handler that validates against the aggregate state and emits <code>OrderPlaced</code> (event), stored in an event store or table plus an outbox; projections update read models; other services react to the event. Commands can fail; events are facts.</p> <p>Q3. "How would you implement undo in a collaborative editor?"</p> <p>Per-user undo stacks of operations; undo generates an inverse operation that is transformed (OT) or merged (CRDT) against concurrent operations from others; don't revert other users' work.</p> <p>Q4. "Stored commands from last year fail to deserialize after a refactor. Prevent it."</p> <p>Schema versioning in every command; tolerant readers; upcasters that convert old versions; contract tests with stored samples of every version; never reuse field meanings.</p> <p>Q5. "An offline-first mobile app syncs commands to the server. What can go wrong?"</p> <p>Duplicates (use IDs), ordering (sequence numbers per device), conflicts (server state changed meanwhile; need conflict resolution rules), clock skew (server validates times), stale permissions, and very old queued commands (expire them).</p> <p>Problem (grocery): "Reorder my usual" adds several items to the cart in one tap. Implement it as a macro command whose undo removes exactly what it added, leaving items that were already in the cart untouched.</p> Solution CartCommands.javaMacroDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.command.cart;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/** Challenge solution: "Reorder my usual" as a macro command with all-or-nothing undo. */
public final class CartCommands {

    private CartCommands() {
    }

    public static class Cart {
        private final Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; lines = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();

        void add(String sku, int qty) { lines.merge(sku, qty, Integer::sum); }

        void remove(String sku, int qty) {
            lines.computeIfPresent(sku, (k, v) -&gt; v - qty &lt;= 0 ? null : v - qty);
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() { return lines.toString(); }
    }

    public interface Command {
        void execute();
        void undo();
    }

    public record AddItem(Cart cart, String sku, int qty) implements Command {
        public void execute() { cart.add(sku, qty); }
        public void undo() { cart.remove(sku, qty); }
    }

    public static final class Macro implements Command {
        private final List&lt;Command&gt; steps;

        public Macro(List&lt;Command&gt; steps) {
            this.steps = List.copyOf(steps);
        }

        public void execute() {
            steps.forEach(Command::execute);
        }

        public void undo() {
            List&lt;Command&gt; reversed = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(steps);
            java.util.Collections.reverse(reversed);
            reversed.forEach(Command::undo); // undo in reverse order
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.command.cart;

import com.handbook.behavioral.command.cart.CartCommands.*;

import java.util.List;

public class MacroDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cart cart = new Cart();
        new AddItem(cart, "BANANA-6", 1).execute();
        System.out.println("Cart before        : " + cart);

        Macro usual = new Macro(List.of(
                new AddItem(cart, "MILK-1L", 2), new AddItem(cart, "BREAD-WW", 1), new AddItem(cart, "BANANA-6", 1)));
        usual.execute();
        System.out.println("After 'my usual'   : " + cart);
        usual.undo();
        System.out.println("After undo         : " + cart);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Cart before        : {BANANA-6=1}
After 'my usual'   : {BANANA-6=2, MILK-1L=2, BREAD-WW=1}
After undo         : {BANANA-6=1}
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the macro is a Composite of commands; undo runs in reverse order; each <code>AddItem</code> undoes only its own quantity, so the banana that was already there stays. Follow-up: if a step fails halfway, undo the steps that already ran (transactional macro).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Re-running a failed payments batch double-paid 8,000 bills.</li> <li>T: Make batch re-runs safe and restore customer trust.</li> <li>A: Introduced command IDs with processed markers written in the same transaction as ledger entries, a dead-letter queue for failures, and a runbook that says "re-run freely".</li> <li>R: Zero duplicate payments in the 18 months since, including 3 more batch crashes that were simply re-run.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Interpreter pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that defines a grammar as classes and evaluates sentences of that language by walking a tree of those objects.</p> <p>Q2. Terminal vs. non-terminal expressions?</p> <p>Terminal expressions are leaves (a number, a variable, a comparison). Non-terminal expressions combine others (AND, OR, +).</p> <p>Q3. Give real examples.</p> <p>Regex (<code>Pattern</code>), SpEL, SQL/JPQL parsing, cron expressions, calculators.</p> <p>Q4. When should you use it?</p> <p>For a simple, stable grammar that needs to be evaluated often, especially when non-developers write the sentences.</p> <p>Q5. Main disadvantage?</p> <p>Complex grammars produce many classes and are hard to maintain; performance can suffer compared with compiled code.</p> <p>Q1. How do you parse text into an AST?</p> <p>Tokenize, then a recursive-descent parser with one method per grammar rule (<code>expr</code>, <code>term</code>, <code>factor</code>); precedence comes from the call hierarchy.</p> <p>Q2. How do you add a new operation (pretty-print, to-SQL) without changing nodes?</p> <p>Use a Visitor, or with sealed node types, a <code>switch</code> with pattern matching in a separate class.</p> <p>Q3. How do you make evaluation fast?</p> <p>Parse once and cache the AST; compile to lambdas; short-circuit <code>AND</code>/<code>OR</code>; order cheap checks first.</p> <p>Q4. Interpreter vs. Specification pattern?</p> <p>Specification composes rule objects in code (<code>isGold().and(totalAbove(999))</code>); Interpreter parses text into such objects.</p> <p>Q5. What are the risks of user-written rules?</p> <p>Wrong meaning (precedence), invalid fields, expensive or infinite evaluation, and code execution if the engine is too powerful. Validate, sandbox and limit.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a promotions engine where marketing writes rules."</p> <p>A small DSL or JSON rules with a schema of fields; parse-on-save with errors; versioned rules with effective dates; simulation on historical data; conflict resolution between promos (priority, stacking rules); compiled evaluation for checkout latency; audit and a kill switch; a/b tests.</p> <p>Q2. "Build your own DSL or use an existing engine (SpEL, CEL, Drools)?"</p> <p>Custom: tight control, small surface, better error messages, but you maintain it. CEL/SpEL (sandboxed): proven, fast, but you must restrict features. Drools: complex rule sets with inference, at the cost of a steep learning curve. Choose based on rule complexity, who writes rules, and security.</p> <p>Q3. "How would you evaluate a rule against 50 million customers?"</p> <p>Translate the AST to SQL or a Spark predicate (visitor) and push it down to the data, instead of loading each customer into memory.</p> <p>Q4. "Protect the system from a malicious or careless rule."</p> <p>Limits on expression size and depth, evaluation timeouts, whitelisted functions only, no reflection or I/O, and staged rollout with monitoring.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you evolve a rule language without breaking stored rules?"</p> <p>Version the grammar; keep old parsers or migrate stored rules with automated rewriters; deprecate constructs with warnings; test with a corpus of real stored rules.</p> <p>Problem: Interpret Roman numerals (<code>MCMXCIV</code> = 1994). Each symbol is a terminal with a value; a smaller symbol before a larger one is subtracted.</p> Solution Roman.javaRomanDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.interpreter.roman;

import java.util.Map;

/** Challenge solution: each symbol is a terminal expression; subtraction rule is the grammar. */
public final class Roman {

    private static final Map&lt;Character, Integer&gt; VALUE =
            Map.of('I', 1, 'V', 5, 'X', 10, 'L', 50, 'C', 100, 'D', 500, 'M', 1000);

    private Roman() {
    }

    public static int interpret(String numeral) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; numeral.length(); i++) {
            Integer current = VALUE.get(numeral.charAt(i));
            if (current == null) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not a Roman numeral: " + numeral);
            }
            boolean subtract = i + 1 &lt; numeral.length() &amp;&amp; VALUE.getOrDefault(numeral.charAt(i + 1), 0) &gt; current;
            total += subtract ? -current : current;
        }
        return total;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.interpreter.roman;

public class RomanDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (String n : new String[] {"III", "IX", "XLII", "MCMXCIV", "MMXXVI"}) {
            System.out.println(n + " = " + Roman.interpret(n));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>III = 3
IX = 9
XLII = 42
MCMXCIV = 1994
MMXXVI = 2026
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: symbols are terminals; the subtraction rule is the grammar; single left-to-right pass, O(n). Follow-up: validate canonical form (<code>IIII</code> and <code>IC</code> are invalid) with a stricter grammar.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Every promotion change needed a developer and a release; marketing waited about 5 days per change.</li> <li>T: Let marketing own promotion rules safely.</li> <li>A: Built a small rule DSL with a recursive-descent parser, a schema of allowed fields, parse-on-save validation, a "show parsed tree" preview, and simulation against yesterday's orders.</li> <li>R: Rule changes went from 5 days to 15 minutes; after adding the preview, zero mis-scoped promos in a year.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Iterator pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that provides a way to access elements sequentially without exposing the collection's internal structure.</p> <p>Q2. <code>Iterator</code> vs. <code>Iterable</code>?</p> <p><code>Iterable</code> is something you can iterate (has <code>iterator()</code>); <code>Iterator</code> is the cursor that does the iterating (<code>hasNext()</code>, <code>next()</code>). For-each works on any <code>Iterable</code>.</p> <p>Q3. What is a fail-fast iterator?</p> <p>One that throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> if the collection is structurally modified during iteration (except through the iterator's own <code>remove()</code>). <code>ArrayList</code> and <code>HashMap</code> iterators are fail-fast.</p> <p>Q4. Fail-fast vs. fail-safe?</p> <p>Fail-fast detects modification and throws. Fail-safe / weakly consistent iterators (<code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>, <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code>) work on a snapshot or tolerate changes and never throw.</p> <p>Q5. How do you remove elements safely while iterating?</p> <p><code>iterator.remove()</code> or <code>collection.removeIf(predicate)</code>.</p> <p>Q1. Write a custom <code>Iterable</code> for a range of numbers.</p> <p>Class with <code>start</code>/<code>end</code>; <code>iterator()</code> returns an anonymous <code>Iterator</code> with a <code>current</code> field; <code>hasNext()</code> returns <code>current &lt; end</code>; <code>next()</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code> at the end, otherwise returns <code>current++</code>.</p> <p>Q2. Internal vs. external iteration?</p> <p>External: the caller drives (<code>while (it.hasNext())</code>), can stop and interleave. Internal: the collection drives (<code>forEach</code>, streams), which is concise and can parallelize.</p> <p>Q3. How does <code>ListIterator</code> differ from <code>Iterator</code>?</p> <p>It's bidirectional (<code>hasPrevious</code>, <code>previous</code>), knows indices, and supports <code>set</code> and <code>add</code> during iteration.</p> <p>Q4. How would you iterate a paginated REST API?</p> <p>An iterator that fetches the next page lazily when the current page is exhausted (Example 1), exposed as <code>Iterable</code> or <code>Stream</code>.</p> <p>Q5. How do iterators enable laziness in streams?</p> <p>Stream pipelines pull elements one at a time through the spliterator; with <code>limit</code> or <code>findFirst</code>, the source stops producing as soon as enough elements have been consumed.</p> <p>Q1. "Merge k sorted lists/streams efficiently." (classic coding round)</p> <p>A min-heap (or max-heap) holding each stream's head: pop the top, push the next from the same stream; O(n log k) time, O(k) memory, lazy (Example 2).</p> <p>Q2. "Design an API to export 50 million records."</p> <p>Keyset pagination or a server-side streaming endpoint (chunked transfer, NDJSON/CSV), back-pressure, resumable cursors (opaque, signed tokens), or an async export job that writes to object storage and returns a link.</p> <p>Q3. "Offset vs. cursor pagination: trade-offs?"</p> <p>Offset: simple, supports jumping to page N, but slow at high offsets and inconsistent under inserts and deletes. Cursor: stable and fast, but no random access. Use cursors for feeds and exports; offsets for small admin tables.</p> <p>Q4. "Implement a BST iterator with O(h) memory." (common at Google/Microsoft)</p> <p>Use an explicit stack: push the left path from the root; <code>next()</code> pops a node, then pushes the left path of its right child. Amortized O(1) per <code>next()</code>, O(h) memory.</p> <p>Q5. "What can go wrong with iterators over remote data?"</p> <p>Hidden latency (N round trips), errors mid-iteration (retry the page, resume from the cursor), consistency (data changes between pages), resource leaks (unclosed cursors). Make these explicit in the API.</p> <p>Problem (logistics): Keep only the last 3 GPS pings of a delivery bike in a fixed-size ring buffer. Iterate oldest to newest, and make the iterator fail-fast if the buffer is modified during iteration.</p> Solution RingBuffer.javaRingDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.iterator.ring;

import java.util.ConcurrentModificationException;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

/** Challenge solution: keeps the last N items; iterates oldest -&gt; newest; fail-fast. */
public class RingBuffer&lt;T&gt; implements Iterable&lt;T&gt; {

    private final Object[] items;
    private int start;
    private int size;
    private int modCount;

    public RingBuffer(int capacity) {
        items = new Object[capacity];
    }

    public void add(T item) {
        int end = (start + size) % items.length;
        items[end] = item;
        if (size &lt; items.length) {
            size++;
        } else {
            start = (start + 1) % items.length; // overwrite the oldest
        }
        modCount++;
    }

    @Override
    public Iterator&lt;T&gt; iterator() {
        return new Iterator&lt;&gt;() {
            private int index;
            private final int expectedModCount = modCount;

            public boolean hasNext() {
                return index &lt; size;
            }

            @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
            public T next() {
                if (modCount != expectedModCount) {
                    throw new ConcurrentModificationException("buffer changed during iteration");
                }
                if (!hasNext()) {
                    throw new NoSuchElementException();
                }
                return (T) items[(start + index++) % items.length];
            }
        };
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.iterator.ring;

import java.util.ConcurrentModificationException;

public class RingDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RingBuffer&lt;String&gt; lastPings = new RingBuffer&lt;&gt;(3); // last 3 GPS pings of a delivery bike
        for (String ping : new String[] {"12.9301,77.6201", "12.9305,77.6210", "12.9312,77.6222", "12.9320,77.6230"}) {
            lastPings.add(ping);
        }
        System.out.print("Last 3 pings:");
        for (String p : lastPings) {
            System.out.print(" [" + p + "]");
        }
        System.out.println();
        try {
            for (String p : lastPings) {
                lastPings.add("12.9333,77.6240"); // modifying while iterating
            }
        } catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {
            System.out.println("Fail-fast: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Last 3 pings: [12.9305,77.6210] [12.9312,77.6222] [12.9320,77.6230]
Fail-fast: buffer changed during iteration
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: circular indexing <code>(start + i) % capacity</code>; overwriting moves <code>start</code>; the iterator snapshots <code>modCount</code> (exactly how <code>ArrayList</code> does fail-fast); <code>NoSuchElementException</code> at the end. For concurrent producers, use a lock or a lock-free ring buffer (LMAX Disruptor).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A full-history CSV export crashed a shared service when a large merchant used it.</li> <li>T: Make exports safe for any account size without a new system.</li> <li>A: Replaced list-based loading with a keyset-paginated iterator streaming rows directly to the response; added a row cap with an async export fallback.</li> <li>R: Memory flat at about 200 MB for any export size; the 9-million-row export finished in 4 minutes with no impact on other tenants.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Mediator pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern where objects communicate through a central mediator instead of directly, which reduces coupling between them.</p> <p>Q2. Real-world examples?</p> <p>Air traffic control, chat rooms, a ride-hailing dispatch center, UI dialogs coordinating widgets, and Spring's <code>DispatcherServlet</code>.</p> <p>Q3. What problem does it solve?</p> <p>Many-to-many dependencies between objects, which become hard to change and reuse.</p> <p>Q4. Mediator vs. Facade?</p> <p>A facade gives clients a simpler interface to a subsystem (one-way); a mediator coordinates peers that talk to it in both directions.</p> <p>Q5. Main drawback?</p> <p>The mediator can become a complex god object.</p> <p>Q1. Mediator vs. Observer?</p> <p>Observer is one-to-many notification; Mediator coordinates interactions with rules. A mediator is often built on top of Observer-style events.</p> <p>Q2. How do you test a mediator?</p> <p>With fake colleagues (or real simple ones) and assertions on the resulting interactions; most rules live there, so it deserves the most tests.</p> <p>Q3. How do you stop the mediator becoming too big?</p> <p>One mediator per collaboration; push domain logic into domain objects; split into sub-mediators by area.</p> <p>Q4. Is <code>DispatcherServlet</code> a mediator?</p> <p>Yes: it coordinates handler mappings, handler adapters, view resolvers and exception resolvers, which don't know each other.</p> <p>Q5. How does a command bus relate to Mediator?</p> <p>A command bus routes a request to its handler, so senders and handlers don't know each other. It's a mediator for requests.</p> <p>Q1. "Design the dispatch system for a ride-hailing company."</p> <p>Geo-partitioned dispatchers (mediators per cell/city) holding available drivers in spatial indexes (geohash, H3); rider requests matched by ETA, rating and fairness; batching of matches every few seconds for global optimization; timeouts and re-matching when drivers decline; event streams for location; surge and supply-demand balancing.</p> <p>Q2. "Orchestration vs. choreography for an order flow across 6 services?"</p> <p>Orchestration (mediator) for complex flows needing visibility, compensation and timeouts; choreography (observer/events) for simple, loosely coupled reactions. Often a mix: orchestrate the core flow, publish events for side effects (emails, analytics).</p> <p>Q3. "How would you scale a chat room with 500,000 members?"</p> <p>Fan-out on read for huge rooms; shard members across gateway servers; the room mediator publishes once to a pub/sub topic; each gateway delivers to its connected members; mentions and pushes processed asynchronously.</p> <p>Q4. "The mediator is a single point of failure. Mitigate."</p> <p>Partition it, run replicas with leader election per partition, keep its state recoverable (event log), and make colleagues tolerate temporary unavailability (retries, local queues).</p> <p>Q5. "When would you avoid Mediator?"</p> <p>When there are only two or three collaborators (direct calls are clearer), or when interactions are simple notifications better served by Observer.</p> <p>Problem: An online auction: bidders must not see or call each other. The house enforces a reserve price and a minimum increment, tells the previous leader when they're outbid, announces the result to everyone, and rejects bids after closing.</p> Solution AuctionHouse.javaAuctionDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.mediator.auction;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/** Challenge solution: bidders never see each other; the auction house mediates every bid. */
public class AuctionHouse {

    private final String lot;
    private final int minIncrement;
    private final List&lt;Bidder&gt; bidders = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    private int highest;
    private Bidder leader;
    private boolean closed;

    public AuctionHouse(String lot, int reservePrice, int minIncrement) {
        this.lot = lot;
        this.highest = reservePrice;
        this.minIncrement = minIncrement;
    }

    public Bidder register(String name) {
        Bidder b = new Bidder(name, this);
        bidders.add(b);
        return b;
    }

    void bid(Bidder bidder, int amount) {
        if (closed) {
            bidder.tell("auction closed");
        } else if (amount &lt; highest + (leader == null ? 0 : minIncrement)) {
            bidder.tell("bid INR " + amount + " rejected, minimum is INR " + (highest + (leader == null ? 0 : minIncrement)));
        } else {
            Bidder previous = leader;
            highest = amount;
            leader = bidder;
            bidder.tell("you lead at INR " + amount);
            if (previous != null &amp;&amp; previous != bidder) {
                previous.tell("outbid, new price INR " + amount);
            }
        }
    }

    public void close() {
        closed = true;
        String result = leader == null ? "unsold" : "sold to " + leader.name + " for INR " + highest;
        bidders.forEach(b -&gt; b.tell(lot + " " + result));
    }

    public static class Bidder {
        private final String name;
        private final AuctionHouse house;

        Bidder(String name, AuctionHouse house) {
            this.name = name;
            this.house = house;
        }

        public void bid(int amount) {
            house.bid(this, amount);
        }

        void tell(String message) {
            System.out.println("  -&gt; " + name + ": " + message);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.mediator.auction;

public class AuctionDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AuctionHouse house = new AuctionHouse("Vintage camera", 5_000, 500);
        AuctionHouse.Bidder anil = house.register("Anil");
        AuctionHouse.Bidder bela = house.register("Bela");
        anil.bid(5_000);
        bela.bid(5_200);
        bela.bid(5_500);
        anil.bid(6_500);
        house.close();
        bela.bid(9_000);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>  -&gt; Anil: you lead at INR 5000
  -&gt; Bela: bid INR 5200 rejected, minimum is INR 5500
  -&gt; Bela: you lead at INR 5500
  -&gt; Anil: outbid, new price INR 5500
  -&gt; Anil: you lead at INR 6500
  -&gt; Bela: outbid, new price INR 6500
  -&gt; Anil: Vintage camera sold to Anil for INR 6500
  -&gt; Bela: Vintage camera sold to Anil for INR 6500
  -&gt; Bela: auction closed
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: bidders hold only the house; all rules (reserve, increment, outbid notice, closing) are in the mediator; for concurrent bidders, make <code>bid()</code> <code>synchronized</code> or serialize bids through a queue so two bidders can't both "lead".</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our checkout widgets were wired to each other directly; a new field caused a currency mismatch in payments.</li> <li>T: Make cross-field rules reliable and testable.</li> <li>A: Introduced a form mediator owning all cross-field rules, removed direct widget references, and wrote table-driven tests for country, currency and payment combinations.</li> <li>R: No cross-field defects in the following 3 releases; adding a "GST number" field took one rule and one test.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Memento pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that saves an object's state into a snapshot so it can be restored later, without exposing its internal details.</p> <p>Q2. Name the participants.</p> <p>Originator (creates and restores), Memento (the snapshot), Caretaker (stores mementos without looking inside).</p> <p>Q3. Give real examples.</p> <p>Undo in editors, save games, version history, browser back/forward state, database savepoints.</p> <p>Q4. Why shouldn't the caretaker read the memento?</p> <p>To keep encapsulation: only the originator knows its internal structure, so it can change without breaking the caretaker.</p> <p>Q5. Main disadvantage?</p> <p>Memory usage when snapshots are large or frequent.</p> <p>Q1. How do you enforce that only the originator reads the memento in Java?</p> <p>Make the memento a nested class with private fields (Example 1), or a narrow public interface with a private implementation.</p> <p>Q2. Memento vs. Command for undo?</p> <p>Memento restores snapshots (simple, more memory); Command reverses operations (less memory, needs an inverse for each operation). They're often combined.</p> <p>Q3. How do you limit memory use?</p> <p>Bound the history, snapshot periodically with commands in between, store diffs, or compress old snapshots.</p> <p>Q4. What must you watch for when copying state?</p> <p>Deep vs. shallow copies of mutable fields, and ordering (see the <code>Map.copyOf</code> note in Example 2).</p> <p>Q5. How do you persist mementos safely?</p> <p>Serialize with a versioned schema, protect integrity (signing) and confidentiality (encryption) if they leave the server, and handle old versions gracefully.</p> <p>Q1. "Design version history for a collaborative document editor."</p> <p>Store operations (for collaboration and fine-grained undo) plus periodic snapshots (for fast loading and named versions); content-addressed storage for structural sharing; retention policies; a "restore version" that creates a new version rather than rewriting history; access control per version.</p> <p>Q2. "How do stream processors achieve fault tolerance with checkpoints?"</p> <p>Periodic consistent snapshots of operator state (Chandy–Lamport-style barriers in Flink) stored durably, together with source offsets; on failure, restore the snapshot and replay from those offsets, giving exactly-once state.</p> <p>Q3. "Event-sourced aggregates take 8 seconds to load. Fix it."</p> <p>Snapshots every N events (versioned), load snapshot + newer events; archive old events; check that snapshotting doesn't block writes (async creation).</p> <p>Q4. "Users want to resume a multi-step application on any device. Design it."</p> <p>Server-side draft store keyed by user and flow, versioned draft schema with migrations, TTL for abandoned drafts, re-validation and re-pricing on resume, and conflict handling if two devices edit the same draft.</p> <p>Q5. "What can't a memento restore?"</p> <p>External side effects (payments, emails, other services' state). For those you need compensating actions (Saga), not snapshots.</p> <p>Problem: An editor keeps text snapshots for undo, but memory is limited: keep only the last 3 snapshots and count how many were dropped. Undo until history runs out.</p> Solution BoundedHistory.javaHistoryDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.memento.history;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Optional;

/** Challenge solution: a caretaker that keeps only the newest N mementos. */
public class BoundedHistory&lt;M&gt; {

    private final int capacity;
    private final Deque&lt;M&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
    private int dropped;

    public BoundedHistory(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    public void push(M memento) {
        if (stack.size() == capacity) {
            stack.removeLast(); // forget the oldest
            dropped++;
        }
        stack.push(memento);
    }

    public Optional&lt;M&gt; pop() {
        return Optional.ofNullable(stack.poll());
    }

    public int size() { return stack.size(); }

    public int dropped() { return dropped; }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.memento.history;

public class HistoryDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BoundedHistory&lt;String&gt; undo = new BoundedHistory&lt;&gt;(3);
        String text = "";
        for (String word : new String[] {"Fresh", " mangoes", " at", " 20%", " off"}) {
            undo.push(text); // snapshot before each edit
            text += word;
        }
        System.out.println("text      : " + text);
        System.out.println("kept/drop : " + undo.size() + "/" + undo.dropped());
        while (true) {
            var previous = undo.pop();
            if (previous.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("no more undo (older snapshots were dropped)");
                break;
            }
            text = previous.get();
            System.out.println("undo -&gt;   : " + text);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>text      : Fresh mangoes at 20% off
kept/drop : 3/2
undo -&gt;   : Fresh mangoes at 20%
undo -&gt;   : Fresh mangoes at
undo -&gt;   : Fresh mangoes
no more undo (older snapshots were dropped)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: an <code>ArrayDeque</code> used as a stack, removing from the bottom when full; <code>Optional</code> for "no more undo"; strings are immutable, so snapshots are safe without copying. Follow-ups: a redo stack, and snapshotting every N edits instead of every edit.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Users lost multi-step insurance applications when switching devices; 30% abandoned.</li> <li>T: Let users resume anywhere, securely.</li> <li>A: Built a server-side draft store with versioned mementos, re-validation on resume, a 30-day TTL, and deletion hooks for privacy requests.</li> <li>R: Abandonment fell from 30% to 18%; zero tampering incidents because no state was trusted from the client.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Null Object pattern?</p> <p>Using an object with neutral, do-nothing behavior instead of <code>null</code>, so callers don't need null checks.</p> <p>Q2. Give examples.</p> <p>A <code>NoDiscount</code> coupon, a no-op logger, an anonymous/guest user, <code>Collections.emptyList()</code>.</p> <p>Q3. Benefits?</p> <p>Fewer NullPointerExceptions, simpler client code, and the default behavior is defined in one place.</p> <p>Q4. Null Object vs. <code>Optional</code>?</p> <p>Null Object behaves neutrally; <code>Optional</code> forces callers to handle absence explicitly.</p> <p>Q5. Should methods return <code>null</code> for empty collections?</p> <p>No. Return an empty collection (<code>List.of()</code>), which is itself a Null Object.</p> <p>Q1. When should you NOT use a Null Object?</p> <p>When absence is an error or must be reported (payment gateway missing, security checks). Fail fast instead.</p> <p>Q2. How do you combine <code>Optional</code> and Null Objects?</p> <p>Repositories return <code>Optional</code>; the service converts with <code>orElse(NULL_OBJECT)</code> where a neutral behavior exists (challenge).</p> <p>Q3. How would you implement a no-op logger?</p> <p>A class implementing the logger interface with empty methods, exposed as a singleton (<code>NoOpLogger.INSTANCE</code>), like SLF4J's <code>NOPLogger</code>.</p> <p>Q4. How do Null Objects help testing?</p> <p>Inject no-op collaborators (audit, metrics, notifications) to test business logic without mocks.</p> <p>Q5. How does Spring Security represent anonymous users?</p> <p>With <code>AnonymousAuthenticationToken</code>: a real <code>Authentication</code> object with role <code>ROLE_ANONYMOUS</code>, so filters and access checks don't deal with <code>null</code>.</p> <p>Q1. "Define a null-safety strategy for a 2-million-line codebase."</p> <p>Nullness annotations (JSpecify) with package-level <code>@NullMarked</code>, static analysis in CI (NullAway), no <code>null</code> returns for collections, <code>Optional</code> for maybe-values in return types, Null Objects for optional collaborators, and incremental adoption module by module.</p> <p>Q2. "Disabled features via no-op implementations: risks?"</p> <p>Silent misconfiguration (the war story). Mitigate with startup logs, health indicators reporting which implementations are active, and production guards that fail if critical features are no-ops.</p> <p>Q3. "How does graceful degradation relate to Null Object?"</p> <p>Fallbacks return neutral results (empty recommendations) when a dependency fails, keeping the page working. They must be metered and alarmed so degraded mode doesn't go unnoticed.</p> <p>Q4. "Design a guest checkout flow."</p> <p>A guest customer object (no saved addresses, no loyalty) implementing the same <code>Customer</code> interface; same checkout path; prompts to sign in at the right moments; merge guest carts on sign-in.</p> <p>Q5. "Kotlin and Scala have null-safe types; how do you get similar safety in Java?"</p> <p>Annotations + static analysis, <code>Optional</code>, Null Objects, records with <code>Objects.requireNonNull</code> in compact constructors, and APIs that never accept or return <code>null</code>.</p> <p>Problem: <code>findById</code> may not find a customer. Pricing applies a loyalty discount (Gold 5%, Platinum 10%). Design it so the repository makes absence explicit, while pricing code has no null checks or <code>Optional</code> handling.</p> Solution CustomerDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.nullobject.customer;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;

/** Challenge solution: Optional at the repository boundary, Null Object inside the domain. */
public class CustomerDemo {

    interface Customer {
        String name();
        String tier();
        int loyaltyDiscountPercent();
    }

    record RegisteredCustomer(String name, String tier) implements Customer {
        public int loyaltyDiscountPercent() {
            return switch (tier) {
                case "GOLD" -&gt; 5;
                case "PLATINUM" -&gt; 10;
                default -&gt; 0;
            };
        }
    }

    static final Customer UNKNOWN = new Customer() {
        public String name() { return "guest"; }
        public String tier() { return "NONE"; }
        public int loyaltyDiscountPercent() { return 0; }
    };

    static final Map&lt;String, Customer&gt; DB = Map.of("C1", new RegisteredCustomer("Asha", "PLATINUM"),
            "C2", new RegisteredCustomer("Ravi", "SILVER"));

    static Optional&lt;Customer&gt; findById(String id) { // repository: absence is explicit
        return Optional.ofNullable(DB.get(id));
    }

    static int priceFor(String customerId, int basePrice) {
        Customer c = findById(customerId).orElse(UNKNOWN); // boundary: convert absence to a Null Object
        return basePrice - basePrice * c.loyaltyDiscountPercent() / 100;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (String id : new String[] {"C1", "C2", "C999"}) {
            Customer c = findById(id).orElse(UNKNOWN);
            System.out.printf("%-5s %-6s tier=%-8s price of INR 1000 item: INR %d%n", id, c.name(), c.tier(), priceFor(id, 1000));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>C1    Asha   tier=PLATINUM price of INR 1000 item: INR 900
C2    Ravi   tier=SILVER   price of INR 1000 item: INR 1000
C999  guest  tier=NONE     price of INR 1000 item: INR 1000
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>Optional</code> at the repository boundary (absence is explicit there); <code>orElse(UNKNOWN)</code> converts it to a Null Object where a neutral behavior (0% discount) is correct; pricing logic stays simple. For operations where absence is an error (send invoice), use <code>orElseThrow()</code> instead.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Checkout code had 40+ null checks for coupons, and a missed one caused an NPE outage during a sale.</li> <li>T: Remove the class of bug.</li> <li>A: Introduced <code>Coupon.NONE</code>, made lookups non-null, added JSpecify annotations and NullAway in CI for the checkout module.</li> <li>R: No NPE incidents in checkout in the next year; the code shrank by about 300 lines.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Observer pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern defining a one-to-many dependency: when the subject changes, all registered observers are notified automatically.</p> <p>Q2. Give real examples.</p> <p>Event listeners in UIs (button clicks), YouTube/newsletter subscriptions, stock price tickers, Spring <code>@EventListener</code>, Kafka consumers.</p> <p>Q3. What are push and pull models?</p> <p>Push: the subject sends the data with the notification. Pull: the subject only signals a change, and observers fetch what they need.</p> <p>Q4. Advantages?</p> <p>Loose coupling, easy to add new observers, supports broadcast communication.</p> <p>Q5. Is <code>java.util.Observable</code> still used?</p> <p>No. It's deprecated since Java 9. Use custom listener interfaces, <code>PropertyChangeListener</code>, Spring events or <code>Flow</code>.</p> <p>Q1. How do you avoid memory leaks with observers?</p> <p>Unsubscribe (return handles from <code>subscribe</code>), tie subscriptions to lifecycles, clear observers when the subject finishes (Example 3), or use weak references carefully.</p> <p>Q2. How do you stop one failing observer from affecting others?</p> <p>Catch exceptions per observer and log them (Example 2), or deliver asynchronously with isolated error handling.</p> <p>Q3. How do Spring application events work?</p> <p>Publish with <code>ApplicationEventPublisher.publishEvent(e)</code>; methods annotated with <code>@EventListener</code> for that event type are called (synchronously by default, <code>@Async</code> for async, <code>@TransactionalEventListener</code> to tie to transaction phases).</p> <p>Q4. How do you make an observer list thread-safe?</p> <p><code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> (cheap reads, expensive writes, ideal when subscriptions change rarely), or iterate over a snapshot copy under a lock.</p> <p>Q5. Observer vs. Pub/Sub?</p> <p>Observer: observers subscribe directly to the subject (in-process). Pub/Sub: a broker sits between publishers and subscribers, which only share a topic, usually across processes.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a notification system that reacts to order, payment and delivery events for 50 million users."</p> <p>Services publish events to Kafka via outbox; a notification service consumes, applies user preferences, de-duplicates (idempotency), batches and rate-limits per channel, and fans out to push/SMS/email providers through per-channel queues. Discuss retries, dead letters, ordering per user, and quiet hours.</p> <p>Q2. "Events are published inside DB transactions. What can go wrong and how do you fix it?"</p> <p>Events for rolled-back changes, and lost events if the process crashes after commit. Fix: after-commit listeners in-process; transactional outbox + relay (or CDC with Debezium) for cross-service events; idempotent consumers.</p> <p>Q3. "A slow observer is slowing down a hot path. Options?"</p> <p>Make it async (executor or queue), batch, apply back-pressure (reactive streams), or move it out of process (publish to a broker). Measure the hot path's latency budget first.</p> <p>Q4. "Choreography or orchestration?"</p> <p>Choreography (Observer) for loosely coupled, independent reactions; orchestration (Mediator) for multi-step business flows needing visibility, timeouts and compensation. Many systems combine them.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you evolve event schemas without breaking consumers?"</p> <p>A schema registry with backward/forward compatibility checks, additive changes only, versioned topics or event types for breaking changes, and consumer-driven contract tests.</p> <p>Problem: Build a typed, thread-safe event bus: subscribers register for an event class; <code>subscribe</code> returns a handle that unsubscribes when closed; publishing an event calls only handlers for that type.</p> Solution EventBus.javaEventBusDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.observer.bus;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

/** Challenge solution: typed, thread-safe event bus with closeable subscriptions. */
public class EventBus {

    private final Map&lt;Class&lt;?&gt;, List&lt;Consumer&lt;Object&gt;&gt;&gt; handlers = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();

    public &lt;T&gt; AutoCloseable subscribe(Class&lt;T&gt; type, Consumer&lt;? super T&gt; handler) {
        Consumer&lt;Object&gt; wrapper = event -&gt; handler.accept(type.cast(event));
        handlers.computeIfAbsent(type, k -&gt; new CopyOnWriteArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(wrapper);
        return () -&gt; handlers.get(type).remove(wrapper);
    }

    public void publish(Object event) {
        handlers.getOrDefault(event.getClass(), List.of()).forEach(h -&gt; h.accept(event));
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.observer.bus;

public class EventBusDemo {

    record UserSignedUp(String email) {
    }

    record PasswordChanged(String email) {
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        EventBus bus = new EventBus();
        AutoCloseable welcome = bus.subscribe(UserSignedUp.class, e -&gt; System.out.println("welcome mail -&gt; " + e.email()));
        bus.subscribe(UserSignedUp.class, e -&gt; System.out.println("CRM contact  -&gt; " + e.email()));
        bus.subscribe(PasswordChanged.class, e -&gt; System.out.println("security mail -&gt; " + e.email()));

        bus.publish(new UserSignedUp("neha@example.com"));
        bus.publish(new PasswordChanged("neha@example.com"));
        welcome.close();
        bus.publish(new UserSignedUp("li@example.com"));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>welcome mail -&gt; neha@example.com
CRM contact  -&gt; neha@example.com
security mail -&gt; neha@example.com
CRM contact  -&gt; li@example.com
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> + <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> make subscribe, unsubscribe and publish safe from any thread; <code>AutoCloseable</code> handles work with try-with-resources; <code>type.cast</code> keeps handlers type-safe. Follow-ups: deliver to handlers of supertypes, isolate handler failures, async delivery.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Welcome emails were sent for sign-ups that later rolled back.</li> <li>T: Guarantee notifications only for committed changes, in-process and across services.</li> <li>A: Switched listeners to after-commit, introduced a transactional outbox with a Debezium relay to Kafka, and made consumers idempotent by event ID.</li> <li>R: Zero phantom notifications since; the outbox also gave us a replayable audit trail of all domain events.</li> </ul>

Variation Notes <code>java.util.function.Predicate</code> Already has <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>negate</code>: the simplest specification. Add names/descriptions when you need explainability Explainable specs (Example 2) Return reasons, not just booleans Parameterized specs (Example 3) Built from request data Specs that compile to queries (coding challenge) Push filtering into the database Spring Data JPA <code>Specification&lt;T&gt;</code> <code>toPredicate(root, query, cb)</code> produces JPA Criteria predicates; <code>repo.findAll(spec.and(other))</code> QueryDSL / jOOQ conditions Type-safe query predicates, composable the same way <p>Spring Data JPA example:</p> <pre><code>static Specification&lt;Product&gt; inStock() { return (root, q, cb) -&gt; cb.gt(root.get("stock"), 0); }
static Specification&lt;Product&gt; priceAtMost(int max) { return (root, q, cb) -&gt; cb.le(root.get("priceInr"), max); }

List&lt;Product&gt; results = productRepository.findAll(inStock().and(priceAtMost(2000)));  // SQL WHERE, not in-memory
</code></pre>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Specification pattern?</p> <p>A pattern that puts a business rule in an object with <code>isSatisfiedBy(candidate)</code>, and lets rules be combined with AND, OR and NOT.</p> <p>Q2. Where is it used?</p> <p>Filtering (search), validation (eligibility), and querying (Spring Data JPA <code>Specification</code>).</p> <p>Q3. How is it related to <code>Predicate</code>?</p> <p><code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code> with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>negate</code> is a minimal specification. The pattern adds names, descriptions, reasons and query translation.</p> <p>Q4. Advantages?</p> <p>Reusable, composable, testable rules with a single definition.</p> <p>Q5. Write a spec for "adult AND resident".</p> <p><code>Spec&lt;Person&gt; adult = Spec.of("adult", p -&gt; p.age() &gt;= 18); Spec&lt;Person&gt; resident = Spec.of("resident", Person::isResident); adult.and(resident).isSatisfiedBy(person);</code></p> <p>Q1. How does Spring Data JPA's <code>Specification</code> work?</p> <p>Each spec returns a JPA Criteria <code>Predicate</code> via <code>toPredicate(root, query, criteriaBuilder)</code>; they combine with <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>; <code>JpaSpecificationExecutor.findAll(spec)</code> runs one SQL query.</p> <p>Q2. How do you report why a candidate failed?</p> <p>Give each rule a reason, evaluate all rules and collect failures (Example 2), or have <code>and</code> return a result object listing failed children.</p> <p>Q3. In-memory vs. database evaluation?</p> <p>In-memory for small sets or objects already loaded; database for large data. Keep one definition (or shared tests) so both agree.</p> <p>Q4. Specification vs. Strategy?</p> <p>Specification is a boolean business rule meant to be combined; Strategy is an interchangeable algorithm producing a result.</p> <p>Q5. How do you test specifications?</p> <p>Table-driven tests per leaf rule (boundary values), plus a few combinations to check AND/OR/NOT and descriptions.</p> <p>Q1. "Design dynamic search filters over 50 million products."</p> <p>Filters map to specifications that translate to the search engine's query DSL (Elasticsearch bool queries) or SQL with indexed columns; facets computed by the engine; validation of allowed filters; cache common combinations; never filter in application memory.</p> <p>Q2. "Eligibility rules change quarterly and must be explainable for 7 years. Design it."</p> <p>Versioned rule sets (specifications with reasons), effective dates, decision records storing rule version + inputs + failed reasons, replay capability to reproduce old decisions, and a review workflow for rule changes.</p> <p>Q3. "How do you avoid the in-memory and SQL versions of a rule diverging?"</p> <p>Single source (AST plus two visitors, or JPA specs evaluated against an in-memory DB in tests), and a shared conformance test suite run against both.</p> <p>Q4. "OR-heavy specifications are slow in Postgres. What do you do?"</p> <p>Check <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>; rewrite ORs as <code>UNION ALL</code> or <code>IN</code>, add composite or partial indexes, push selective filters first, or move faceted search to a search engine.</p> <p>Q5. "Specification vs. a rules engine (Drools)?"</p> <p>Specifications: simple, explicit, compile-time-checked combinations in code. Rules engines: many interacting rules, inference and business-authored rules, at the cost of complexity and opacity. Start with specifications.</p> <p>Problem: Make specifications that produce a parameterized SQL <code>WHERE</code> clause (never concatenating values into SQL), support AND/OR/NOT, keep parameters in the right order, and show that an injection attempt stays a harmless parameter.</p> Solution SqlSpec.javaSqlDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.specification.sql;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/** Challenge solution: specifications that translate themselves into a parameterized SQL WHERE clause. */
public record SqlSpec(String where, List&lt;Object&gt; params) {

    public static SqlSpec eq(String column, Object value) {
        return new SqlSpec(column + " = ?", List.of(value));
    }

    public static SqlSpec lte(String column, Object value) {
        return new SqlSpec(column + " &lt;= ?", List.of(value));
    }

    public static SqlSpec gte(String column, Object value) {
        return new SqlSpec(column + " &gt;= ?", List.of(value));
    }

    public SqlSpec and(SqlSpec other) {
        return combine("AND", other);
    }

    public SqlSpec or(SqlSpec other) {
        return combine("OR", other);
    }

    public SqlSpec not() {
        return new SqlSpec("NOT (" + where + ")", params);
    }

    private SqlSpec combine(String op, SqlSpec other) {
        List&lt;Object&gt; all = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(params);
        all.addAll(other.params);
        return new SqlSpec("(" + where + " " + op + " " + other.where + ")", List.copyOf(all));
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.specification.sql;

import static com.handbook.behavioral.specification.sql.SqlSpec.*;

public class SqlDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SqlSpec filter = gte("stock", 1).and(lte("price_inr", 2000))
                .and(eq("brand", "Pulse").or(gte("rating", 4.5)));
        System.out.println("SELECT * FROM products WHERE " + filter.where());
        System.out.println("params: " + filter.params());

        SqlSpec attack = eq("brand", "x' OR '1'='1");
        System.out.println("Injection attempt stays a parameter: " + attack.where() + " " + attack.params());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE ((stock &gt;= ? AND price_inr &lt;= ?) AND (brand = ? OR rating &gt;= ?))
params: [1, 2000, Pulse, 4.5]
Injection attempt stays a parameter: brand = ? [x' OR '1'='1]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: values go only into the params list (bind with <code>PreparedStatement.setObject</code> in order); combinators add parentheses so precedence is explicit; records keep specs immutable. Column names must come from a whitelist, never from user input.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Marketing's "pre-approved" emails used different eligibility logic than the application, causing complaints.</li> <li>T: One eligibility definition for every channel.</li> <li>A: Built eligibility specifications with reasons in the domain module, translated to SQL for campaigns, and shared a 200-profile conformance suite across both paths.</li> <li>R: Pre-approval-to-approval mismatch fell from 31% to under 2%; audit questions answered from stored reasons in minutes.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the State pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern where an object changes its behavior when its internal state changes, by delegating to a state object that represents the current state.</p> <p>Q2. Give examples.</p> <p>Order lifecycle, ATM, vending machine, traffic light, document approval, TCP connection.</p> <p>Q3. State vs. Strategy?</p> <p>Same structure, different intent. State: states switch themselves based on events. Strategy: the client chooses an algorithm, and strategies don't know each other.</p> <p>Q4. Why not use if-else or switch?</p> <p>With many states and actions, conditionals spread each state's rules across methods, and adding a state means editing every switch. State keeps each state's rules in one class.</p> <p>Q5. Implement a vending machine with states.</p> <p>States <code>NoCoin</code>, <code>HasCoin</code>, <code>Dispensing</code>, <code>SoldOut</code>; each implements <code>insertCoin</code>, <code>selectItem</code>, <code>dispense</code> (rejecting what's not allowed) and returns the next state. The <code>VendingMachine</code> context delegates.</p> <p>Q1. How do you implement State with enums?</p> <p>Each enum constant overrides the methods valid for it and returns the next constant; the base methods throw (see the coding challenge). Good for simple workflows.</p> <p>Q2. Where should state-specific data live?</p> <p>In the state object when it only matters in that state (PIN attempts); in the context when it's shared across states (balance).</p> <p>Q3. How do you persist a state machine in a database?</p> <p>Store the state as a column; apply transitions with optimistic locking or conditional updates; record a transition history table.</p> <p>Q4. How do you handle duplicate events?</p> <p>Idempotency: an event that leads to the state you're already in is a success; track event IDs; reject genuinely invalid transitions.</p> <p>Q5. How do you test a state machine?</p> <p>A table of (state, event) → expected state / error for every combination, which is easy with a pure transition function (Example 3).</p> <p>Q1. "Design the payment lifecycle for a payments platform."</p> <p>States: created → requires_action (3-D Secure) → processing → succeeded / failed / canceled, plus refunds (partial and full) and disputes. Durable state with a transition log; idempotency keys on API calls; webhooks for state changes via outbox; timeouts for stuck processing (reconciliation with the bank); strict invalid-transition handling.</p> <p>Q2. "Two microservices both update an order's state. How do you avoid conflicts?"</p> <p>Single owner of the order state (the order service); others send commands/events; transitions validated by the owner; optimistic concurrency; or a workflow/saga orchestrator owning the flow.</p> <p>Q3. "State objects vs. a transition table vs. a workflow engine?"</p> <p>State objects: rich behavior, in-memory. Transition tables: data-driven, easier to visualize and configure. Workflow engines: long-running, durable timers, retries, human tasks; heavier, but they solve persistence and time.</p> <p>Q4. "How do you migrate a state machine when adding new states to live data?"</p> <p>Add states backward-compatibly (old states still valid), deploy code that understands both, migrate rows if needed, then enable new transitions. Never reinterpret existing states.</p> <p>Q5. "Booleans vs. a status field: argue it."</p> <p>N booleans create 2ⁿ combinations, most impossible; a status enum + state machine makes illegal combinations unrepresentable, gives one place for rules, and gives auditable transitions.</p> <p>Problem (content platform): A document goes DRAFT → IN_REVIEW → APPROVED → PUBLISHED; a reviewer can reject it back to DRAFT. Any other action must fail with a clear message. Implement it with an <code>enum</code>.</p> Solution DocumentStatus.javaApprovalDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.state.approval;

/** Challenge solution: an enum where each constant is a state with its own transitions. */
public enum DocumentStatus {

    DRAFT {
        public DocumentStatus submit() { return IN_REVIEW; }
    },
    IN_REVIEW {
        public DocumentStatus approve() { return APPROVED; }
        public DocumentStatus reject() { return DRAFT; } // back to the author
    },
    APPROVED {
        public DocumentStatus publish() { return PUBLISHED; }
    },
    PUBLISHED;

    public DocumentStatus submit() { return illegal("submit"); }

    public DocumentStatus approve() { return illegal("approve"); }

    public DocumentStatus reject() { return illegal("reject"); }

    public DocumentStatus publish() { return illegal("publish"); }

    private DocumentStatus illegal(String action) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot " + action + ": document is " + this);
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.state.approval;

public class ApprovalDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DocumentStatus s = DocumentStatus.DRAFT;
        s = s.submit();
        System.out.println("submitted -&gt; " + s);
        s = s.reject();
        System.out.println("rejected  -&gt; " + s);
        s = s.submit().approve();
        System.out.println("approved  -&gt; " + s);
        try {
            s.approve();
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("error     -&gt; " + e.getMessage());
        }
        System.out.println("published -&gt; " + s.publish());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>submitted -&gt; IN_REVIEW
rejected  -&gt; DRAFT
approved  -&gt; APPROVED
error     -&gt; Cannot approve: document is APPROVED
published -&gt; PUBLISHED
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: constant-specific method bodies override only legal actions; base methods throw, so the machine is closed by default; enums are serializable and easy to store in a column. Limits: no per-instance data (use classes or records for that).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Duplicate cancel requests caused double refunds on sale days.</li> <li>T: Make order transitions correct under retries and concurrency.</li> <li>A: Replaced boolean flags with a status enum and a State-pattern transition function, conditional SQL updates, idempotency keys, and a transition history table.</li> <li>R: Double refunds dropped to zero; support could finally answer "what happened to my order?" from the history.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Strategy pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that defines a family of algorithms, puts each in its own class behind a common interface, and makes them interchangeable at runtime.</p> <p>Q2. Give a Java example.</p> <p><code>Comparator</code>: <code>Collections.sort(list, comparator)</code> uses whatever comparison strategy you pass.</p> <p>Q3. Which SOLID principles does it follow?</p> <p>Open/Closed (add strategies without changing the context), Single Responsibility (one algorithm per class), and Dependency Inversion (the context depends on the interface).</p> <p>Q4. Strategy vs. State?</p> <p>Same structure. Strategy is chosen by the client and strategies are independent; State objects switch themselves as the object's state changes.</p> <p>Q5. Write a payment strategy example.</p> <p>Interface <code>PaymentStrategy { void pay(int amount); }</code>, classes <code>UpiPayment</code>, <code>CardPayment</code>, <code>WalletPayment</code>; <code>Checkout</code> holds a <code>PaymentStrategy</code> set by the user's choice and calls <code>pay()</code>.</p> <p>Q1. How do lambdas change the Strategy pattern?</p> <p>Single-method strategies become lambdas or method references; no class per strategy. Use classes when strategies have state, multiple methods or need names.</p> <p>Q2. Who should choose the strategy?</p> <p>Not the context. A selector/factory, configuration, dependency injection, or an experiment platform.</p> <p>Q3. How do you use Strategy with Spring?</p> <p>Each strategy is a bean implementing the interface; inject <code>Map&lt;String, Strategy&gt;</code> (bean name → bean) and pick by key at runtime.</p> <p>Q4. Strategy vs. Template Method?</p> <p>Strategy uses composition (swap the whole algorithm at runtime); Template Method uses inheritance (subclasses fill in steps of a fixed algorithm).</p> <p>Q5. How do you test strategies?</p> <p>Each strategy alone with inputs and expected outputs; the selector separately (which strategy for which context); the context with a fake strategy.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a pricing engine that supports many pricing strategies per region and A/B tests."</p> <p>Strategy interface + registry; selection by region/segment/experiment from config; shadow evaluation of new strategies; guardrails (min/max price); decision logging with strategy and version; fast rollback via flags; offline evaluation on historical data.</p> <p>Q2. "Design ride matching for a city with 50,000 drivers."</p> <p>Spatial indexing (geohash/H3) to get candidates; a matching strategy (nearest, ETA-based, batched global optimization with the Hungarian algorithm) chosen per market; constraints (vehicle type, accessibility); fairness; continuous evaluation of metrics (pickup ETA, cancellation rate).</p> <p>Q3. "How would you switch the strategy in production safely?"</p> <p>Feature flag with percentage rollout, shadow mode first, metric comparison, automatic rollback triggers, and decision logs for audit.</p> <p>Q4. "Many strategies need different inputs. How do you design the interface?"</p> <p>Pass a context object (record) with everything strategies might need; or use a two-step design where the selector also prepares inputs. Avoid one giant parameter list.</p> <p>Q5. "When is Strategy over-engineering?"</p> <p>Two stable variants that never change (a simple <code>if</code> is clearer), or "strategies" that are really just different parameters of one algorithm (make it configurable instead).</p> <p>Problem (e-commerce search): Product results can be sorted by <code>relevance</code> (ties broken by lower price), <code>price_asc</code>, or <code>rating</code> (ties broken by more reviews). The sort key comes from the URL. Implement it without <code>if/else</code> on the sort key.</p> Solution SortingDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.strategy.sorting;

import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/** Challenge solution: sort options on a product listing page as Comparator strategies. */
public class SortingDemo {

    record Product(String name, int priceInr, double rating, int reviews, double relevance) {
    }

    static final Map&lt;String, Comparator&lt;Product&gt;&gt; SORTS = Map.of(
            "price_asc", Comparator.comparingInt(Product::priceInr),
            "rating", Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::rating).reversed()
                    .thenComparing(Comparator.comparingInt(Product::reviews).reversed()), // tie-breaker
            "relevance", Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::relevance).reversed()
                    .thenComparingInt(Product::priceInr));

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;Product&gt; results = List.of(
                new Product("Steel Bottle 1L", 499, 4.5, 1200, 0.92),
                new Product("Copper Bottle 1L", 899, 4.7, 310, 0.88),
                new Product("Glass Bottle 750ml", 349, 4.5, 4100, 0.92),
                new Product("Plastic Bottle 1L", 149, 3.9, 800, 0.60));

        for (String sort : List.of("relevance", "price_asc", "rating")) {
            List&lt;String&gt; names = results.stream().sorted(SORTS.get(sort)).map(Product::name).toList();
            System.out.printf("%-10s %s%n", sort, names);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>relevance  [Glass Bottle 750ml, Steel Bottle 1L, Copper Bottle 1L, Plastic Bottle 1L]
price_asc  [Plastic Bottle 1L, Glass Bottle 750ml, Steel Bottle 1L, Copper Bottle 1L]
rating     [Copper Bottle 1L, Glass Bottle 750ml, Steel Bottle 1L, Plastic Bottle 1L]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: each sort is a <code>Comparator</code> strategy, composed with <code>thenComparing</code> for tie-breakers (Glass and Steel tie on relevance, so price decides; Steel and Glass tie on rating, so review count decides); a <code>Map</code> replaces <code>if/else</code>; an unknown key should return 400 or fall back to relevance.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our router sent all payments to the cheapest gateway, which failed badly during a bank outage.</li> <li>T: Make routing reflect real business cost and be changeable without deploys.</li> <li>A: Refactored routing into strategies (fee, success, expected cost), selected per merchant via flags, fed live success rates, added a circuit breaker and decision logging.</li> <li>R: Payment success rose 2.3 points overall; during the next outage traffic shifted within 5 minutes automatically.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Template Method pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern where a base class defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a method, and subclasses implement specific steps without changing the overall structure.</p> <p>Q2. Why is the template method usually <code>final</code>?</p> <p>So subclasses can't change the order of steps or skip mandatory ones.</p> <p>Q3. What is a hook method?</p> <p>An optional step with a default implementation that subclasses may override (e.g., <code>skipFirstLine()</code> returning false).</p> <p>Q4. Give examples in Java.</p> <p><code>HttpServlet.service()</code> calling <code>doGet()</code>/<code>doPost()</code>, <code>AbstractList</code>, Spring's <code>JdbcTemplate</code>.</p> <p>Q5. What is the Hollywood Principle?</p> <p>"Don't call us, we'll call you": the framework/base class calls your code at the right time.</p> <p>Q1. Template Method vs. Strategy?</p> <p>Template Method varies steps via inheritance (fixed at compile time); Strategy swaps the whole algorithm via composition (at runtime).</p> <p>Q2. How does <code>JdbcTemplate</code> apply this pattern?</p> <p>It handles connection, statement, execution, exception translation and cleanup (the skeleton); you provide the SQL and a <code>RowMapper</code> callback (the variable step).</p> <p>Q3. What can go wrong with inheritance-based templates?</p> <p>Tight coupling to the base class, deep hierarchies, and subclasses that violate the template's assumptions (LSP).</p> <p>Q4. Can you implement Template Method without inheritance?</p> <p>Yes: pass the steps as lambdas to a method that runs the skeleton ("template + callbacks").</p> <p>Q5. Where do you put logging, metrics and error handling?</p> <p>In the template, so every variant gets them consistently.</p> <p>Q1. "Design an internal job framework used by 40 teams."</p> <p>A template (<code>final execute()</code>) handling distributed locking, scheduling, retries, timeouts, metrics, tracing and alerting; teams implement <code>doWork()</code> and optional hooks (<code>shouldRun</code>, <code>onFailure</code>). Or a callback API. Version the framework; document hooks; test the template with fake jobs.</p> <p>Q2. "How would you enforce compliance steps (audit, consent checks) across many services?"</p> <p>Shared libraries with template methods/callbacks that wrap business logic; AOP for cross-cutting concerns; or platform-level enforcement (gateways, sidecars). Each has trade-offs in flexibility and bypass risk.</p> <p>Q3. "A template has grown 12 hooks and 3 levels of subclasses. Refactor?"</p> <p>Split the varying parts into strategies injected into one template, flatten the hierarchy, remove rarely used hooks, and add tests that pin the template's order.</p> <p>Q4. "Inheritance vs. composition for a payments SDK's flows?"</p> <p>For external SDK users, composition/callbacks are safer (no fragile base-class problem, easier evolution). Internally, a <code>final</code> template with hooks works well when all variants are in your control.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you test a template method?"</p> <p>Test the skeleton with a fake subclass that records the call order; test each concrete step separately; and test the hooks' default behavior.</p> <p>Problem: Several scheduled jobs (invoice generation, cache warm-up, GST report) all need: a "should run" switch, a lock so two instances don't run at once, start/finish logging, failure handling, and guaranteed lock release even when the job throws. Teams should only write the job's work.</p> Solution ScheduledJob.javaJobsDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.template.jobs;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

/** Challenge solution: every scheduled job gets locking, skipping and cleanup for free. */
public abstract class ScheduledJob {

    private static final Set&lt;String&gt; LOCKS = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(); // stand-in for a DB/Redis lock

    public final void execute() {
        if (!shouldRun()) {
            System.out.println(name() + ": skipped");
            return;
        }
        if (!LOCKS.add(name())) {
            System.out.println(name() + ": another instance is running, skipping");
            return;
        }
        try {
            System.out.println(name() + ": started");
            doWork();
            System.out.println(name() + ": finished");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(name() + ": FAILED - " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            LOCKS.remove(name()); // always released, even on failure
        }
    }

    protected abstract String name();

    protected abstract void doWork();

    protected boolean shouldRun() { return true; }

    static boolean isLocked(String name) { return LOCKS.contains(name); }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.template.jobs;

public class JobsDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new ScheduledJob() {
            protected String name() { return "invoice-generation"; }
            protected void doWork() { System.out.println("  generated 1,240 invoices"); }
        }.execute();

        new ScheduledJob() {
            protected String name() { return "cache-warmup"; }
            protected boolean shouldRun() { return false; } // disabled during the sale freeze
            protected void doWork() { }
        }.execute();

        new ScheduledJob() {
            protected String name() { return "gst-report"; }
            protected void doWork() { throw new IllegalStateException("tax service unavailable"); }
        }.execute();
        System.out.println("gst-report lock released? " + !ScheduledJob.isLocked("gst-report"));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>invoice-generation: started
  generated 1,240 invoices
invoice-generation: finished
cache-warmup: skipped
gst-report: started
gst-report: FAILED - tax service unavailable
gst-report lock released? true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>final execute()</code> owns the lifecycle; <code>doWork()</code> is the only required step; <code>shouldRun()</code> is a hook; <code>finally</code> guarantees lock release. In production the lock would be distributed (ShedLock, Redis), and failures would emit metrics and alerts.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Each team wrote its own scheduled jobs; some forgot locking, so duplicate invoices went out when two pods ran the same job.</li> <li>T: Make every job safe by default.</li> <li>A: Built a job base class with a <code>final execute()</code> handling distributed locks, metrics and alerts; migrated 23 jobs; added a lint rule against raw <code>@Scheduled</code> methods.</li> <li>R: Zero duplicate-run incidents after migration; new jobs needed about 20 lines instead of 150.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Visitor pattern?</p> <p>A behavioral pattern that lets you add new operations to a set of classes without modifying them, by moving each operation into a separate visitor class.</p> <p>Q2. What is double dispatch?</p> <p>The method called depends on two runtime types: <code>element.accept(visitor)</code> dispatches on the element, and <code>visitor.visit(this)</code> dispatches on the visitor, so the right combination runs.</p> <p>Q3. When should you use Visitor?</p> <p>When the element types are stable but you keep adding operations (tax, shipping, export).</p> <p>Q4. Main disadvantage?</p> <p>Adding a new element type requires changing every visitor.</p> <p>Q5. Give a Java example.</p> <p><code>Files.walkFileTree(path, FileVisitor)</code> and compiler/AST visitors.</p> <p>Q1. Why must every element implement <code>accept</code> itself?</p> <p>Because overload resolution is compile-time: inside each class, <code>this</code> has that class's static type, so <code>v.visit(this)</code> selects the right overload.</p> <p>Q2. How do Java 21 features change Visitor?</p> <p>Sealed interfaces + pattern-matching <code>switch</code> give exhaustive type-based dispatch without <code>accept</code>/visitor boilerplate (Example 2).</p> <p>Q3. How do you return values from visitors?</p> <p>Make the visitor generic: <code>interface Visitor&lt;R&gt; { R visitX(X x); }</code> and <code>&lt;R&gt; R accept(Visitor&lt;R&gt; v)</code> (Example 3).</p> <p>Q4. Visitor vs. Strategy?</p> <p>Strategy swaps one algorithm for one context type; Visitor defines an operation across many element types.</p> <p>Q5. How do you visit a tree?</p> <p>Composite nodes call <code>accept</code> on their children (structure-driven), or the visitor recurses into children (visitor-driven, which allows pruning).</p> <p>Q1. "Explain the expression problem and how you'd choose a design."</p> <p>Adding types is easy with OOP polymorphism, adding operations is easy with Visitor/pattern matching; neither makes both easy. Choose based on which axis changes more; in Java 21, sealed + switch for operations, open interfaces for types.</p> <p>Q2. "Design a SQL query builder that supports Postgres, MySQL and BigQuery."</p> <p>Build an AST of the query (Composite), then dialect visitors render it (quoting, limits, functions); add optimizer passes as visitors; tests per dialect with golden outputs.</p> <p>Q3. "A visitor-based tax engine must support 30 countries. Structure?"</p> <p>Stable item categories as elements; one visitor per jurisdiction (or a visitor parameterized by rate tables); effective-dated rules; exhaustive handling; golden-file tests per country.</p> <p>Q4. "How would you add a new element type to a public visitor API without breaking third parties?"</p> <p>Add <code>visit(NewType)</code> as a default method that falls back to a generic <code>visitOther(Element)</code> (or throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>), document it, and version the API. Accept the safety trade-off consciously.</p> <p>Q5. "When would you choose a classic visitor over pattern matching in 2026?"</p> <p>When element types live in a library you don't control (can't seal), when you need two-hierarchy double dispatch, or when third parties add operations via a published interface.</p> <p>Problem: Shapes (circle, rectangle, triangle) need area and perimeter, and more operations later (bounding box, SVG). Implement a generic <code>ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt;</code> so operations return values and shape classes never change.</p> Solution ShapesDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.behavioral.visitor.shapes;

import java.util.List;

/** Challenge solution: area and perimeter as visitors over a closed shape hierarchy. */
public class ShapesDemo {

    interface ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt; {
        R circle(Circle c);
        R rectangle(Rectangle r);
        R triangle(Triangle t);
    }

    interface Shape {
        &lt;R&gt; R accept(ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt; v);
    }

    record Circle(double r) implements Shape {
        public &lt;R&gt; R accept(ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt; v) { return v.circle(this); }
    }

    record Rectangle(double w, double h) implements Shape {
        public &lt;R&gt; R accept(ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt; v) { return v.rectangle(this); }
    }

    record Triangle(double a, double b, double c) implements Shape {
        public &lt;R&gt; R accept(ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt; v) { return v.triangle(this); }
    }

    static final ShapeVisitor&lt;Double&gt; AREA = new ShapeVisitor&lt;&gt;() {
        public Double circle(Circle c) { return Math.PI * c.r() * c.r(); }
        public Double rectangle(Rectangle r) { return r.w() * r.h(); }
        public Double triangle(Triangle t) { // Heron's formula
            double s = (t.a() + t.b() + t.c()) / 2;
            return Math.sqrt(s * (s - t.a()) * (s - t.b()) * (s - t.c()));
        }
    };

    static final ShapeVisitor&lt;Double&gt; PERIMETER = new ShapeVisitor&lt;&gt;() {
        public Double circle(Circle c) { return 2 * Math.PI * c.r(); }
        public Double rectangle(Rectangle r) { return 2 * (r.w() + r.h()); }
        public Double triangle(Triangle t) { return t.a() + t.b() + t.c(); }
    };

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (Shape s : List.of(new Circle(1), new Rectangle(3, 4), new Triangle(3, 4, 5))) {
            System.out.printf("%-30s area=%6.2f perimeter=%6.2f%n", s, s.accept(AREA), s.accept(PERIMETER));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Circle[r=1.0]                  area=  3.14 perimeter=  6.28
Rectangle[w=3.0, h=4.0]        area= 12.00 perimeter= 14.00
Triangle[a=3.0, b=4.0, c=5.0]  area=  6.00 perimeter= 12.00
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>&lt;R&gt; R accept(ShapeVisitor&lt;R&gt; v)</code> gives typed results; visitors are stateless constants (safe to reuse); the triangle uses Heron's formula. Then mention the sealed + switch alternative: <code>double area(Shape s) { return switch (s) { case Circle c -&gt; ...; ... }; }</code>.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A new item type silently got 0% GST because the tax visitor had no-op defaults.</li> <li>T: Fix the shortfall and make tax rules impossible to forget.</li> <li>A: Removed visitor defaults, moved tax rules to a sealed hierarchy with exhaustive switches, and added a test running every operation over every item type.</li> <li>R: The next two item types (gift cards, refurbished electronics) failed to compile until tax rules were added. That is exactly what we wanted.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is double-checked locking?</p> <p>A lazy-initialization technique: check if the instance exists without a lock; if not, lock and check again before creating it, so the lock is only used during initialization.</p> <p>Q2. Why check twice?</p> <p>Between the first check and getting the lock, another thread may have created the instance. The second check prevents creating it twice.</p> <p>Q3. Why is <code>volatile</code> needed?</p> <p>To stop reordering: without it, another thread can see the reference before the object is fully constructed and use a half-built object.</p> <p>Q4. Write a DCL Singleton.</p> <p><code>private static volatile X instance;</code> and <code>getInstance()</code>: <code>X i = instance; if (i == null) { synchronized (X.class) { i = instance; if (i == null) instance = i = new X(); } } return i;</code></p> <p>Q5. What's a simpler alternative for singletons?</p> <p>The initialization-on-demand holder idiom or an enum singleton.</p> <p>Q1. Why was DCL broken before Java 5?</p> <p>The old memory model allowed the reference write to be reordered before constructor writes even with <code>volatile</code>; JSR-133 (Java 5) strengthened <code>volatile</code> semantics, making DCL correct.</p> <p>Q2. How do you lazily create one object per key safely?</p> <p><code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent(key, factory)</code> (atomic per key), or a cache library.</p> <p>Q3. Why read the volatile field into a local variable?</p> <p>Fewer volatile reads on the fast path (performance), and it avoids reading two different values in one call.</p> <p>Q4. What happens if initialization throws an exception in DCL?</p> <p>The field stays <code>null</code>; the next call retries. Decide whether that's the behavior you want.</p> <p>Q5. Is DCL OK with virtual threads?</p> <p>Yes, since the lock is only held briefly during initialization. If initialization blocks on I/O, prefer <code>ReentrantLock</code> (avoids pinning on JDK 21–23).</p> <p>Q1. "Explain the Java Memory Model reason DCL needs volatile, in terms of happens-before."</p> <p>Without <code>volatile</code>, there's no happens-before edge between the constructor's writes in the creating thread and the reads in another thread that sees the non-null reference via a data race. A volatile write of the reference after construction and a volatile read before use create that edge, guaranteeing the fields are visible.</p> <p>Q2. "When is it safe to lazily cache without any synchronization?"</p> <p>Racy single-check: the cached value is immutable (safe publication via final fields) or a primitive that's written atomically (not <code>long</code>/<code>double</code>), and recomputation is idempotent and cheap, like <code>String.hashCode()</code>.</p> <p>Q3. "Lazy loading caused latency spikes after deploys. What would you change?"</p> <p>Eager warm-up behind a readiness probe; pre-load models and caches; traffic shifting (canary); keep lazy loading only for rarely used resources.</p> <p>Q4. "Design a Lazy utility for a large codebase." <p>Volatile value + DCL; clear the supplier after use (avoid memory leaks); reject <code>null</code> or support it with a sentinel; decide on exception caching; optional reset for tests; document thread-safety.</p> <p>Q5. "What JDK features are coming for lazy constants?"</p> <p>Stable values (JEP 502, preview): JVM-trusted lazily initialized constants that can be constant-folded like <code>static final</code>, replacing many DCL and holder use cases.</p> <p>Problem: Write a reusable, thread-safe <code>Lazy&lt;T&gt;</code> (a memoizing <code>Supplier</code>) that runs its initializer exactly once even under heavy contention, rejects <code>null</code>, and releases the initializer after use so anything it captured can be garbage collected.</p> Solution Lazy.javaLazyDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.dcl.lazy;

import java.util.Objects;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

/** Challenge solution: a thread-safe memoizing supplier (like Kotlin's lazy or Guava's memoize). */
public final class Lazy&lt;T&gt; implements Supplier&lt;T&gt; {

    private volatile Supplier&lt;? extends T&gt; initializer; // cleared after use so captured objects can be GC'd
    private volatile T value;

    private Lazy(Supplier&lt;? extends T&gt; initializer) {
        this.initializer = Objects.requireNonNull(initializer);
    }

    public static &lt;T&gt; Lazy&lt;T&gt; of(Supplier&lt;? extends T&gt; initializer) {
        return new Lazy&lt;&gt;(initializer);
    }

    @Override
    public T get() {
        T v = value;
        if (v == null) {
            synchronized (this) {
                v = value;
                if (v == null) {
                    v = Objects.requireNonNull(initializer.get(), "initializer returned null");
                    value = v;            // publish (volatile write)
                    initializer = null;   // release the lambda and whatever it captured
                }
            }
        }
        return v;
    }

    public boolean isInitialized() {
        return value != null;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.dcl.lazy;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class LazyDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AtomicInteger loads = new AtomicInteger();
        Lazy&lt;String&gt; holidayCalendar = Lazy.of(() -&gt; {
            loads.incrementAndGet();
            return "Bank holiday calendar 2027 (loaded from the core banking system)";
        });
        System.out.println("Initialized before first use? " + holidayCalendar.isInitialized());
        try (ExecutorService ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 200; i++) {
                ex.submit(holidayCalendar::get);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Value : " + holidayCalendar.get());
        System.out.println("Loads : " + loads.get());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Initialized before first use? false
Value : Bank holiday calendar 2027 (loaded from the core banking system)
Loads : 1
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: DCL with a <code>volatile</code> value; the initializer runs inside the lock; <code>initializer = null</code> after success avoids holding captured objects forever; <code>null</code> is rejected because <code>null</code> means "not initialized yet". The unit test releases 100 threads at the same instant with a latch and asserts exactly one initialization, repeated 5 times.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Rare <code>NullPointerException</code>s in pricing appeared only under peak load after deploys.</li> <li>T: Find and permanently fix a bug that wouldn't reproduce locally.</li> <li>A: Reviewed every lazy-initialization site, found DCL without <code>volatile</code>, fixed it, added a shared <code>Lazy&lt;T&gt;</code> utility and a lint rule against hand-written DCL, and moved critical resources to eager warm-up.</li> <li>R: The errors disappeared; post-deploy p99 also improved because the first requests no longer paid for initialization.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a <code>Future</code> in Java?</p> <p>An object representing the result of an asynchronous computation; <code>get()</code> blocks until it's available.</p> <p>Q2. Limitations of <code>Future</code> (before Java 8)?</p> <p>No way to chain steps, combine futures, complete it manually, or attach callbacks; <code>get()</code> blocks. <code>CompletableFuture</code> fixes these.</p> <p>Q3. <code>thenApply</code> vs <code>thenCompose</code>?</p> <p><code>thenApply</code> transforms a result with a plain function (<code>map</code>); <code>thenCompose</code> chains a function that returns another future (<code>flatMap</code>).</p> <p>Q4. How do you run three calls in parallel and wait for all?</p> <p>Three <code>supplyAsync</code> calls, then <code>CompletableFuture.allOf(f1, f2, f3).join()</code> and read each result.</p> <p>Q5. <code>get()</code> vs <code>join()</code>?</p> <p>Both block. <code>get()</code> throws checked <code>InterruptedException</code>/<code>ExecutionException</code>; <code>join()</code> throws unchecked <code>CompletionException</code>.</p> <p>Q1. How do you handle exceptions in a <code>CompletableFuture</code> chain?</p> <p><code>exceptionally</code> (recover with a value), <code>handle</code> (result or error into a value), <code>whenComplete</code> (side effects like logging, result unchanged). Unwrap <code>CompletionException</code> for the cause.</p> <p>Q2. Which thread runs <code>supplyAsync</code> and its callbacks?</p> <p><code>supplyAsync</code> without an executor uses <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code>. Non-async callbacks (<code>thenApply</code>) run on the thread that completed the previous stage, or on the caller if it's already done; <code>*Async</code> variants use the given or common executor.</p> <p>Q3. How do you add a timeout?</p> <p><code>orTimeout(t, unit)</code> fails with <code>TimeoutException</code>; <code>completeOnTimeout(value, t, unit)</code> completes with a fallback (Java 9+).</p> <p>Q4. <code>anyOf</code> vs a "first successful" combinator?</p> <p><code>anyOf</code> completes with the first future to complete, even if it failed. For first success, track failures and complete only on success, or after all fail (challenge).</p> <p>Q5. Should you use <code>CompletableFuture</code> with virtual threads?</p> <p>Often you don't need to: virtual threads make simple blocking code scalable. Use <code>CompletableFuture</code> for composition, async APIs and timeouts, still with an explicit executor.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a search aggregator calling 20 suppliers with a 1-second SLA."</p> <p>Parallel calls with per-supplier timeouts under the SLA, partial results, hedging for slow suppliers, circuit breakers per supplier, caching popular searches, streaming results to the UI as they arrive (SSE), and deadline propagation.</p> <p>Q2. "Our async code loses trace IDs and the security context. Why, and how do you fix it?"</p> <p><code>ThreadLocal</code>s don't cross thread boundaries. Use context-propagating executors (<code>TaskDecorator</code>, Micrometer Context Propagation), or Reactor context, or <code>ScopedValue</code> with structured concurrency.</p> <p>Q3. "How do you cancel in-flight work when the user navigates away?"</p> <p>Propagate cancellation: cancel the top-level future, use executors with interruptible tasks (<code>Future.cancel(true)</code>), set client timeouts, or use structured concurrency, which cancels child tasks when the scope closes.</p> <p>Q4. "CompletableFuture vs Reactor vs virtual threads for a new service?"</p> <p>Virtual threads for request/response services with blocking libraries (simplest); Reactor when you need streaming with back-pressure or are already reactive end-to-end; <code>CompletableFuture</code> for targeted fan-out and async APIs.</p> <p>Q5. "Explain hedged requests and when they're worth it."</p> <p>Send a second request to another replica if the first hasn't answered by roughly p95 latency, and use whichever answers first. It cuts tail latency at the cost of extra load (about 5%). Only for idempotent reads.</p> <p>Problem: Download an app update from several mirrors in parallel. Return the first successful download; ignore failures unless all mirrors fail, in which case fail with a clear message. (<code>anyOf</code> isn't enough: it returns the first completion, even a failure.)</p> Solution FirstSuccessful.javaMirrorsDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.future.mirrors;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/** Challenge solution: complete with the first SUCCESSFUL result; fail only if every attempt fails. */
public final class FirstSuccessful {

    private FirstSuccessful() {
    }

    public static &lt;T&gt; CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt; of(List&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;&gt; attempts) {
        CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt; result = new CompletableFuture&lt;&gt;();
        AtomicInteger failures = new AtomicInteger();
        for (CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt; attempt : attempts) {
            attempt.whenComplete((value, error) -&gt; {
                if (error == null) {
                    result.complete(value); // first success wins; later calls are ignored
                } else if (failures.incrementAndGet() == attempts.size()) {
                    result.completeExceptionally(new IllegalStateException("all " + attempts.size() + " attempts failed", error));
                }
            });
        }
        return result;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.future.mirrors;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletionException;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class MirrorsDemo {

    static CompletableFuture&lt;String&gt; download(ExecutorService io, String mirror, long ms, boolean fails) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(ms);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
            if (fails) {
                throw new IllegalStateException(mirror + " returned 500");
            }
            return "app-v2.apk from " + mirror;
        }, io);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (ExecutorService io = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            // The fastest mirror fails; anyOf() would report that failure. FirstSuccessful waits for a good one.
            String got = FirstSuccessful.of(List.of(
                    download(io, "mirror-mumbai", 20, true),
                    download(io, "mirror-singapore", 80, false),
                    download(io, "mirror-frankfurt", 150, false))).join();
            System.out.println("Downloaded: " + got);

            try {
                FirstSuccessful.of(List.of(download(io, "a", 10, true), download(io, "b", 20, true))).join();
            } catch (CompletionException e) {
                System.out.println("All mirrors down: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Downloaded: app-v2.apk from mirror-singapore
All mirrors down: all 2 attempts failed
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: a manually completed <code>CompletableFuture</code> acts as the promise; <code>complete()</code> is idempotent (first call wins); an <code>AtomicInteger</code> counts failures to detect "all failed"; follow-up: cancel the remaining downloads after the first success.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our product page called 6 services sequentially; p99 was 1.8 s and one slow service could stall everything.</li> <li>T: Get p99 under 400 ms without changing the downstream services.</li> <li>A: Parallelized independent calls with <code>CompletableFuture</code> on a dedicated executor, added per-call timeouts with fallbacks for optional sections, and hedged the slowest read.</li> <li>R: p99 dropped to 320 ms; when the reviews service had an outage, pages still rendered without reviews.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is an immutable object?</p> <p>An object whose state can't change after creation, like <code>String</code>, <code>Integer</code>, <code>LocalDate</code>.</p> <p>Q2. How do you create an immutable class?</p> <p><code>final</code> class (or record), <code>private final</code> fields, no setters, defensive copies of mutable inputs and outputs, and initialize everything in the constructor.</p> <p>Q3. Why is <code>String</code> immutable?</p> <p>For security (class names, file paths can't be changed after checks), thread safety, the string pool (sharing literals), and cached hash codes (fast map keys).</p> <p>Q4. Are immutable objects thread-safe?</p> <p>Yes: no thread can modify them, so no synchronization is needed to read them.</p> <p>Q5. Is a record automatically immutable?</p> <p>Shallowly: its fields are final, but if a field holds a mutable object (an <code>ArrayList</code>), that object can still change. Copy it in the compact constructor.</p> <p>Q1. <code>List.copyOf</code> vs <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code>?</p> <p><code>copyOf</code> makes an independent, unmodifiable copy. <code>unmodifiableList</code> is a read-only view of the original, which still reflects later changes to it.</p> <p>Q2. How do you "modify" an immutable object?</p> <p>Return a new instance with the change (<code>withX</code>, <code>plus</code>), or use a builder with <code>toBuilder()</code>.</p> <p>Q3. What are final-field semantics in the JMM?</p> <p>Correctly constructed objects' <code>final</code> fields are visible to all threads after construction without synchronization, provided <code>this</code> doesn't escape the constructor.</p> <p>Q4. How do you share changing configuration safely without locks?</p> <p>Keep an immutable snapshot in an <code>AtomicReference</code>; readers <code>get()</code>; writers build a new snapshot and swap (Example 3).</p> <p>Q5. Why are immutable objects good map keys?</p> <p>Their <code>hashCode</code> can't change after insertion; a mutated key in a <code>HashMap</code> becomes unreachable.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a thread-safe, high-read configuration system with rollback."</p> <p>Versioned immutable snapshots in an <code>AtomicReference</code>; validate before publishing; keep the last N snapshots for instant rollback; distribute with a version number; log which version each request used.</p> <p>Q2. "What are the costs of immutability, and how do you mitigate them?"</p> <p>Allocation and copying. Mitigate with structural sharing (persistent collections), batching changes into one new snapshot, and confining mutation to a builder or one thread before publishing.</p> <p>Q3. "How does immutability help distributed systems?"</p> <p>Immutable data can be cached and replicated without invalidation problems; events and logs can be replayed; conflicts are avoided (CRDTs, append-only stores).</p> <p>Q4. "Can you safely publish an immutable object through a non-volatile field?"</p> <p>Yes, if it's properly immutable (all fields final, no escaping <code>this</code>). Readers may see a stale reference but never a partially constructed object. Mutable objects need safe publication (volatile, locks).</p> <p>Q5. "Records vs. Lombok <code>@Value</code> vs. hand-written immutable classes?"</p> <p>Records: language-level, concise, no inheritance, value semantics. <code>@Value</code>: works on older Java, supports more customization. Hand-written: when you need caching, lazy derived fields or a custom API. All need defensive copies for mutable components.</p> <p>Problem: <code>MutableBooking</code> has <code>final</code> fields and no setters, but it isn't immutable. Show two ways it leaks mutable state, then write an immutable <code>Booking</code>.</p> Solution BookingDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.immutable.booking;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.List;

/** Challenge: find the leaks in MutableBooking, then fix them in Booking. */
public class BookingDemo {

    /** BEFORE: looks immutable (final fields, no setters) but leaks mutable state. */
    static final class MutableBooking {
        private final Date checkIn;
        private final List&lt;String&gt; guests;

        MutableBooking(Date checkIn, List&lt;String&gt; guests) {
            this.checkIn = checkIn;      // stores the caller's object
            this.guests = guests;        // stores the caller's list
        }

        Date checkIn() { return checkIn; }           // hands out the internal object

        List&lt;String&gt; guests() { return guests; }     // hands out the internal list
    }

    /** AFTER: an immutable record with immutable types and defensive copies. */
    record Booking(LocalDate checkIn, List&lt;String&gt; guests) {
        Booking {
            guests = List.copyOf(guests); // copy in; List.copyOf is also unmodifiable for callers
        }
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("deprecation")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Date date = new Date(126, 11, 27); // 27 Dec 2026, java.util.Date is mutable
        List&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of("Asha"));
        MutableBooking leaky = new MutableBooking(date, names);
        date.setDate(31);                  // caller changes its Date...
        leaky.guests().add("Intruder");    // ...or anyone changes the returned list
        System.out.println("Leaky booking   : check-in day " + leaky.checkIn().getDate() + ", guests " + leaky.guests());

        List&lt;String&gt; names2 = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of("Asha"));
        Booking safe = new Booking(LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 27), names2);
        names2.add("Intruder");
        try {
            safe.guests().add("Intruder");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Fixed booking   : guests list is unmodifiable");
        }
        System.out.println("Fixed booking   : " + safe);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Leaky booking   : check-in day 31, guests [Asha, Intruder]
Fixed booking   : guests list is unmodifiable
Fixed booking   : Booking[checkIn=2026-12-27, guests=[Asha]]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: leak 1: storing the caller's <code>Date</code>/list (the caller can change them later); leak 2: returning internal objects (anyone can change them). Fix: an immutable type (<code>LocalDate</code> instead of <code>Date</code>) and <code>List.copyOf</code> in the compact constructor, which copies in and makes the list unmodifiable.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: 0.3% of invoices didn't match charged amounts because a shared mutable order changed after payment.</li> <li>T: Guarantee every downstream service sees exactly the paid order.</li> <li>A: Introduced an immutable order snapshot (record + defensive copies) created at payment time; all consumers moved to it; post-payment add-ons became separate orders.</li> <li>R: Invoice/charge mismatches went to zero; support tickets for "wrong invoice" dropped by 95%.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Producer-Consumer problem?</p> <p>Producers create data and consumers process it, sharing a buffer. The challenge is to coordinate them safely: producers wait when the buffer is full, consumers wait when it's empty, without races.</p> <p>Q2. How do you solve it in Java?</p> <p>Use a <code>BlockingQueue</code>: producers call <code>put()</code>, consumers call <code>take()</code>; the queue handles all locking and waiting.</p> <p>Q3. <code>put()</code> vs <code>offer()</code>?</p> <p><code>put()</code> blocks until space is available; <code>offer()</code> returns false immediately (or after a timeout) if the queue is full.</p> <p>Q4. How do you stop consumers?</p> <p>Send a poison pill (one per consumer), or interrupt the consumer threads (<code>shutdownNow</code>).</p> <p>Q5. Write it with <code>wait()</code>/<code>notify()</code>.</p> <p>A <code>synchronized</code> buffer: <code>put</code> waits in a while loop while full, adds, then <code>notifyAll()</code>; <code>take</code> waits while empty, removes, then <code>notifyAll()</code> (see the coding challenge).</p> <p>Q1. Why must <code>wait()</code> be called in a loop?</p> <p>Because of spurious wakeups and because another thread may have changed the condition between <code>notifyAll()</code> and this thread reacquiring the lock. Always re-check the condition.</p> <p>Q2. <code>notify()</code> vs <code>notifyAll()</code>?</p> <p><code>notify()</code> wakes one arbitrary waiter, which may be the wrong kind (a producer when a consumer is needed), causing a lost wakeup and a hang. <code>notifyAll()</code> is safer for shared conditions; or use <code>ReentrantLock</code> with separate <code>Condition</code>s (<code>notFull</code>, <code>notEmpty</code>).</p> <p>Q3. <code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code> vs <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code>?</p> <p>Array: fixed, bounded, single lock. Linked: optionally bounded (unbounded by default), separate locks for head and tail, usually higher throughput.</p> <p>Q4. How does <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> use this pattern?</p> <p><code>submit()</code> produces tasks into its <code>BlockingQueue</code>; worker threads consume them. When the queue is full and max threads are busy, the rejection policy applies.</p> <p>Q5. How do you implement batching on the consumer side?</p> <p><code>poll(timeout)</code> for the first item, then <code>drainTo(batch, max - 1)</code>; write the batch; repeat (Example 2).</p> <p>Q1. "Design an ingestion pipeline for 1 million events per second."</p> <p>Partitioned Kafka topics (key by entity for ordering), stateless producers with batching and compression, consumer groups sized to partitions, idempotent consumers, batch writes to storage, DLQ for poison messages, back-pressure via consumer lag monitoring and autoscaling.</p> <p>Q2. "The queue keeps growing. Diagnose and fix."</p> <p>Measure arrival rate vs. service rate and consumer latency; look for slow dependencies, GC, lock contention. Fix: scale consumers (if the downstream allows), batch, optimize consumers, shed or degrade low-priority work, and bound the queue with an explicit full policy.</p> <p>Q3. "Guarantee per-customer ordering with multiple consumers."</p> <p>Partition by key (hash customer ID to N queues, one consumer each, or Kafka partitions); within a partition processing is sequential.</p> <p>Q4. "In-memory queue vs. message broker?"</p> <p>In-memory: fast and simple, but no durability, single process. Broker: durability, replay, cross-service fan-out, at the cost of operations and latency. Use in-memory for best-effort work (logs, metrics) and brokers for business events.</p> <p>Q5. "How do reactive streams handle back-pressure differently?"</p> <p>Consumers request(n) items; producers emit at most what was requested (pull-based), instead of blocking threads on a full queue.</p> <p>Problem: Implement a bounded buffer without <code>java.util.concurrent</code>: <code>put</code> blocks when full, <code>take</code> blocks when empty, using <code>synchronized</code>, <code>wait()</code> and <code>notifyAll()</code>. Prove nothing is lost with one producer and one consumer (sum of 1..1000).</p> Solution BoundedBuffer.javaBufferDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.producerconsumer.buffer;

/** Challenge solution: the classic bounded buffer with intrinsic locks, wait() and notifyAll(). */
public class BoundedBuffer&lt;T&gt; {

    private final Object[] items;
    private int head;
    private int tail;
    private int count;

    public BoundedBuffer(int capacity) {
        items = new Object[capacity];
    }

    public synchronized void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {
        while (count == items.length) { // ALWAYS a loop: spurious wakeups and competing producers
            wait();
        }
        items[tail] = item;
        tail = (tail + 1) % items.length;
        count++;
        notifyAll(); // wake consumers (and producers; notifyAll avoids lost wakeups)
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {
        while (count == 0) {
            wait();
        }
        T item = (T) items[head];
        items[head] = null; // let GC reclaim it
        head = (head + 1) % items.length;
        count--;
        notifyAll();
        return item;
    }

    public synchronized int size() {
        return count;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.producerconsumer.buffer;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class BufferDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        BoundedBuffer&lt;Integer&gt; buffer = new BoundedBuffer&lt;&gt;(4);
        AtomicLong sum = new AtomicLong();

        Thread consumer = Thread.ofPlatform().start(() -&gt; {
            try {
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1_000; i++) {
                    sum.addAndGet(buffer.take());
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 1_000; i++) {
            buffer.put(i);
        }
        consumer.join();
        System.out.println("Sum of 1..1000 received: " + sum.get() + " (expected 500500)");
        System.out.println("Buffer empty at the end: " + (buffer.size() == 0));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Sum of 1..1000 received: 500500 (expected 500500)
Buffer empty at the end: true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: circular array with <code>head</code>, <code>tail</code>, <code>count</code>; <code>while</code>, not <code>if</code>, around <code>wait()</code>; <code>notifyAll()</code> to avoid lost wakeups; null out taken slots for GC. The unit test runs 4 producers and 4 consumers on virtual threads and checks all 10,000 items arrive. In real code, use <code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code>.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: An unbounded async-logging queue caused OOM on all checkout pods during an incident.</li> <li>T: Make logging safe under downstream failure.</li> <li>A: Bounded the queue, added priority-based discarding, exposed queue depth as a metric with alerts, and wrote a load test that slows the log collector.</li> <li>R: The next collector outage caused dropped debug logs, not an outage.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a read-write lock?</p> <p>A lock with a shared read lock (many readers at once) and an exclusive write lock (one writer, no readers).</p> <p>Q2. When should you use it?</p> <p>For read-heavy shared data with occasional writes, like caches and configuration.</p> <p>Q3. Which Java class implements it?</p> <p><code>ReentrantReadWriteLock</code> (and <code>StampedLock</code> for optimistic reads).</p> <p>Q4. Can a reader and a writer hold it at the same time?</p> <p>No. Readers can share with readers only.</p> <p>Q5. Why not just use <code>synchronized</code>?</p> <p><code>synchronized</code> makes readers wait for each other unnecessarily; a read-write lock lets them run in parallel.</p> <p>Q1. Can you upgrade a read lock to a write lock?</p> <p>No with <code>ReentrantReadWriteLock</code>: it deadlocks. Release the read lock, acquire the write lock, and re-check the condition. Downgrading (write → read) is allowed.</p> <p>Q2. What is writer starvation?</p> <p>In non-fair mode, a continuous stream of readers can keep a writer waiting indefinitely. Fair mode or a writer-preference policy prevents it.</p> <p>Q3. What is a <code>StampedLock</code> optimistic read?</p> <p><code>tryOptimisticRead()</code> returns a stamp without locking; read fields into locals; <code>validate(stamp)</code> tells you whether a write happened; if so, retry with a real read lock.</p> <p>Q4. When is <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> better than a map with a read-write lock?</p> <p>When operations are per key and you don't need consistency across several keys. It has lock-free reads and fine-grained writes.</p> <p>Q5. Do read-write locks work well with virtual threads?</p> <p>Yes. <code>java.util.concurrent</code> locks don't pin carrier threads (unlike <code>synchronized</code> before JDK 24).</p> <p>Q1. "Design an in-memory config service read 100k times per second, updated once a minute."</p> <p>Immutable snapshot + <code>AtomicReference</code>: readers do one volatile read; the updater builds a new snapshot off-lock, validates it, swaps it, and publishes a version. No read locks at all. Add a version for cache invalidation downstream.</p> <p>Q2. "A cache refresh under a write lock causes latency spikes. Fix it."</p> <p>Move I/O and computation outside the lock; hold the write lock only for the swap, or replace it with copy-on-write snapshots.</p> <p>Q3. "How do databases let readers and writers proceed concurrently?"</p> <p>MVCC: writers create new row versions; readers see a consistent snapshot of older versions, with no read locks. Conflicts are detected at write time.</p> <p>Q4. "When would you pick StampedLock over ReentrantReadWriteLock?"</p> <p>Very read-heavy, short reads of a few fields, no reentrancy needed, and the team is comfortable with the validation idiom. Benchmark first.</p> <p>Q5. "Explain lock striping."</p> <p>Split one lock into N locks by key hash (like early <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> segments), so operations on different keys rarely contend. It increases concurrency for keyed data structures.</p> <p>Problem: Implement a read-write lock from scratch with <code>synchronized</code>, <code>wait()</code> and <code>notifyAll()</code>: multiple concurrent readers, one exclusive writer, and writer preference so writers aren't starved. Prove it with transfers that keep <code>A + B = 1000</code> while readers check the invariant.</p> Solution SimpleReadWriteLock.javaCustomLockDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.rwlock.custom;

/** Challenge solution: a read-write lock with writer preference, built on wait/notifyAll. */
public class SimpleReadWriteLock {

    private int activeReaders;
    private boolean writerActive;
    private int waitingWriters;

    public synchronized void lockRead() throws InterruptedException {
        while (writerActive || waitingWriters &gt; 0) { // writer preference: don't starve writers
            wait();
        }
        activeReaders++;
    }

    public synchronized void unlockRead() {
        activeReaders--;
        if (activeReaders == 0) {
            notifyAll();
        }
    }

    public synchronized void lockWrite() throws InterruptedException {
        waitingWriters++;
        try {
            while (writerActive || activeReaders &gt; 0) {
                wait();
            }
        } finally {
            waitingWriters--;
        }
        writerActive = true;
    }

    public synchronized void unlockWrite() {
        writerActive = false;
        notifyAll();
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.rwlock.custom;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class CustomLockDemo {

    static int balanceA = 500;
    static int balanceB = 500; // transfers keep A + B = 1000

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleReadWriteLock lock = new SimpleReadWriteLock();
        AtomicInteger wrongTotals = new AtomicInteger();
        try (ExecutorService ex = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4)) {
            ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2_000; i++) {
                    lock.lockWrite();
                    try {
                        balanceA -= 10;
                        balanceB += 10;
                    } finally {
                        lock.unlockWrite();
                    }
                }
                return null;
            });
            for (int r = 0; r &lt; 3; r++) {
                ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 20_000; i++) {
                        lock.lockRead();
                        try {
                            if (balanceA + balanceB != 1_000) {
                                wrongTotals.incrementAndGet();
                            }
                        } finally {
                            lock.unlockRead();
                        }
                    }
                    return null;
                });
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Final A + B       : " + (balanceA + balanceB));
        System.out.println("Inconsistent reads: " + wrongTotals.get());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Final A + B       : 1000
Inconsistent reads: 0
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: counters for active readers, an active writer and waiting writers, all guarded by the object's monitor; readers wait if a writer is active or waiting (writer preference); conditions checked in <code>while</code> loops; <code>notifyAll</code> on release. Not reentrant and no fairness among writers; that's what <code>ReentrantReadWriteLock</code> adds.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Checkout froze for 3 s every 30 s; profiling showed threads waiting on a read lock.</li> <li>T: Remove the freeze without losing price consistency.</li> <li>A: Found remote calls inside the write lock; moved them outside, then replaced the lock with an immutable snapshot behind an <code>AtomicReference</code>.</li> <li>R: The periodic spikes disappeared; p99 dropped from 3.1 s to 140 ms.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a thread pool and why use it?</p> <p>A set of reusable worker threads that execute submitted tasks. It avoids the cost of creating threads per task and limits concurrency.</p> <p>Q2. What does <code>ExecutorService</code> provide?</p> <p><code>execute</code>, <code>submit</code> (returns a <code>Future</code>), <code>invokeAll</code>/<code>invokeAny</code>, and lifecycle methods <code>shutdown</code>, <code>shutdownNow</code>, <code>awaitTermination</code>.</p> <p>Q3. <code>execute()</code> vs <code>submit()</code>?</p> <p><code>execute(Runnable)</code> returns nothing (exceptions go to the thread's handler); <code>submit()</code> returns a <code>Future</code> that holds the result or exception.</p> <p>Q4. <code>shutdown()</code> vs <code>shutdownNow()</code>?</p> <p><code>shutdown()</code> stops accepting new tasks and lets queued ones finish; <code>shutdownNow()</code> interrupts running tasks and returns the queued ones.</p> <p>Q5. Types of pools in <code>Executors</code>?</p> <p><code>newFixedThreadPool</code>, <code>newCachedThreadPool</code>, <code>newSingleThreadExecutor</code>, <code>newScheduledThreadPool</code>, <code>newWorkStealingPool</code>, and (Java 21) <code>newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor</code>.</p> <p>Q1. Explain <code>corePoolSize</code>, <code>maximumPoolSize</code> and the queue.</p> <p>New tasks start core threads first; then they go to the queue; only when the queue is full are extra threads up to max created; beyond that the rejection policy applies. (So with an unbounded queue, max is never reached.)</p> <p>Q2. How do you size a pool?</p> <p>CPU-bound: about the number of cores. I/O-bound: cores × (1 + wait/compute), or use virtual threads. Then measure.</p> <p>Q3. Why avoid <code>Executors.newFixedThreadPool</code> in production?</p> <p>Its unbounded queue hides overload until OOM. Build a <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> with a bounded queue and an explicit policy.</p> <p>Q4. What is <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code> good for?</p> <p>Back-pressure without losing tasks: when saturated, the submitter runs the task itself, slowing the rate of submission.</p> <p>Q5. How do you handle exceptions in pool tasks?</p> <p>Catch and log inside tasks; check <code>Future.get()</code> (<code>ExecutionException</code>); set an <code>UncaughtExceptionHandler</code> via a <code>ThreadFactory</code> for <code>execute()</code>.</p> <p>Q1. "Our API's p99 jumped from 200 ms to 30 s but CPU is at 20%. Diagnose."</p> <p>Likely pool exhaustion: threads blocked on a slow dependency, requests waiting in the queue. Check thread dumps (many <code>WAITING</code> on sockets), pool metrics, downstream latency. Fix: timeouts, bulkheads per dependency, circuit breakers, virtual threads for I/O, and limits on concurrent downstream calls.</p> <p>Q2. "Design thread usage for a service that does CPU-heavy pricing and calls 4 downstream APIs per request."</p> <p>Virtual threads per request for I/O orchestration; a bounded CPU pool (cores) for pricing computation; semaphores/bulkheads per downstream; timeouts and fallbacks; metrics per pool.</p> <p>Q3. "Explain thread-pool-induced deadlock and how to avoid it."</p> <p>Tasks waiting on sub-tasks in the same saturated pool. Avoid with separate pools per stage, <code>ForkJoinPool</code> (work-stealing handles joins), async composition without blocking, or virtual threads.</p> <p>Q4. "When do you still need thread pools with virtual threads?"</p> <p>CPU-bound work (cap at cores), bulkheads (hard concurrency caps for isolation), integration with libraries that need executors, and scheduled tasks.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you shut down a service with in-flight work safely?"</p> <p>Stop accepting (readiness probe false, <code>shutdown()</code>), finish in-flight tasks with a deadline (<code>awaitTermination</code>), then <code>shutdownNow()</code>; make tasks idempotent and durable (outbox) so interrupted work can be retried.</p> <p>Problem: Build a minimal fixed-size thread pool from scratch: N worker threads, a bounded task queue (submission blocks when full), a failing task must not kill its worker, and <code>shutdownAndWait()</code> must finish all queued work before stopping.</p> Solution SimpleThreadPool.javaSimplePoolDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.threadpool.custom;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

/** Challenge solution: a minimal fixed thread pool, to understand what ThreadPoolExecutor does. */
public class SimpleThreadPool {

    private static final Runnable SHUTDOWN = () -&gt; { };

    private final BlockingQueue&lt;Runnable&gt; tasks;
    private final List&lt;Thread&gt; workers = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    private volatile boolean accepting = true;

    public SimpleThreadPool(int threads, int queueCapacity) {
        tasks = new LinkedBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(queueCapacity);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; threads; i++) {
            Thread t = new Thread(this::workLoop, "simple-pool-" + (i + 1));
            workers.add(t);
            t.start();
        }
    }

    private void workLoop() {
        try {
            while (true) {
                Runnable task = tasks.take();
                if (task == SHUTDOWN) {
                    return;
                }
                try {
                    task.run();
                } catch (RuntimeException e) { // a failing task must not kill the worker
                    System.out.println("task failed: " + e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    public void execute(Runnable task) throws InterruptedException {
        if (!accepting) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("pool is shut down");
        }
        tasks.put(task); // blocks when the queue is full (back-pressure)
    }

    public void shutdownAndWait() throws InterruptedException {
        accepting = false;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; workers.size(); i++) {
            tasks.put(SHUTDOWN); // queued after all real tasks, so pending work completes first
        }
        for (Thread t : workers) {
            t.join();
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.threadpool.custom;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class SimplePoolDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SimpleThreadPool pool = new SimpleThreadPool(3, 10);
        AtomicInteger done = new AtomicInteger();
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 50; i++) {
            int n = i;
            pool.execute(() -&gt; {
                if (n == 13) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException("task 13 hit a bad record");
                }
                done.incrementAndGet();
            });
        }
        pool.shutdownAndWait();
        System.out.println("Tasks completed: " + done.get() + " of 50 (one failed, workers survived)");
        try {
            pool.execute(() -&gt; { });
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("After shutdown: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>task failed: task 13 hit a bad record
Tasks completed: 49 of 50 (one failed, workers survived)
After shutdown: pool is shut down
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: workers loop on <code>take()</code>; a <code>try/catch</code> per task keeps workers alive; shutdown enqueues one sentinel per worker behind the real tasks (graceful), then <code>join()</code>s; <code>volatile accepting</code> stops new submissions. Follow-ups: futures/results, <code>shutdownNow</code> with interrupts, dynamic resizing (all of which <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> already does).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: An <code>@Async</code> email executor with an unbounded queue crashed the order service during a provider outage.</li> <li>T: Make async work safe under downstream failure.</li> <li>A: Replaced default executors with explicit bounded pools and CallerRunsPolicy, added client timeouts, moved emails to an outbox-backed worker, and added pool metrics and alerts.</li> <li>R: The next email-provider outage delayed emails by 20 minutes; no crash, no lost messages.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What are virtual threads?</p> <p>Lightweight threads managed by the JVM (Java 21). They're cheap to create and block, so you can run millions of concurrent tasks with simple blocking code.</p> <p>Q2. How do you create one?</p> <p><code>Thread.ofVirtual().start(task)</code>, <code>Thread.startVirtualThread(task)</code>, or <code>Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()</code>.</p> <p>Q3. Virtual vs. platform threads?</p> <p>Platform threads map 1:1 to OS threads (expensive, about 1 MB stack). Virtual threads are scheduled by the JVM on a few carrier threads and store their stacks on the heap.</p> <p>Q4. Do virtual threads make code run faster?</p> <p>Not faster per task. They increase throughput for I/O-bound workloads by letting many more tasks wait concurrently. CPU-bound work doesn't benefit.</p> <p>Q5. Should you pool virtual threads?</p> <p>No. Create one per task. Limit resources (connections, permits) instead.</p> <p>Q1. What is pinning?</p> <p>When a virtual thread can't unmount from its carrier while blocking: inside <code>synchronized</code> (before JDK 24) or during native calls. It ties up a carrier and can reduce throughput or even deadlock.</p> <p>Q2. How do you limit concurrency with virtual threads?</p> <p><code>Semaphore</code> permits, bounded connection pools, Resilience4j bulkheads and rate limiters, all around the scarce resource.</p> <p>Q3. How do you enable virtual threads in Spring Boot?</p> <p><code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code> (Boot 3.2+, Java 21+).</p> <p>Q4. What happens to <code>ThreadLocal</code> with virtual threads?</p> <p>It works, but each virtual thread has its own copy; with millions of threads, <code>ThreadLocal</code> caches multiply memory. Prefer <code>ScopedValue</code> or shared objects.</p> <p>Q5. How do you detect pinning?</p> <p>JFR event <code>jdk.VirtualThreadPinned</code>, or <code>-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full</code> on JDK 21–23.</p> <p>Q1. "Migrate a high-traffic Spring service from Tomcat's 200-thread pool to virtual threads. Plan and risks?"</p> <p>Upgrade JDK (24+ ideally), enable virtual threads, inventory implicit limits the pool provided, size DB/HTTP pools and add semaphores per downstream, scan for pinning and <code>ThreadLocal</code> usage, load test with production-like downstreams, roll out gradually with p99 and error-rate monitoring.</p> <p>Q2. "Virtual threads vs. WebFlux for a new service?"</p> <p>Virtual threads for request/response with blocking libraries (simpler code and debugging); WebFlux for streaming with back-pressure or an existing reactive ecosystem. Both need explicit downstream limits.</p> <p>Q3. "What is structured concurrency and why does it matter?"</p> <p><code>StructuredTaskScope</code> treats related subtasks as a unit: fork, join, cancel siblings on failure, and propagate errors, so no orphaned threads and clear lifetimes. It's the natural companion to virtual threads.</p> <p>Q4. "A service on JDK 21 with virtual threads hangs under load. What do you check?"</p> <p>Pinning (<code>synchronized</code> around blocking I/O in your code or libraries), exhausted resource pools (connections), deadlocks, carrier saturation by CPU-heavy tasks; collect a thread dump (<code>jcmd &lt;pid&gt; Thread.dump_to_file -format=json</code>) and JFR.</p> <p>Q5. "Do virtual threads change how you size HikariCP?"</p> <p>No. The database's capacity does. Size the pool to what the database can serve (often 10–50 per instance), use a short connection timeout, and let virtual threads wait cheaply for connections.</p> <p>Problem: A profile page needs the user, their recent orders and recommendations. Recommendations are optional and sometimes very slow. Write it with plain blocking code on virtual threads: run the three calls in parallel, give recommendations 200 ms, fall back to trending titles and cancel the slow call.</p> Solution ProfilePageDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.concurrency.virtualthreads.blocking;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

/** Challenge solution: an async page written as plain blocking code on virtual threads. */
public class ProfilePageDemo {

    static String user(String id) throws InterruptedException { Thread.sleep(40); return "Asha (Gold member)"; }

    static List&lt;String&gt; orders(String id) throws InterruptedException { Thread.sleep(60); return List.of("ORD-1", "ORD-2"); }

    static List&lt;String&gt; recommendations(String id) throws InterruptedException { Thread.sleep(2_000); return List.of("slow"); }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        try (ExecutorService io = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            Future&lt;String&gt; user = io.submit(() -&gt; user("U1"));
            Future&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; orders = io.submit(() -&gt; orders("U1"));
            Future&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; recs = io.submit(() -&gt; recommendations("U1"));

            List&lt;String&gt; recommended;
            try {
                recommended = recs.get(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS); // optional section: short timeout
            } catch (TimeoutException e) {
                recs.cancel(true); // interrupts the virtual thread doing the slow call
                recommended = List.of("Trending: Monsoon Express");
            }
            System.out.println("User            : " + user.get());          // plain blocking calls,
            System.out.println("Recent orders   : " + orders.get());        // normal stack traces,
            System.out.println("Recommendations : " + recommended);         // normal try/catch
            System.out.println("Slow call cancelled: " + recs.isCancelled());
        } catch (ExecutionException e) {
            System.out.println("Essential call failed: " + e.getCause());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>User            : Asha (Gold member)
Recent orders   : [ORD-1, ORD-2]
Recommendations : [Trending: Monsoon Express]
Slow call cancelled: true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>submit</code> + <code>Future.get(timeout)</code> replaces <code>CompletableFuture</code> chains; <code>cancel(true)</code> interrupts the virtual thread, so the slow call actually stops (unlike <code>completeOnTimeout</code>); exceptions surface as normal <code>ExecutionException</code>s with readable stack traces. With structured concurrency, the scope would also cancel siblings automatically on failure.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our order API needed 3× more throughput; it spent 80% of request time waiting on downstream calls.</li> <li>T: Scale without a reactive rewrite.</li> <li>A: Upgraded to Java 21, enabled virtual threads, replaced <code>synchronized</code> in hot I/O paths with <code>ReentrantLock</code>, added semaphores per downstream and right-sized HikariCP, and load-tested against production-sized dependencies.</li> <li>R: Throughput went up 4.2× on the same hardware with unchanged business code; p99 improved 35%; no database overload thanks to the explicit limits.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Abstract Factory pattern?</p> <p>A creational pattern that provides an interface for creating families of related objects without specifying their concrete classes. The client picks a concrete factory once and gets matching products from it.</p> <p>Q2. Difference between Factory Method and Abstract Factory?</p> <p>Factory Method creates one product and usually relies on inheritance (a subclass overrides the creation method). Abstract Factory creates a family of products and relies on composition (you pass a factory object in). Abstract Factory is often built from several factory methods.</p> <p>Q3. Give a real example.</p> <p>A UI toolkit with <code>WindowsFactory</code> and <code>MacFactory</code>, each creating a matching <code>Button</code> and <code>Checkbox</code>. In the JDK: <code>DocumentBuilderFactory</code>, and <code>java.sql.Connection</code> creating statements for one driver.</p> <p>Q4. What problem does it prevent?</p> <p>Mixing incompatible objects, for example a Mac button with a Windows checkbox, or German tax with Indian currency formatting. Since every product comes from one factory, they are consistent.</p> <p>Q5. What's the main drawback?</p> <p>Adding a new kind of product means changing the abstract factory interface and every concrete factory. It also adds many classes.</p> <p>Q1. How do you choose which concrete factory to use?</p> <p>Once, at the edge of the system: from configuration (Spring <code>@Profile</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code>), from a request attribute (tenant, country, device), or with a static factory method like <code>DeviceUiFactory.forDevice(type)</code>. Then pass it in through dependency injection.</p> <p>Q2. How would you test code that uses an Abstract Factory?</p> <p>Create an in-memory family (a test factory returning fakes) and inject it, as in <code>InvoiceArchiverTest</code>. For the real families, write contract tests: the same test class run against each factory to prove they behave the same way.</p> <p>Q3. How does Spring implement this pattern?</p> <p>A <code>@Configuration</code> class with several <code>@Bean</code> methods is a concrete factory for a family; <code>@Profile</code> / <code>@Conditional</code> picks which configuration is active. Auto-configured <code>DataSource</code> + <code>JdbcTemplate</code> + <code>TransactionManager</code> is a real-world family.</p> <p>Q4. Can you add a new product to the family without breaking all factories?</p> <p>Yes, temporarily, with a <code>default</code> method on the factory interface (a no-op or an exception), then implement it in each family and remove the default. That keeps the build green during a gradual rollout.</p> <p>Q5. Is it over-engineering for two databases (MySQL and Postgres)?</p> <p>Usually, yes. JDBC already abstracts the family (<code>Connection</code> creates matching statements), and JPA/Hibernate dialects handle the differences. Only add your own abstract factory where your code must create several matching objects per variant.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a multi-cloud storage and messaging layer for a SaaS product. What would you abstract and what would you not?"</p> <p>Abstract only the seams that matter (object storage, queues, secrets) as ports, with one adapter family per cloud created by an abstract factory. Don't abstract cloud-specific strengths you depend on (e.g., DynamoDB streams) unless portability is a hard requirement. Cover per-tenant selection, client lifecycle and pooling, contract tests per family against local emulators (LocalStack, Azurite), observability tagged by family, and the cost of the lowest-common-denominator problem.</p> <p>Q2. "Products in a family are owned by different teams and drift apart. How do you keep them consistent?"</p> <p>Contract tests shared by all families; an ArchUnit rule banning direct construction of products outside factories; versioned family modules released together; and a runtime self-check at start-up (each product reports its region/provider, and the factory asserts they match).</p> <p>Q3. "Add a new country to a global checkout in a week. Walk me through it."</p> <p>New <code>RegionFactory</code> implementation (tax, formatting, delivery, and in reality payment methods, address format, legal texts); register it in the region lookup; run the shared contract tests; dark-launch to internal users using a feature flag; and check legal rounding rules and tax-inclusive vs. exclusive pricing. No changes to checkout, cart or receipts, and that is the measurable win of the pattern.</p> <p>Q4. "When does Abstract Factory turn into an anti-pattern?"</p> <p>When there's only one family, when the interface keeps growing (a sign it should be split), when families are chosen per call (risking mixes), or when it duplicates what the DI container already does with profiles.</p> <p>Q5. "Relate Abstract Factory to Hexagonal / Clean Architecture."</p> <p>Product interfaces are ports owned by the domain; each concrete factory is an adapter family in the infrastructure layer; the composition root (Spring config) chooses the family. Dependencies point inwards: the domain compiles without any vendor SDK.</p> <p>Problem: Launch Japan on the grocery checkout from Example 2 without changing <code>CheckoutCalculator</code>. Rules: yen has no minor unit; consumption tax is 8% for food (round down); minimum order JPY 1,000; delivery is free from JPY 2,000, otherwise JPY 330; promise "same-day delivery"; format as <code>JPY 3,240</code>.</p> Solution: a new concrete factory JapanRegion.javaJapanDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.abstractfactory.region;

import java.util.Locale;

/** Challenge solution: a new family, and no change to CheckoutCalculator. */
public class JapanRegion implements RegionFactory {

    @Override
    public TaxPolicy taxPolicy() {
        return new TaxPolicy() {
            public String name() { return "Consumption tax 8%"; } // reduced rate for food
            public long taxOn(long yen) { return yen * 8 / 100; } // yen has no minor unit; round down
        };
    }

    @Override
    public PriceFormatter priceFormatter() {
        return yen -&gt; String.format(Locale.ROOT, "JPY %,d", yen);
    }

    @Override
    public DeliveryRules deliveryRules() {
        return new DeliveryRules() {
            public long minimumOrder() { return 1_000; }
            public long deliveryFee(long subtotal) { return subtotal &gt;= 2_000 ? 0 : 330; }
            public String promise() { return "same-day delivery"; }
        };
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.abstractfactory.region;

public class JapanDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CheckoutCalculator japan = new CheckoutCalculator(new JapanRegion());
        System.out.println("JP: " + japan.summary(3_000));
        System.out.println("JP: " + japan.summary(1_500));
        System.out.println("JP: " + japan.summary(800));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>JP: subtotal JPY 3,000 + Consumption tax 8% JPY 240 + delivery JPY 0 = JPY 3,240 (same-day delivery)
JP: subtotal JPY 1,500 + Consumption tax 8% JPY 120 + delivery JPY 330 = JPY 1,950 (same-day delivery)
JP: Minimum order is JPY 1,000
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: one new class, zero edits to the client, which is Open/Closed at the family level. The existing <code>CheckoutCalculatorTest</code> gets one more test that runs the Japan family through the unchanged calculator. Using <code>Locale.ROOT</code> keeps the output identical on every machine.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our ERP was hard-wired to AWS; a large pharma prospect required Azure.</li> <li>T: Support Azure for that tenant within a quarter, without forking the codebase.</li> <li>A: Defined storage, queue and secrets ports; wrapped AWS in an adapter family; built the Azure family; selected the family per tenant; ran one contract suite against LocalStack and Azurite.</li> <li>R: Closed the deal; a third family (on-prem MinIO + RabbitMQ) later took two weeks instead of a quarter.</li> </ul>

Variant When Fluent nested builder (Example 1) Default choice for classes with 4+ fields or several optional ones Step builder (Example 2) Required fields must be enforced at compile time; public APIs and SDKs GoF Director + Builder interface (Example 3) Same steps produce different outputs (documents, reports, UIs) <code>toBuilder()</code> (coding challenge) Create a modified copy of an immutable object Records with "withers" Few fields: <code>order.withSlot(EXPRESS)</code> returning a new record Lombok <code>@Builder</code> / <code>@Builder(toBuilder = true)</code> Lots of DTOs; less code, but generated, and weak at cross-field validation <p>Records and builders work well together. Keep the record as the product (immutable, <code>equals</code>, validation in the compact constructor) and add a small static nested builder only when there are many optional fields. Java has no named parameters, so the builder is how you get them.</p> <p>Generic self-typed builders for class hierarchies (Effective Java, Item 2):</p> <pre><code>abstract static class Builder&lt;T extends Builder&lt;T&gt;&gt; {
    abstract T self();
    public T topping(String t) { toppings.add(t); return self(); }
}
</code></pre> <p>This lets <code>NyPizza.Builder</code> chain parent methods without casts.</p>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Builder pattern and why use it?</p> <p>A creational pattern that builds a complex object step by step and returns it with <code>build()</code>. It solves the telescoping constructor problem, improves readability, and allows immutable objects.</p> <p>Q2. Write a Builder for a <code>User</code> with required <code>name</code> and optional <code>email</code>, <code>phone</code>.</p> <p>Static nested <code>Builder</code> class; <code>User.builder(name)</code> takes the required field; <code>email()</code>/<code>phone()</code> return <code>this</code>; <code>build()</code> calls a private <code>User(Builder)</code> constructor. All <code>User</code> fields <code>final</code>, no setters (see Example 1).</p> <p>Q3. What is the telescoping constructor problem?</p> <p>Many overloaded constructors with growing parameter lists (<code>User(name)</code>, <code>User(name, email)</code>, <code>User(name, email, phone)</code>...). They're hard to read, and arguments of the same type are easy to swap.</p> <p>Q4. Builder vs. setters (JavaBeans)?</p> <p>Setters leave the object mutable and possibly half-built; a builder returns a complete, validated, immutable object in one step.</p> <p>Q5. Name builders in the JDK.</p> <p><code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>Stream.builder()</code>, <code>HttpRequest.newBuilder()</code>, <code>Locale.Builder</code>, and Java 21's <code>Thread.ofVirtual()</code>.</p> <p>Q1. Where do you validate, in the builder methods or <code>build()</code>?</p> <p>Single-field checks can go in the methods (fail early), but cross-field rules belong in <code>build()</code> or in the product's constructor. Invariants belong in the product, so they hold however it's created.</p> <p>Q2. How do you enforce required fields?</p> <p>Pass them to <code>builder(...)</code> (Example 1) or use a step builder (Example 2) so the compiler enforces the order. Checking for <code>null</code> in <code>build()</code> works but only fails at runtime.</p> <p>Q3. What is <code>toBuilder()</code> and when is it useful?</p> <p>It returns a builder pre-filled from an existing immutable object, so you can make a modified copy (<code>base.toBuilder().timeout(10s).build()</code>). It's useful for configuration objects and test data.</p> <p>Q4. Builder vs. records?</p> <p>Records give immutability and <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> for free but have only a canonical constructor. For a few fields that's enough; for many optional fields, add a nested builder that constructs the record.</p> <p>Q5. What does the GoF Director do?</p> <p>It holds a construction recipe (the order of steps) and works with any builder that implements the builder interface, so the same recipe produces different representations (Example 3).</p> <p>Q1. "Design the configuration API for an HTTP client SDK used by 500 internal teams."</p> <p>An immutable <code>ClientConfig</code> built by a builder with safe defaults (timeouts, connection pool sizes, TLS), <code>toBuilder()</code> for overrides, and <code>build()</code> enforcing safety rules (retries only for idempotent requests, max timeout). Builders let you add options without breaking 500 callers. Also discuss documentation via method names, deprecating options, and exposing the effective config in logs and metrics.</p> <p>Q2. "Builders allocate an extra object. Is that a problem at 100k RPS?"</p> <p>Usually no: short-lived objects die young in the TLAB, and the JIT's escape analysis often removes them. Measure with JFR/JMH before optimizing. The real costs are defensive copies of big collections; avoid repeated <code>toBuilder()</code> in hot loops.</p> <p>Q3. "How do you keep a builder, a product and <code>toBuilder()</code> in sync as fields are added?"</p> <p>Code generation (Lombok, Immutables, AutoValue, records + a generated builder), or a round-trip test: build with all fields set, <code>toBuilder().build()</code>, assert equal. Reflection-based tests can also check that every product field has a builder setter.</p> <p>Q4. "Design a fluent query builder (like jOOQ or Criteria API). What are the hard parts?"</p> <p>Type safety (step interfaces so <code>WHERE</code> comes after <code>FROM</code>), immutability (each call returns a new builder, so partial queries can be shared and reused), SQL injection (bind parameters, never string concatenation), and dialect differences (render via a visitor per database). It's Builder + Composite + Visitor.</p> <p>Q5. "When would you not use a builder?"</p> <p>Small value objects (2–3 required fields), objects created only by frameworks (JPA entities, where builders fight the no-arg constructor), and very hot paths where profiling shows the builder matters.</p> <p>Problem (fintech SDK): Build an immutable <code>ApiRequest</code> with: required HTTPS URL; method GET by default, <code>post(body)</code> switches to POST; headers (keep insertion order, but the product must be immutable); timeout (default 2 s); retries (default 0). Add <code>toBuilder()</code>. Safety rule: a POST with retries &gt; 0 must have an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header.</p> Solution ApiRequest.javaApiRequestDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.builder.api;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

/** Challenge solution: immutable request with defaults, validation and toBuilder(). */
public record ApiRequest(String method, String url, Map&lt;String, String&gt; headers,
                         String body, Duration timeout, int maxRetries) {

    public ApiRequest {
        headers = Map.copyOf(headers);
    }

    public static Builder builder(String url) {
        return new Builder(url);
    }

    public Builder toBuilder() {
        Builder b = new Builder(url);
        b.method = method;
        b.headers.putAll(headers);
        b.body = body;
        b.timeout = timeout;
        b.maxRetries = maxRetries;
        return b;
    }

    public static final class Builder {
        private final String url;
        private String method = "GET";
        private final Map&lt;String, String&gt; headers = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();
        private String body;
        private Duration timeout = Duration.ofSeconds(2);
        private int maxRetries = 0;

        private Builder(String url) {
            this.url = url;
        }

        public Builder post(String body) { this.method = "POST"; this.body = body; return this; }
        public Builder header(String name, String value) { headers.put(name, value); return this; }
        public Builder timeout(Duration timeout) { this.timeout = timeout; return this; }
        public Builder retries(int maxRetries) { this.maxRetries = maxRetries; return this; }

        public ApiRequest build() {
            if (!url.startsWith("https://")) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Only HTTPS URLs are allowed: " + url);
            }
            if (method.equals("POST") &amp;&amp; maxRetries &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; !headers.containsKey("Idempotency-Key")) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Retried POSTs need an Idempotency-Key header");
            }
            return new ApiRequest(method, url, headers, body, timeout, maxRetries);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.builder.api;

import java.time.Duration;

public class ApiRequestDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApiRequest base = ApiRequest.builder("https://api.example.com/payments")
                .header("Authorization", "Bearer t0k3n")
                .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .build();
        System.out.println(base.method() + " " + base.url() + " timeout=" + base.timeout().toSeconds() + "s");

        ApiRequest charge = base.toBuilder()
                .post("{\"amount\":499}")
                .header("Idempotency-Key", "ORD-77")
                .retries(3)
                .build();
        System.out.println(charge.method() + " retries=" + charge.maxRetries() + " headers=" + charge.headers().keySet().stream().sorted().toList());
        System.out.println("Original unchanged? " + base.method().equals("GET"));

        try {
            base.toBuilder().post("{}").retries(2).build();
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Rejected: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>GET https://api.example.com/payments timeout=5s
POST retries=3 headers=[Authorization, Idempotency-Key]
Original unchanged? true
Rejected: Retried POSTs need an Idempotency-Key header
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>Map.copyOf</code> in the compact constructor makes headers immutable; <code>toBuilder()</code> copies every field so the original is untouched; the idempotency rule is cross-field so it lives in <code>build()</code>; and <code>LinkedHashMap</code> in the builder keeps headers in the order they were added.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our order service had 9 constructors and frequent bugs from swapped <code>String</code> arguments.</li> <li>T: Make order creation safe and readable without a big-bang rewrite.</li> <li>A: Introduced an immutable <code>Order</code> with a builder that validated cross-field rules, plus a test-data builder; migrated callers module by module and deprecated the old constructors.</li> <li>R: Argument-order bugs dropped to zero; tests became about 40% shorter; adding a "gift message" field touched 2 files instead of 31.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Factory pattern?</p> <p>A creational pattern that hides object-creation logic behind a method. The caller asks for an object by type and gets back an interface, without knowing the concrete class. It reduces coupling and follows the Open/Closed Principle.</p> <p>Q2. Difference between Simple Factory and Factory Method?</p> <p>Simple Factory is a single method (often static) with a <code>switch</code> that returns different classes. Factory Method (GoF) is an abstract method that subclasses override to decide the class. Simple Factory isn't one of the 23 GoF patterns, but it's the one used most often in practice.</p> <p>Q3. Give a JDK example.</p> <p><code>Calendar.getInstance()</code>, <code>NumberFormat.getInstance(locale)</code>, <code>List.of(...)</code>, and <code>Collection.iterator()</code>, which is the textbook GoF Factory Method because each collection subclass creates its own iterator.</p> <p>Q4. Why not just use <code>new</code>?</p> <p><code>new</code> ties the caller to one concrete class. With a factory the caller depends on an interface, so you can add or swap implementations, return cached objects, or inject fakes in tests without changing the caller.</p> <p>Q5. Write a factory for shapes (Circle, Square, Rectangle).</p> <p>Interface <code>Shape { double area(); }</code>, three implementations, and <code>ShapeFactory.create(ShapeType type)</code> using a <code>switch</code> expression on an enum with no <code>default</code>, as in Example 1.</p> <p>Q1. How do you avoid the giant <code>switch</code> in a factory?</p> <p>Use a registry: <code>Map&lt;Type, Supplier&lt;Product&gt;&gt;</code> filled at start-up (Example 3), or let Spring inject <code>Map&lt;String, Product&gt;</code>. New types are registered, not added to a switch. Alternatively, use <code>sealed</code> + an exhaustive <code>switch</code> expression so the compiler guards completeness.</p> <p>Q2. How does Factory Method relate to Template Method?</p> <p>They're often used together. A <code>final</code> template method defines the workflow and calls an abstract factory method for the object it needs (Example 2's <code>plan()</code> calls <code>createTransport()</code>).</p> <p>Q3. How would you unit test code that uses a factory?</p> <p>Inject the factory (or a <code>Supplier</code>) into the client, then pass a fake factory that returns stubs. Test the factory separately: every key maps to the expected type, and unknown keys fail fast.</p> <p>Q4. When is a factory over-engineering?</p> <p>When there's one implementation and no realistic second one, when creation is trivial, or when a DI container already gives you the instance. YAGNI.</p> <p>Q5. Should a factory return a new instance or a cached one?</p> <p>It depends on the product. Stateless and thread-safe products (most processors, strategies) can be cached and shared. Stateful ones must be new per call. A good factory hides this decision from the caller, which is one of its main benefits.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a notification system that supports email, SMS and push, and a new channel every quarter."</p> <p>Channel interface + registry-based factory; channels are plug-ins (Spring beans or <code>ServiceLoader</code>). Then go beyond creation: user preferences and fallbacks (push fails, so send SMS), rate limits per channel, templates per locale, async dispatch via a queue per channel so one slow provider doesn't block others, idempotency keys to avoid duplicate sends on retry, and delivery status callbacks. The factory is the small part; say so, and show how it isolates the rest.</p> <p>Q2. "Your factory creates an expensive client (HTTP connection pool) per call and p99 latency spikes. Fix it."</p> <p>The product is stateless and thread-safe, so cache it: make the registry hold instances (or memoized suppliers) instead of constructors. Be clear about lifecycle: who closes the pools on shutdown? In Spring, make them singleton beans with <code>@PreDestroy</code>. Measure before and after (allocation rate, connection churn, p99).</p> <p>Q3. "How would you roll out a new payment gateway to 1% of merchants safely?"</p> <p>Put the decision in the factory: a feature flag keyed by merchant picks the new or old implementation (see the deep dive). Add metrics per implementation, an automatic kill switch on error-rate SLO breach, and shadow traffic if possible. Callers don't change, which is the point of the pattern.</p> <p>Q4. "Factory Method vs. Dependency Injection: aren't they the same?"</p> <p>DI is a generalized, external factory: the container builds the object graph. Use DI for wiring at start-up (singletons, long-lived services). Use a factory when the choice depends on runtime data (the request's payment method, the tenant). In Spring they combine: inject the factory, and the factory picks a bean per request.</p> <p>Q5. "How do plug-in architectures (JDBC drivers, IDE plug-ins) find implementations?"</p> <p>Service Provider Interface + <code>ServiceLoader</code>: implementations declare themselves in <code>META-INF/services/&lt;interface&gt;</code> or <code>module-info</code> (<code>provides X with Y</code>), and the host discovers them at runtime. It's a registry factory filled by the classpath. Discuss class-loader isolation, versioning, and failing fast when a required provider is missing.</p> <p>Problem (ride-hailing): This code is repeated in three services. Refactor it so callers write <code>FareCalculator.of(type).fareInr(km)</code>, the compiler catches a missing ride type, and Sedan has a minimum fare of INR 150.</p> <pre><code>int fare;
if (type.equals("AUTO")) fare = 30 + 12 * km;
else if (type.equals("MINI")) fare = 50 + 14 * km;
else if (type.equals("SEDAN")) fare = 80 + 18 * km;
else fare = 0; // silent default!
</code></pre> Solution: factory method on a functional interface FareCalculator.javaRideDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.factorymethod.ride;

public interface FareCalculator {

    int fareInr(int distanceKm);

    /** Factory method on the interface itself, like {@code List.of(...)}. */
    static FareCalculator of(RideType type) {
        return switch (type) {
            case AUTO -&gt; km -&gt; 30 + 12 * km;
            case MINI -&gt; km -&gt; 50 + 14 * km;
            case SEDAN -&gt; km -&gt; Math.max(150, 80 + 18 * km); // minimum fare
        };
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.factorymethod.ride;

public class RideDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (RideType type : RideType.values()) {
            System.out.printf("%-5s  3 km = INR %3d | 10 km = INR %3d%n",
                    type, FareCalculator.of(type).fareInr(3), FareCalculator.of(type).fareInr(10));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>AUTO   3 km = INR  66 | 10 km = INR 150
MINI   3 km = INR  92 | 10 km = INR 190
SEDAN  3 km = INR 150 | 10 km = INR 260
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the enum replaces strings; the <code>switch</code> expression has no <code>default</code>, so adding <code>BIKE</code> without a fare is a compile error; each product is a lambda because <code>FareCalculator</code> has one abstract method; and the minimum-fare rule lives with its product, not in the callers.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Payment-method logic was copy-pasted across checkout, refunds and reconciliation; adding a wallet took 3 sprints and caused a refund bug.</li> <li>T: Make adding a payment method a one-team, one-PR change.</li> <li>A: Introduced a <code>PaymentProcessor</code> interface and a Spring map-injected factory, moved each method into its own module, and added an enum-driven completeness test.</li> <li>R: The next method (BNPL) shipped in 4 days with zero changes to checkout; the refund defect class disappeared.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Object Pool pattern?</p> <p>A creational pattern that keeps a set of reusable objects. Clients borrow an object and return it instead of creating and destroying one each time. It's used for expensive objects like database connections.</p> <p>Q2. Give real examples.</p> <p>JDBC connection pools (HikariCP), HTTP connection pools, thread pools (<code>ExecutorService</code>) and Redis connection pools.</p> <p>Q3. What happens when the pool is empty?</p> <p>Depending on configuration, the pool creates a new object (if below the maximum), makes the caller wait (with a timeout), or fails immediately.</p> <p>Q4. Why reset an object when it's returned?</p> <p>So the next borrower gets a clean state: no open transaction, no leftover data from the previous user.</p> <p>Q5. Should we pool every object?</p> <p>No. Only expensive or limited resources. For normal objects, the JVM's allocation and garbage collection are faster than a pool.</p> <p>Q1. How do you prevent leaks?</p> <p>Make release automatic: return an <code>AutoCloseable</code> lease for try-with-resources, or use execute-around (<code>pool.withSession(work)</code>). Add leak detection that logs where an object was borrowed if it isn't returned within N seconds.</p> <p>Q2. How would you implement a thread-safe pool?</p> <p>A <code>BlockingQueue</code> for idle objects plus a <code>Semaphore</code> or atomic counter for the maximum (see the coding challenge). Validate on borrow, reset on return, and always bound waits with a timeout.</p> <p>Q3. How do you size a DB connection pool?</p> <p>Start from Little's Law (throughput × hold time), add headroom, and check the database's total limit across all instances. Then load-test and watch acquire-time percentiles. It's usually much smaller than the thread count.</p> <p>Q4. With virtual threads, do we still need pools?</p> <p>You stop pooling threads, but you still pool connections and other limited resources. Virtual threads make it easy to create far more concurrent work than a database can handle, so resource limits matter more.</p> <p>Q5. What does HikariCP's <code>maxLifetime</code> do and why?</p> <p>It retires connections after a set time, so they're replaced before the database or network infrastructure kills them silently. It should be shorter than any infrastructure idle timeout.</p> <p>Q1. "Our service times out under load and the DB is at 20% CPU. Diagnose."</p> <p>Likely pool starvation: check pending acquires, active connections and acquire-time p99. Common causes: slow calls while holding a connection, long transactions, leaks, or a pool that's too small for the hold time. Fix hold time first (acquire late, release early), add bulkheads, set aggressive acquire timeouts, and only then resize.</p> <p>Q2. "100 pods each with a pool of 25 connect to one Postgres. What breaks, and what do you do?"</p> <p>2,500 connections, and each Postgres connection is a process with real memory cost, so the database thrashes. Options: smaller per-pod pools, a connection proxy (PgBouncer in transaction mode, RDS Proxy), read replicas for reads, or moving work to async queues. Size pools as a fleet-wide budget.</p> <p>Q3. "Design a pool for an external API that allows 10 concurrent sessions per API key, shared by 30 instances."</p> <p>A local pool can't enforce a global limit. Use a distributed semaphore (Redis with leases and TTLs), or route all calls through a dedicated gateway service that owns the 10 sessions. Discuss lease expiry when an instance dies, fairness, and back-pressure to callers.</p> <p>Q4. "When is pooling a bad idea?"</p> <p>For cheap objects (allocation is faster), objects that are hard to reset (risk of leaking data between users or tenants), and when pooling hides a design problem like long hold times. Also for objects that can't be safely shared between threads sequentially.</p> <p>Q5. "Pool vs. Flyweight: what's the difference?"</p> <p>A pool gives exclusive, temporary use of a mutable object. A flyweight is an immutable, shared object used by many at once. Connections are pooled; <code>Integer.valueOf</code> cached instances are flyweights.</p> <p>Problem: Write a generic, thread-safe <code>ObjectPool&lt;T&gt;</code> with a maximum size, a factory (<code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code>), a validator (<code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code>, discard invalid objects), a reset step (<code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code>) and a borrow timeout. Creation failures must not "leak" capacity.</p> Solution ObjectPool.javaGenericPoolDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.objectpool.generic;

import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;
import java.util.function.Consumer;
import java.util.function.Predicate;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

/** Challenge solution: a small, thread-safe, generic object pool. */
public final class ObjectPool&lt;T&gt; {

    private final BlockingQueue&lt;T&gt; idle = new LinkedBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;();
    private final Semaphore permits;
    private final Supplier&lt;T&gt; factory;
    private final Predicate&lt;T&gt; validator;
    private final Consumer&lt;T&gt; reset;

    public ObjectPool(int maxSize, Supplier&lt;T&gt; factory, Predicate&lt;T&gt; validator, Consumer&lt;T&gt; reset) {
        this.permits = new Semaphore(maxSize, true); // fair: first come, first served
        this.factory = factory;
        this.validator = validator;
        this.reset = reset;
    }

    public T borrow(long timeout, TimeUnit unit) throws InterruptedException, TimeoutException {
        if (!permits.tryAcquire(timeout, unit)) {
            throw new TimeoutException("Pool exhausted");
        }
        try {
            T obj;
            while ((obj = idle.poll()) != null) {
                if (validator.test(obj)) {
                    return obj;
                }
            }
            return factory.get();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            permits.release(); // creation failed: give the permit back
            throw e;
        }
    }

    public void giveBack(T obj) {
        try {
            reset.accept(obj);
            idle.offer(obj);
        } finally {
            permits.release();
        }
    }

    public int idleCount() {
        return idle.size();
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.objectpool.generic;

import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

public class GenericPoolDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ObjectPool&lt;StringBuilder&gt; buffers = new ObjectPool&lt;&gt;(2,
                () -&gt; new StringBuilder(8_192), sb -&gt; sb.capacity() &gt;= 8_192, sb -&gt; sb.setLength(0));

        StringBuilder first = buffers.borrow(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        first.append("invoice #1");
        buffers.giveBack(first);

        StringBuilder again = buffers.borrow(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        System.out.println("Reused same buffer? " + (again == first) + ", cleared? " + again.isEmpty());

        StringBuilder second = buffers.borrow(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        try {
            buffers.borrow(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        } catch (TimeoutException e) {
            System.out.println("Third borrow: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        buffers.giveBack(again);
        buffers.giveBack(second);
        System.out.println("Idle buffers: " + buffers.idleCount());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Reused same buffer? true, cleared? true
Third borrow: Pool exhausted
Idle buffers: 2
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the semaphore caps borrowed objects (in use, not just created), so the maximum holds even while objects are being created; invalid idle objects are dropped and replaced; if the factory throws, the permit is released so capacity isn't lost; and <code>giveBack</code> releases the permit in <code>finally</code>, so a failing reset can't shrink the pool. A follow-up would add leak detection and metrics.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Checkout timed out site-wide on our biggest sale day while the database looked idle.</li> <li>T: Restore service, then make sure one slow dependency couldn't take down the site again.</li> <li>A: Identified connection-pool starvation from a fraud call made inside a transaction; moved the call out, cut the acquire timeout to 2 s, split pools per workload, and added pending-acquire alerts.</li> <li>R: Checkout p99 fell from 30 s to 180 ms; the next year's sale ran at 3× the traffic without pool alerts.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Prototype pattern?</p> <p>A creational pattern that creates new objects by copying an existing object (the prototype) instead of creating them from scratch. It's useful when creation is expensive or the object is pre-configured.</p> <p>Q2. What is the difference between a shallow copy and a deep copy?</p> <p>A shallow copy copies field values, so referenced objects are shared. A deep copy also copies the referenced mutable objects, so the two are fully independent.</p> <p>Q3. How does <code>clone()</code> work in Java?</p> <p>The class implements the <code>Cloneable</code> marker interface and overrides <code>clone()</code>, calling <code>super.clone()</code>, which makes a field-by-field shallow copy without calling constructors. Without <code>Cloneable</code> it throws <code>CloneNotSupportedException</code>.</p> <p>Q4. Why do many experts avoid <code>clone()</code>?</p> <p>It's shallow by default, skips constructors, doesn't work well with <code>final</code> fields, returns <code>Object</code>, and uses a checked exception. Copy constructors or static copy factories are clearer.</p> <p>Q5. Give a real-world example.</p> <p>Document or report templates, product variants, game characters spawned from a prefab, and Spring's <code>prototype</code> scope (a new bean per request).</p> <p>Q1. How do you deep-copy an object that contains a <code>List&lt;Order&gt;</code>?</p> <p>Create a new list and copy each <code>Order</code> (<code>list.stream().map(Order::copy).toList()</code>). If <code>Order</code> is immutable, copying the list alone is enough.</p> <p>Q2. What is a prototype registry?</p> <p>A map of named, pre-configured prototypes; clients ask for a key and receive a copy (Example 1). It allows new "types" to be added as data at runtime.</p> <p>Q3. Spring <code>prototype</code> scope vs. the Prototype pattern?</p> <p>Spring's <code>prototype</code> scope means a new instance per request, built by the container, not copied. The pattern is about cloning a configured instance. They share a name, not a mechanism. Also note: Spring doesn't manage the destruction of prototype beans.</p> <p>Q4. How would you test a copy method?</p> <p>Copy, then mutate every mutable field of the copy and assert the original is unchanged; also assert the copy's values are equal right after copying. Add the test when you add a field.</p> <p>Q5. How do you handle circular references in a deep copy?</p> <p>Keep an <code>IdentityHashMap&lt;original, copy&gt;</code> of objects already copied. Before copying a node, check the map and reuse the existing copy. This also preserves shared references inside the graph.</p> <p>Q1. "Design 'create project from template' for a SaaS with 50k tenants."</p> <p>Templates as versioned data (JSON) in a store; instances created by deserializing and resetting identity (new IDs, owners, timestamps); copy is deep by construction because it comes from serialized data. Record the template ID and version in each instance. Discuss large templates (async creation with progress), permissions (who can publish templates), and migrations when templates change.</p> <p>Q2. "A shared config object is read by 200 threads and occasionally updated. How do you avoid locks and torn reads?"</p> <p>Immutable snapshot + atomic swap: build a new config (copying and modifying the old one), then <code>AtomicReference.set()</code>. Readers call <code>get()</code> and see a consistent snapshot without locking. This is copy-on-write.</p> <p>Q3. "Our deep-copy code is 15% of CPU in a hot path. What do you do?"</p> <p>Profile to confirm; then avoid copying: make objects immutable and share them, use persistent data structures (structural sharing), copy lazily (copy-on-write when a mutation actually happens), or reduce what's copied (only the mutable part).</p> <p>Q4. "Is <code>clone()</code> broken? Defend your answer."</p> <p>Its design is flawed: <code>Cloneable</code> has no <code>clone</code> method, so you can't call <code>clone()</code> through the interface; the copy bypasses constructors; it's shallow by default; and it fights <code>final</code>. It works for arrays and simple classes, but copy constructors are safer and clearer. Bloch: "a fine approach to object copying is to provide a copy constructor or copy factory."</p> <p>Q5. "Where does Prototype show up in distributed systems?"</p> <p>Immutable snapshots shipped between services; container images and VM templates (clone a golden image); database template schemas (Postgres <code>CREATE DATABASE ... TEMPLATE</code>); and infrastructure as code modules instantiated per environment.</p> <p>Problem (grocery "Reorder"): A customer taps "Reorder" on last week's cart. Implement <code>Cart.copy()</code> so that changing quantities or adding lines in the new cart never changes last week's cart. Lines are mutable (the quantity can change).</p> Solution Cart.javaReorderDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.prototype.cart;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/** Challenge solution: "Reorder" copies a past cart; the copy must be independent. */
public class Cart {

    public static final class Line {
        private final String sku;
        private int quantity;

        public Line(String sku, int quantity) {
            this.sku = sku;
            this.quantity = quantity;
        }

        Line copy() {
            return new Line(sku, quantity);
        }

        public void setQuantity(int quantity) {
            this.quantity = quantity;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return sku + "x" + quantity;
        }
    }

    private final String customerId;
    private final List&lt;Line&gt; lines = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    public Cart(String customerId) {
        this.customerId = customerId;
    }

    public Cart add(String sku, int quantity) {
        lines.add(new Line(sku, quantity));
        return this;
    }

    public Line line(int index) {
        return lines.get(index);
    }

    public Cart copy() {
        Cart copy = new Cart(customerId);
        lines.forEach(l -&gt; copy.lines.add(l.copy()));
        return copy;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return customerId + " " + lines;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.prototype.cart;

public class ReorderDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cart lastWeek = new Cart("CUST-42").add("MILK-1L", 2).add("EGGS-12", 1);
        Cart reorder = lastWeek.copy();
        reorder.line(0).setQuantity(4);
        reorder.add("BREAD-WW", 1);

        System.out.println("Last week: " + lastWeek);
        System.out.println("Reorder:   " + reorder);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Last week: CUST-42 [MILK-1Lx2, EGGS-12x1]
Reorder:   CUST-42 [MILK-1Lx4, EGGS-12x1, BREAD-WWx1]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>Line</code> is mutable, so each line is copied (<code>Line.copy()</code>), not just the list. The customer ID is a <code>String</code>, which is immutable, so it can be shared. A follow-up improvement is to make <code>Line</code> an immutable record, so <code>setQuantity</code> returns a new line and copying becomes trivial.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Tenants were seeing other tenants' notification settings after workspace creation.</li> <li>T: Find the cause fast and make it impossible to recur.</li> <li>A: Traced it to a one-level copy of nested mutable maps; replaced settings with immutable records, created workspaces from versioned JSON templates, and added a mutate-every-field copy test to CI.</li> <li>R: Closed the incident in a day; no recurrence in two years; the template versioning later enabled a "what changed" feature for admins.</li> </ul>

Variant Lazy? Thread-safe? Reflection-safe? Serialization-safe? Use when Eager <code>static final</code> field ❌ ✅ ❌ (guard needed) ❌ (<code>readResolve</code> needed) Cheap object, always used <code>synchronized getInstance()</code> ✅ ✅ ❌ ❌ Never in hot paths (locks every call) Double-checked locking ✅ ✅ (with <code>volatile</code>) ❌ (guard needed) ❌ You need lazy init and constructor arguments from runtime Holder idiom ✅ ✅ ❌ (guard needed) ❌ Default choice for a lazy class-based Singleton Enum ✅ (on class init) ✅ ✅ ✅ Default choice when you don't need to extend a class <p>Making a class-based Singleton serialization-safe:</p> <pre><code>public final class AppClock implements Serializable {
    private static final AppClock INSTANCE = new AppClock();
    private AppClock() {}
    public static AppClock getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }

    @Serial
    private Object readResolve() {   // deserialization returns the existing instance
        return INSTANCE;
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Modern Java notes:</p> <ul> <li>Virtual threads (Java 21): avoid holding a <code>synchronized</code> lock around slow I/O inside a Singleton. Before JDK 24 (JEP 491) that pins the carrier thread. Use <code>ReentrantLock</code> or the holder/enum forms, which need no lock at all.</li> <li>Records can't be Singletons by themselves (their canonical constructor is public), but a record is great as the immutable state that a Singleton holds.</li> <li>Multiton: one instance per key, for example per tenant. <code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent</code> makes it trivial. See the coding challenge below.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Typical of Cognizant, TCS, Infosys, Accenture, Wipro and Capgemini technical rounds.</p> <p>Q1. What is the Singleton pattern?</p> <p>A creational pattern that ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it. It's done with a private constructor, a static field that holds the instance, and a static <code>getInstance()</code> method.</p> <p>Q2. Write a thread-safe Singleton.</p> <p>The cleanest answer is the holder idiom (Example 2) or an enum (Example 1). If they want lazy initialization with explicit locking, write double-checked locking with <code>volatile</code> (Example 3) and explain both null checks.</p> <p>Q3. Difference between eager and lazy initialization?</p> <p>Eager creates the instance when the class loads (simple and thread-safe, but it costs memory even if never used). Lazy creates it on first <code>getInstance()</code> call (saves resources, but needs thread-safety care).</p> <p>Q4. Why is the constructor private?</p> <p>To stop other classes from calling <code>new</code>. That's how the class controls the number of instances.</p> <p>Q5. Can we break a Singleton? How?</p> <p>Yes, in three ways: reflection (<code>setAccessible(true)</code> on the constructor), serialization (deserializing creates a new object), and cloning (if it implements <code>Cloneable</code>). Defences: a constructor guard, <code>readResolve()</code>, overriding <code>clone()</code> to throw. Or use an enum, which is immune to all three.</p> <p>Q1. Why is <code>volatile</code> needed in double-checked locking?</p> <p>Because <code>instance = new X()</code> isn't atomic. Reordering can publish the reference before the constructor finishes. <code>volatile</code> sets up a happens-before relationship so other threads never see a half-built object.</p> <p>Q2. Why does the holder idiom work without synchronization?</p> <p>The JVM initializes a class lazily and exactly once, under an internal lock (JLS §12.4.2). <code>Holder</code> is only initialized when <code>getInstance()</code> first touches it, so you get lazy and thread-safe for free.</p> <p>Q3. How do you unit test code that depends on a Singleton?</p> <p>Don't call <code>getInstance()</code> deep inside business logic. Extract an interface, make the singleton implement it, and inject it through the constructor. In tests, pass a fake. In Spring, use <code>@MockitoBean</code>.</p> <p>Q4. Spring singleton vs. GoF Singleton?</p> <p>A GoF Singleton is one per class loader, enforced by the class itself. A Spring singleton is one per <code>ApplicationContext</code>, enforced by the container. The class has a public constructor and can be created again in tests.</p> <p>Q5. Is Singleton an anti-pattern?</p> <p>It's overused, not wrong. It becomes an anti-pattern when it's used as global mutable state or a service locator. It's fine for real one-per-process invariants, especially when it's injected, not looked up.</p> <p>Open-ended, with follow-up chains typical of Google, Microsoft, Amazon and SAP.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a unique ID generator. Start with one JVM, then make it distributed."</p> <p>Single JVM: Singleton + <code>AtomicLong</code> (Example 2). Distributed: a single counter is a bottleneck, so use Snowflake-style IDs (timestamp + node ID + per-node sequence), which are k-sortable with no coordination. Discuss clock skew (refuse to issue IDs or wait when the clock moves backwards), node-ID assignment (from ZooKeeper or the pod ordinal), and the throughput limit (4,096 IDs/ms per node with a 12-bit sequence).</p> <p>Follow-up: "What if IDs must be gap-free?" → you need a central sequence (DB or Raft log). Gap-free and highly available pull against each other, so be explicit about the trade-off.</p> <p>Q2. "Your Singleton cache becomes a lock-contention hotspot at 50k RPS. What do you do?"</p> <p>Profile first (JFR, async-profiler). Then remove the global lock: switch to <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> or Caffeine, stripe the state, or keep immutable snapshots that are swapped atomically (<code>AtomicReference</code>, copy-on-write). With virtual threads, make sure no <code>synchronized</code> block wraps I/O.</p> <p>Q3. "How many instances of a Singleton can exist in one JVM?"</p> <p>One per class loader. In app servers (Tomcat, SAP NetWeaver), OSGi or plugin systems, the same class loaded by two loaders gives two singletons. Real JVM-wide singletons need a class loaded by a shared parent loader.</p> <p>Q4. "Run a nightly job exactly once across 12 pods."</p> <p>A JVM Singleton doesn't help. Use distributed locking/leader election: ShedLock on a DB table, a Kubernetes Lease, or move it to a Kubernetes CronJob. Make the job idempotent anyway, because locks can expire mid-run.</p> <p>Q5. "When would you reject a Singleton in code review?"</p> <p>When it holds request/user-scoped data, when it's a service locator that hides dependencies, when tests have to reset static state with reflection, or when a DI container already gives singleton scope for free.</p> <p>Problem (SAP-style multi-tenant SaaS): Your ERP serves many tenants (countries). Each tenant needs exactly one <code>TenantConfig</code> instance, created lazily on first use, and creation must be thread-safe. <code>TenantConfig.forTenant("IN")</code> must always return the same object, and <code>forTenant("DE")</code> a different one. India uses <code>INR</code>, Germany <code>EUR</code>, everyone else <code>USD</code>.</p> Solution: Multiton with <code>computeIfAbsent</code> TenantConfig.javaTenantDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.singleton.tenant;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

/**
 * Coding-challenge solution: one config object per tenant (a "Multiton").
 */
public final class TenantConfig {

    private static final Map&lt;String, TenantConfig&gt; INSTANCES = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();

    private final String tenantId;
    private final String currency;

    private TenantConfig(String tenantId) {
        this.tenantId = tenantId;
        this.currency = switch (tenantId) {
            case "IN" -&gt; "INR";
            case "DE" -&gt; "EUR";
            default -&gt; "USD";
        };
        System.out.println("[TenantConfig] Loaded tenant " + tenantId);
    }

    public static TenantConfig forTenant(String tenantId) {
        return INSTANCES.computeIfAbsent(tenantId, TenantConfig::new); // atomic per key
    }

    public String tenantId() {
        return tenantId;
    }

    public String currency() {
        return currency;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.creational.singleton.tenant;

public class TenantDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TenantConfig india = TenantConfig.forTenant("IN");
        TenantConfig germany = TenantConfig.forTenant("DE");
        TenantConfig indiaAgain = TenantConfig.forTenant("IN");

        System.out.println(india.tenantId() + " -&gt; " + india.currency());
        System.out.println(germany.tenantId() + " -&gt; " + germany.currency());
        System.out.println("Same IN instance? " + (india == indiaAgain));
        System.out.println("IN and DE differ? " + (india != germany));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>[TenantConfig] Loaded tenant IN
[TenantConfig] Loaded tenant DE
IN -&gt; INR
DE -&gt; EUR
Same IN instance? true
IN and DE differ? true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>computeIfAbsent</code> on <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> is atomic per key, so two threads asking for <code>"IN"</code> at once still build only one. Keep the mapping function short and non-recursive, because it runs while that map bin is locked.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Billing service produced duplicate invoice numbers after we scaled to 3 pods.</li> <li>T: Restore uniqueness without a long outage before month-end close.</li> <li>A: Found the per-JVM Singleton counter, replaced it with a DB sequence using pooled allocation, added a unique constraint and a reconciliation script.</li> <li>R: Zero duplicates since. I also added "per-process assumptions" to our design-review checklist.</li> </ul>

S: Single ResponsibilityO: Open/ClosedL: Liskov SubstitutionI: Interface SegregationD: Dependency Inversion <p>A class should have one reason to change.</p> <pre><code>// ❌ Mixes pricing, persistence and notification
class OrderService {
    void placeOrder(Order o) { /* price + save to DB + send SMS */ }
}

// ✅ One job each
class PriceCalculator { Money total(Order o) { /* ... */ return null; } }
class OrderRepository { void save(Order o) { /* ... */ } }
class OrderNotifier   { void orderPlaced(Order o) { /* ... */ } }
</code></pre> <p>Open for extension, closed for modification. Add new behavior by adding code, not by editing working code.</p> <pre><code>// ✅ New discount = new class. No if/else edits.
interface DiscountPolicy { int apply(int amount); }
record FlatDiscount(int off) implements DiscountPolicy {
    public int apply(int amount) { return Math.max(0, amount - off); }
}
record PercentDiscount(int pct) implements DiscountPolicy {
    public int apply(int amount) { return amount * (100 - pct) / 100; }
}
</code></pre> <p>Patterns that deliver OCP: Strategy, Decorator, Observer, Template Method, Factory.</p> <p>A subtype must be usable anywhere its parent is expected without surprising the caller.</p> <pre><code>// ❌ Classic violation: Square changes Rectangle's contract
class Rectangle { void setWidth(int w) {} void setHeight(int h) {} }
class Square extends Rectangle { /* setWidth also changes height! */ }
</code></pre> <p>Rule of thumb: if a subclass throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code> for a parent method, you probably broke LSP.</p> <p>Many small, focused interfaces beat one big one.</p> <pre><code>// ❌ A printer that must "fax"
interface Machine { void print(); void scan(); void fax(); }

// ✅
interface Printer { void print(); }
interface Scanner { void scan(); }
</code></pre> <p>High-level modules depend on abstractions, not on concrete classes.</p> <pre><code>// ✅ CheckoutService doesn't know (or care) which gateway is used
interface PaymentGateway { boolean charge(String orderId, int amountInr); }

class CheckoutService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    CheckoutService(PaymentGateway gateway) { this.gateway = gateway; } // injected
}
</code></pre> <p>This is what Spring's dependency injection gives you for free.</p>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is an anti-corruption layer?</p> <p>A translation layer between our system and an external/legacy system, so the external model doesn't leak into our code.</p> <p>Q2. Which design patterns does an ACL use?</p> <p>Adapter (protocol/interface), Facade (simplify), and translators/mappers (data and codes).</p> <p>Q3. Give an example.</p> <p>Converting a legacy ERP's <code>MAT_NO</code>, <code>KGM</code> and <code>"1.299,00"</code> into our <code>Product(sku, unit=KILOGRAM, pricePaise=129900)</code>.</p> <p>Q4. Why not just use the legacy data directly?</p> <p>Its conventions would spread everywhere, making our code hard to change and the legacy system hard to replace.</p> <p>Q5. Where is it used in microservices migrations?</p> <p>Between new services and the old monolith/ERP during a strangler-fig migration.</p> <p>Q1. How do you handle unknown values from the foreign system?</p> <p>Decide per boundary: reject (with logging and alerts) for risky data, or map to an explicit <code>UNKNOWN</code> for non-critical data. Never guess silently.</p> <p>Q2. ACL vs. adapter?</p> <p>An adapter converts one interface; an ACL protects a whole domain model, combining adapters, facades and translators at a system boundary.</p> <p>Q3. How do you test an ACL?</p> <p>Unit tests with recorded real responses (including edge cases), contract tests against the foreign system, and property tests for conversions.</p> <p>Q4. Should the ACL be its own service?</p> <p>If many services need the same legacy system (or access is complex/licensed), yes; otherwise, a module in each consuming service keeps autonomy.</p> <p>Q5. Is MapStruct enough for an ACL?</p> <p>It helps with field mapping, but code tables, unit conversions, validation and protocol handling need explicit code.</p> <p>Q1. "When do you use an ACL vs. conforming to the upstream model?"</p> <p>Conform when the upstream model is good and stable (and you're happy to depend on it); use an ACL when it's legacy, poorly fitting or likely to be replaced, or controlled by another organization.</p> <p>Q2. "Design integration between new microservices and SAP ECC during a migration."</p> <p>ACL/integration services exposing domain APIs and events; reads via BAPIs/OData or CDC; writes via official interfaces (IDocs/BAPIs); translation of codes and units; caching; monitoring of rejected records; ACL implementations swapped as capabilities move to S/4HANA or new services.</p> <p>Q3. "How do you keep many ACLs consistent across teams?"</p> <p>Shared libraries for common mappings (units, currencies), a canonical event schema for integration events, and contract tests, while keeping each team's domain model their own.</p> <p>Q4. "What security concerns belong at the ACL?"</p> <p>Input validation, safe parsers (XXE, deserialization), size limits, authentication to the foreign system, and data minimization (don't import fields you don't need, especially PII).</p> <p>Q5. "An ACL adds 80 ms per call to a slow legacy API. Options?"</p> <p>Cache reference data, batch requests, replicate via CDC into a local read model, or call asynchronously where the use case allows.</p> <p>Problem: The ACL works in both directions. The warehouse system only accepts orders as fixed-width records: 10-char order number, 20-char upper-case name without accents, a 5-digit zero-padded quantity, and a <code>yyMMdd</code> date. Translate our <code>OrderLine</code>s and guarantee every record is exactly 41 characters.</p> Solution LegacyOrderExporter.javaLegacyExportDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.acl.outbound;

import java.text.Normalizer;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Coding challenge: the ACL works in both directions. The warehouse system only accepts orders as
 * fixed-width records: 10-char order number, 20-char customer name (upper case, no accents),
 * quantity right-aligned in 5 digits, and a YYMMDD date. Translate our Order into that format.
 */
public final class LegacyOrderExporter {

    public record OrderLine(String orderId, String customerName, int quantity, LocalDate date) {
    }

    public static String toRecord(OrderLine o) {
        String id = o.orderId().replace("-", "");
        String name = Normalizer.normalize(o.customerName(), Normalizer.Form.NFD)
                .replaceAll("\\p{M}", "").toUpperCase();
        if (o.quantity() &lt; 0 || o.quantity() &gt; 99_999) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("quantity does not fit in 5 digits: " + o.quantity());
        }
        return pad(id, 10) + pad(name, 20) + String.format("%05d", o.quantity())
                + o.date().format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyMMdd"));
    }

    private static String pad(String s, int width) {
        return s.length() &gt;= width ? s.substring(0, width) : s + " ".repeat(width - s.length());
    }

    public static List&lt;String&gt; export(List&lt;OrderLine&gt; orders) {
        return orders.stream().map(LegacyOrderExporter::toRecord).toList();
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.acl.outbound;

import com.handbook.microservices.acl.outbound.LegacyOrderExporter.OrderLine;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;

public class LegacyExportDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: outbound translation to a fixed-width legacy format ===");
        List&lt;String&gt; records = LegacyOrderExporter.export(List.of(
                new OrderLine("ORD-1", "Asha Rao", 2, LocalDate.of(2026, 10, 13)),
                new OrderLine("ORD-12345678901", "Zoë Fernandes-D'Souza", 12, LocalDate.of(2026, 10, 13))));
        System.out.println("|" + "0123456789".repeat(5).substring(0, 41) + "|");
        records.forEach(r -&gt; System.out.println("|" + r + "|"));
        System.out.println("Every record is 41 characters: " + records.stream().allMatch(r -&gt; r.length() == 41));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: outbound translation to a fixed-width legacy format ===
|01234567890123456789012345678901234567890|
|ORD1      ASHA RAO            00002261013|
|ORD1234567ZOE FERNANDES-D'SOUZ00012261013|
Every record is 41 characters: true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>Normalizer</code> + removing combining marks turns "Zoë" into "ZOE"; truncate and pad to exact widths; validate values that can't fit (quantity over 99,999) instead of corrupting the record; test the exact length.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Three new services each parsed our ERP's material data differently; prices disagreed between the app and invoices.</li> <li>T: One correct, reusable interpretation of ERP data.</li> <li>A: Built an ACL integration service exposing <code>Product</code> and <code>Price</code> APIs and clean events, with explicit unit/currency conversions, a 4,000-record real-data test corpus, and alerts on unmapped codes.</li> <li>R: Price mismatches dropped to zero; when we moved to a new ERP a year later, only the ACL changed.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is an API gateway?</p> <p>A single entry point for clients that routes requests to services and handles cross-cutting concerns like authentication, rate limiting and logging.</p> <p>Q2. Why use one?</p> <p>Clients don't need to know internal services; security and rate limiting are implemented once; internal services aren't exposed.</p> <p>Q3. Name some gateways.</p> <p>Spring Cloud Gateway, Netflix Zuul (older), Kong, NGINX, Envoy, AWS API Gateway, Apigee.</p> <p>Q4. Gateway vs. load balancer?</p> <p>A load balancer spreads traffic across instances of one service; a gateway routes to different services by path or headers and adds edge features.</p> <p>Q5. Is the gateway a single point of failure?</p> <p>Potentially. Run multiple instances across zones behind a load balancer, keep them stateless and autoscale.</p> <p>Q1. Where should authentication and authorization happen?</p> <p>Authentication (and coarse scopes) at the gateway; fine-grained authorization in the services that own the data.</p> <p>Q2. How does Spring Cloud Gateway route to services?</p> <p>Routes with predicates (path, host, header) and filters (StripPrefix, rate limiter, retry, circuit breaker), with <code>lb://service-name</code> URIs resolved through service discovery.</p> <p>Q3. How do you rate limit at the gateway?</p> <p>Per key or user, with a token bucket (often Redis-backed for many instances), returning <code>429</code> and <code>Retry-After</code>.</p> <p>Q4. What should never go in a gateway?</p> <p>Business logic (pricing, validation of orders); it creates a hidden shared monolith.</p> <p>Q5. Gateway vs. BFF?</p> <p>A gateway is generic edge infrastructure; a BFF is a backend tailored to one client type (mobile, web) that aggregates and shapes data.</p> <p>Q1. "Design the edge layer for an app with 50M users and 200 services."</p> <p>Global DNS/anycast → CDN/WAF → regional load balancers → stateless gateway fleet (Envoy) with JWT validation, Redis-backed rate limits, routing via discovery, timeouts and circuit breakers → BFFs per client → services. GitOps-managed routes with per-team ownership, canary config rollout, full tracing.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you safely propagate user identity to internal services?"</p> <p>Strip incoming identity headers; issue a short-lived internal signed token (or JWT pass-through) and use mTLS between hops; services validate tokens rather than trusting plain headers.</p> <p>Q3. "How would you implement canary releases at the gateway?"</p> <p>Weighted routes or sticky hashing by user ID (coding challenge), header overrides for testers, automated analysis of error rate and latency, and automatic rollback.</p> <p>Q4. "Gateway latency budget is 5 ms at p99. What do you watch?"</p> <p>Avoid synchronous calls to external stores per request (cache JWKS, local token bucket approximations), connection pooling, efficient filters, and capacity headroom. Measure per filter.</p> <p>Q5. "Multi-tenant SaaS: what's tenant routing at the gateway responsible for?"</p> <p>Resolving the tenant (host, token claim), routing to the right shard or region (data residency), enforcing tenant-level quotas, maintenance controls, and passing tenant context. Isolation must still be enforced in services and databases.</p> <p>Problem: Route about 10% of users to <code>checkout v2</code>. The same user must always get the same version (sticky), and QA engineers must be able to force v2 with an <code>X-Canary: true</code> header.</p> Solution CanaryRouter.javaCanaryDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.gateway.canary;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.Http.Request;
import com.handbook.microservices.support.Http.Response;

import java.util.function.Function;

/** Challenge solution: send a stable 10% of users (plus anyone with X-Canary: true) to v2. */
public class CanaryRouter {

    private final Function&lt;Request, Response&gt; stable;
    private final Function&lt;Request, Response&gt; canary;
    private final int canaryPercent;

    public CanaryRouter(Function&lt;Request, Response&gt; stable, Function&lt;Request, Response&gt; canary, int canaryPercent) {
        this.stable = stable;
        this.canary = canary;
        this.canaryPercent = canaryPercent;
    }

    public Response handle(Request req) {
        boolean forced = "true".equals(req.header("X-Canary"));
        String user = req.header("X-User-Id");
        boolean bucket = user != null &amp;&amp; Math.floorMod(user.hashCode(), 100) &lt; canaryPercent; // sticky per user
        return (forced || bucket ? canary : stable).apply(req);
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.gateway.canary;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.Http.Request;
import com.handbook.microservices.support.Http.Response;

import java.util.Map;

public class CanaryDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CanaryRouter router = new CanaryRouter(r -&gt; Response.of(200, "checkout v1"), r -&gt; Response.of(200, "checkout v2"), 10);
        int v2 = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1_000; i++) {
            if (router.handle(Request.get("/checkout", Map.of("X-User-Id", "user-" + i))).body().endsWith("v2")) {
                v2++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Users routed to v2: " + v2 + " of 1000 (target ~10%)");
        System.out.println("user-7 twice: " + router.handle(Request.get("/checkout", Map.of("X-User-Id", "user-7"))).body()
                + ", " + router.handle(Request.get("/checkout", Map.of("X-User-Id", "user-7"))).body() + " (sticky)");
        System.out.println("QA with header: " + router.handle(Request.get("/checkout", Map.of("X-User-Id", "qa", "X-Canary", "true"))).body());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Users routed to v2: 94 of 1000 (target ~10%)
user-7 twice: checkout v1, checkout v1 (sticky)
QA with header: checkout v2
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: hash the user ID (not random per request) for stickiness; <code>floorMod</code> avoids negative buckets; a header override for testing. In production, use a stable hash (Murmur3) with a salt per experiment, so the same users aren't always the canary group.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Each of our 14 services validated JWTs differently, and two had no rate limiting.</li> <li>T: Consistent edge security without slowing teams down.</li> <li>A: Introduced a gateway with centralized JWT validation, per-client rate limits, request IDs and header stripping; route config moved to GitOps with per-team ownership.</li> <li>R: Removed about 2,000 lines of duplicated auth code; a later credential-stuffing attack was absorbed by rate limits without service impact.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a BFF?</p> <p>A backend built for one specific frontend (mobile, web) that aggregates data from microservices and returns exactly what that UI needs.</p> <p>Q2. Why not one API for all clients?</p> <p>Different clients need different data and payload sizes; a shared API gets bloated and every change needs every client team's agreement.</p> <p>Q3. Who should own a BFF?</p> <p>The team that owns the client it serves.</p> <p>Q4. BFF vs. API gateway?</p> <p>The gateway handles generic concerns (auth, rate limits, routing) for everyone; a BFF shapes and aggregates data for one client type. They're often used together.</p> <p>Q5. Should a BFF contain business logic?</p> <p>No. Only aggregation, formatting and client-specific adaptation.</p> <p>Q1. How do you keep BFF latency low?</p> <p>Call services in parallel (<code>CompletableFuture</code>, virtual threads), set per-call timeouts, cache stable data, and skip optional calls when they're slow.</p> <p>Q2. One downstream service fails. What does the BFF return?</p> <p>For critical data, an error; for optional data, a partial response with a fallback and a <code>degraded</code> flag so the UI can hide that section.</p> <p>Q3. How does GraphQL relate to BFF?</p> <p>GraphQL lets clients choose fields, so a GraphQL server can act as a BFF; resolvers fan out to services.</p> <p>Q4. How do you avoid code duplication across BFFs?</p> <p>Shared client libraries, and pushing genuinely shared aggregation down into a service. Accept some duplication as the price of independence.</p> <p>Q5. How do you test a BFF?</p> <p>Unit tests for shaping logic, consumer-driven contract tests (Pact) against downstream services, and screen-level tests from the client.</p> <p>Q1. "Design the API layer for a streaming service with phones, TVs, consoles and the web."</p> <p>Gateway → per-experience BFFs (or a GraphQL federation layer) → domain services. Device capability detection (image sizes, codecs), parallel fan-out with budgets, personalisation as an optional section with cached fallbacks, CDN for artwork, server-driven layouts.</p> <p>Q2. "BFFs keep growing business logic. How do you stop it?"</p> <p>Architecture rules (ArchUnit) forbidding domain logic packages, code review guidelines, moving any duplicated rule into the owning service, and making the BFF's purpose explicit in its README and ownership.</p> <p>Q3. "How does a BFF help with versioning external APIs?"</p> <p>The BFF owns the external contract and maps it to internal models, so v1 and v2 can coexist while internal services change freely (Example 3).</p> <p>Q4. "What's the latency budget math for a BFF that calls 4 services?"</p> <p>In parallel, roughly max(service latencies) + BFF overhead; the p99 of the max of N calls is worse than each service's p99. Use hedged requests or timeouts for tail latency, and make non-critical calls optional.</p> <p>Q5. "When would you NOT use BFFs?"</p> <p>One client type, a small team, or clients with nearly identical needs. A single well-designed API (or GraphQL) is simpler.</p> <p>Problem: Build a product page BFF that calls product, price and reviews in parallel with a 200 ms deadline each. Reviews are optional (empty list fallback), price may be missing, and the page fails only if the product itself is unavailable. Report degraded sections.</p> Solution ProductPageBff.javaProductPageDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.bff.resilient;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.Json;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

/**
 * Coding challenge: fan out to three services in parallel, give each a deadline,
 * and degrade gracefully instead of failing the whole page.
 */
public final class ProductPageBff {

    private final ExecutorService pool;
    private final Supplier&lt;Object&gt; product;
    private final Supplier&lt;Object&gt; price;
    private final Supplier&lt;Object&gt; reviews;
    private final long timeoutMillis;

    public ProductPageBff(ExecutorService pool, Supplier&lt;Object&gt; product, Supplier&lt;Object&gt; price,
                          Supplier&lt;Object&gt; reviews, long timeoutMillis) {
        this.pool = pool;
        this.product = product;
        this.price = price;
        this.reviews = reviews;
        this.timeoutMillis = timeoutMillis;
    }

    public Map&lt;String, Object&gt; page() {
        List&lt;String&gt; degraded = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        var p = call("product", product, null, degraded);
        var pr = call("price", price, null, degraded);
        var r = call("reviews", reviews, List.of(), degraded); // optional section: empty fallback
        CompletableFuture.allOf(p, pr, r).join();
        if (p.join() == null) {
            return Json.obj("error", "PRODUCT_UNAVAILABLE"); // the page is useless without the product
        }
        return Json.obj("product", p.join(), "price", pr.join(), "reviews", r.join(),
                "degraded", List.copyOf(degraded));
    }

    private CompletableFuture&lt;Object&gt; call(String name, Supplier&lt;Object&gt; s, Object fallback, List&lt;String&gt; degraded) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(s, pool)
                .orTimeout(timeoutMillis, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)
                .exceptionally(e -&gt; {
                    synchronized (degraded) {
                        degraded.add(name);
                    }
                    return fallback;
                });
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.bff.resilient;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.Json;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class ProductPageDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var neverReleased = new CountDownLatch(1);
        try (var pool = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            System.out.println("=== Challenge: parallel fan-out with graceful degradation ===");

            var healthy = new ProductPageBff(pool, () -&gt; "Alphonso mangoes 1 kg", () -&gt; "₹399",
                    () -&gt; List.of("Sweet!", "Fresh"), 200);
            System.out.println("All services up      -&gt; " + Json.of(healthy.page()));

            var slowReviews = new ProductPageBff(pool, () -&gt; "Alphonso mangoes 1 kg", () -&gt; "₹399", () -&gt; {
                await(neverReleased); // reviews service hangs
                return List.of();
            }, 200);
            System.out.println("Reviews hang         -&gt; " + Json.of(slowReviews.page()));

            var priceDown = new ProductPageBff(pool, () -&gt; "Alphonso mangoes 1 kg",
                    () -&gt; { throw new IllegalStateException("pricing 503"); }, () -&gt; List.of("Sweet!"), 200);
            System.out.println("Pricing returns 503  -&gt; " + Json.of(priceDown.page()));

            var productDown = new ProductPageBff(pool, () -&gt; { throw new IllegalStateException("503"); },
                    () -&gt; "₹399", () -&gt; List.of(), 200);
            System.out.println("Catalog returns 503  -&gt; " + Json.of(productDown.page()));
            neverReleased.countDown();
        }
    }

    private static void await(CountDownLatch latch) {
        try {
            latch.await();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: parallel fan-out with graceful degradation ===
All services up      -&gt; {"product":"Alphonso mangoes 1 kg","price":"₹399","reviews":["Sweet!","Fresh"],"degraded":[]}
Reviews hang         -&gt; {"product":"Alphonso mangoes 1 kg","price":"₹399","reviews":[],"degraded":["reviews"]}
Pricing returns 503  -&gt; {"product":"Alphonso mangoes 1 kg","price":null,"reviews":["Sweet!"],"degraded":["price"]}
Catalog returns 503  -&gt; {"error":"PRODUCT_UNAVAILABLE"}
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: calls start at the same time, so latency is roughly the slowest one (capped by the timeout); <code>orTimeout</code> makes the future fail but doesn't stop the underlying work, so also set timeouts on the HTTP client itself; classify sections as critical vs. optional.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our Android app made 7 API calls to render the home screen; on slow networks it took 6 s.</li> <li>T: Cut home-screen time without changing the domain services.</li> <li>A: Built a mobile BFF owned by the Android/iOS team: one call, parallel fan-out, optional sections with fallbacks, 320 px images.</li> <li>R: Home screen p75 went from 6.1 s to 1.8 s; the mobile team shipped home-screen changes weekly without backend tickets.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a circuit breaker?</p> <p>A wrapper around remote calls that stops calling a failing service for a while (fails fast or uses a fallback), then tests whether it has recovered.</p> <p>Q2. Name its three states.</p> <p>CLOSED (normal), OPEN (fail fast), HALF_OPEN (a few trial calls).</p> <p>Q3. Which library do you use in Spring Boot?</p> <p>Resilience4j with <code>@CircuitBreaker(name = "...", fallbackMethod = "...")</code>. Hystrix is legacy.</p> <p>Q4. What is a fallback method?</p> <p>An alternative response when the call fails or the circuit is open: cached data, a default, or a friendly error. It has the same parameters plus a <code>Throwable</code>.</p> <p>Q5. Why is it important in microservices?</p> <p>It prevents cascading failures: one slow service can't exhaust threads in all its callers.</p> <p>Q1. Count-based vs. time-based sliding window?</p> <p>Count-based: the last N calls. Time-based: calls in the last N seconds. Time-based behaves better under very variable traffic.</p> <p>Q2. Why <code>minimumNumberOfCalls</code>?</p> <p>So 1 failure out of 1 call (100%) after a restart doesn't open the circuit.</p> <p>Q3. How do slow calls trip a breaker?</p> <p>Calls longer than <code>slowCallDurationThreshold</code> are counted as slow; if the slow-call rate exceeds its threshold, the circuit opens even without errors.</p> <p>Q4. Retry + circuit breaker: which wraps which?</p> <p>By default in Resilience4j, Retry wraps the CircuitBreaker: each attempt is recorded, and once OPEN, retries fail fast. Don't retry <code>CallNotPermittedException</code>.</p> <p>Q5. What should not count as a failure?</p> <p>Business/validation errors (4xx), like "insufficient funds". Only infrastructure failures should count.</p> <p>Q1. "Design resilience for checkout calling 5 dependencies."</p> <p>Classify dependencies (critical: inventory, payment; optional: recommendations, loyalty), a timeout + breaker + bulkhead per dependency, fallbacks per business rules (queue payment, skip loyalty), a deadline for the whole request, and metrics/alerts per breaker.</p> <p>Q2. "100 instances each have their own breaker. Problems?"</p> <p>Each sees only its own traffic, so they open at different times and low-traffic instances may never reach the minimum. Usually acceptable; alternatives are mesh-level outlier detection or shared state (rarely worth it).</p> <p>Q3. "How do you pick thresholds?"</p> <p>From SLOs and real traffic: window large enough for statistical meaning, wait duration near typical recovery time, slow-call threshold from the dependency's p99. Validate with chaos experiments and tune from metrics.</p> <p>Q4. "When is a fallback dangerous?"</p> <p>When stale or default data causes wrong decisions: account balances, stock levels for overselling, prices. Then fail clearly instead.</p> <p>Q5. "Circuit breaker vs. outlier detection in a service mesh?"</p> <p>App breakers protect per dependency/operation with business-aware fallbacks; mesh outlier detection ejects individual bad hosts from load balancing. They complement each other.</p> <p>Problem: Implement a minimal, thread-safe circuit breaker in about 40 lines: it opens after N consecutive failures, stays open for T ms, then lets one trial call through.</p> Solution MiniCircuitBreaker.javaMiniBreakerDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.circuitbreaker.mini;

import java.util.function.LongSupplier;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

/** Coding challenge: a minimal, thread-safe breaker that opens after N consecutive failures. */
public final class MiniCircuitBreaker {

    private final int failureThreshold;
    private final long openMillis;
    private final LongSupplier nowMillis;
    private int consecutiveFailures;
    private long openedAt = -1;

    public MiniCircuitBreaker(int failureThreshold, long openMillis, LongSupplier nowMillis) {
        this.failureThreshold = failureThreshold;
        this.openMillis = openMillis;
        this.nowMillis = nowMillis;
    }

    public synchronized &lt;T&gt; T call(Supplier&lt;T&gt; action, Supplier&lt;T&gt; fallback) {
        boolean open = openedAt &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; nowMillis.getAsLong() - openedAt &lt; openMillis;
        if (open) {
            return fallback.get();
        }
        // CLOSED, or the open period is over (HALF_OPEN): let this call through as a trial.
        try {
            T result = action.get();
            consecutiveFailures = 0;
            openedAt = -1;
            return result;
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            if (++consecutiveFailures &gt;= failureThreshold || openedAt &gt;= 0) {
                openedAt = nowMillis.getAsLong(); // trip, or re-trip after a failed trial
            }
            return fallback.get();
        }
    }

    public synchronized String state() {
        if (openedAt &lt; 0) {
            return "CLOSED";
        }
        return nowMillis.getAsLong() - openedAt &lt; openMillis ? "OPEN" : "HALF_OPEN";
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.circuitbreaker.mini;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class MiniBreakerDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AtomicLong now = new AtomicLong(0);
        var breaker = new MiniCircuitBreaker(3, 5_000, now::get);
        String[] results = {"ok", "fail", "fail", "fail", "ok", "ok"};

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: minimal circuit breaker (3 consecutive failures, 5 s open) ===");
        for (String r : results) {
            String out = breaker.call(() -&gt; {
                if (r.equals("fail")) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException();
                }
                return "real";
            }, () -&gt; "fallback");
            System.out.printf("t=%4dms service=%-4s -&gt; %-8s state=%s%n", now.get(), r, out, breaker.state());
            now.addAndGet(1_000);
        }
        now.set(9_000);
        String out = breaker.call(() -&gt; "real", () -&gt; "fallback");
        System.out.printf("t=%4dms service=ok   -&gt; %-8s state=%s%n", now.get(), out, breaker.state());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: minimal circuit breaker (3 consecutive failures, 5 s open) ===
