🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is externalized configuration?</p> <p>Keeping environment-specific settings outside the application build, so the same artifact runs anywhere with different config.</p> <p>Q2. What is Spring Cloud Config Server?</p> <p>A server that serves configuration (often from a Git repo) to Spring applications, per application name and profile.</p> <p>Q3. What are Spring profiles?</p> <p>Named sets of configuration (<code>dev</code>, <code>prod</code>) activated with <code>spring.profiles.active</code>, overriding defaults.</p> <p>Q4. Where should passwords be stored?</p> <p>In a secret store (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Kubernetes Secrets via external secret managers), not in Git or the JAR.</p> <p>Q5. How do you change config without restarting?</p> <p>Spring Cloud's <code>@RefreshScope</code> beans with <code>/actuator/refresh</code> (or Spring Cloud Bus to refresh all instances).</p> <p>Q1. Explain property precedence in Spring Boot.</p> <p>Command-line args &gt; environment variables &gt; profile-specific files &gt; application files &gt; defaults (simplified). With a config server, imported sources slot into this order.</p> <p>Q2. <code>@Value</code> vs. <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code>?</p> <p><code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> binds a typed group of properties (validated, refactor-friendly, IDE metadata); <code>@Value</code> injects single values.</p> <p>Q3. What happens if the config server is down at startup?</p> <p>Depends on <code>fail-fast</code>: either startup fails (with retries), or it continues with local defaults. Choose deliberately and run the server HA.</p> <p>Q4. How does Spring Cloud Bus help?</p> <p>It broadcasts a refresh event (via Kafka/RabbitMQ) to all instances, so one call refreshes the whole fleet.</p> <p>Q5. ConfigMaps vs. config server?</p> <p>ConfigMaps are Kubernetes-native and simple; a config server adds Git versioning, profiles and merging across apps, and works outside Kubernetes.</p> <p>Q1. "Config changes cause more outages than code at our company. What do you do?"</p> <p>Treat config as code: PRs, schema validation, policy checks, staged rollout with automatic rollback on error-rate increase, ownership per file, and fewer global settings.</p> <p>Q2. "Explain dynamic secrets."</p> <p>The secret store creates short-lived credentials per consumer on demand (e.g. a DB user with a 1-hour lease) and revokes them on expiry; a leak is short-lived and attributable.</p> <p>Q3. "How do services authenticate to Vault without a stored secret?"</p> <p>Workload identity: Kubernetes service account tokens, cloud IAM roles; Vault verifies them and issues a token.</p> <p>Q4. "Feature flags vs. config: where's the line?"</p> <p>Flags are evaluated per request/user with targeting and are short-lived (release toggles) or operational (kill switches); config is per deployment. Use a flag service for user targeting and experiments.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you test configuration?"</p> <p>Bind and validate config for every environment in CI (load each profile and start the context with <code>@SpringBootTest</code> or a binder test), lint YAML, and diff effective config between environments.</p> <p>Problem: Bind raw string properties into a typed <code>CheckoutConfig</code> record. Validate ranges and formats, and fail at startup with all problems listed at once, so engineers don't fix them one restart at a time.</p> Solution CheckoutConfig.javaConfigValidationDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.config.validation;

import java.net.URI;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;

/**
 * Coding challenge: bind raw string properties into a typed, validated record and FAIL FAST at
 * startup, reporting every problem at once, instead of failing on the first checkout at 2 a.m.
 */
public record CheckoutConfig(int maxItems, Duration paymentTimeout, URI paymentUrl) {

    public static final class InvalidConfigException extends RuntimeException {
        InvalidConfigException(List&lt;String&gt; errors) {
            super("invalid configuration:\n  - " + String.join("\n  - ", errors));
        }
    }

    public static CheckoutConfig bind(Map&lt;String, String&gt; p) {
        List&lt;String&gt; errors = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        Integer maxItems = parse(p, "checkout.max-items", Integer::valueOf, errors);
        Duration timeout = parse(p, "checkout.payment-timeout", Duration::parse, errors);
        URI url = parse(p, "checkout.payment-url", URI::create, errors);

        if (maxItems != null &amp;&amp; (maxItems &lt; 1 || maxItems &gt; 500)) {
            errors.add("checkout.max-items must be 1..500 (was " + maxItems + ")");
        }
        if (timeout != null &amp;&amp; (timeout.isZero() || timeout.compareTo(Duration.ofSeconds(10)) &gt; 0)) {
            errors.add("checkout.payment-timeout must be &gt; 0 and &lt;= PT10S (was " + timeout + ")");
        }
        if (url != null &amp;&amp; !"https".equals(url.getScheme())) {
            errors.add("checkout.payment-url must use https (was " + url + ")");
        }
        if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
            throw new InvalidConfigException(errors);
        }
        return new CheckoutConfig(maxItems, timeout, url);
    }

    private static &lt;T&gt; T parse(Map&lt;String, String&gt; p, String key, Function&lt;String, T&gt; parser,
                               List&lt;String&gt; errors) {
        String raw = p.get(key);
        if (raw == null) {
            errors.add(key + " is missing");
            return null;
        }
        try {
            return parser.apply(raw.trim());
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            errors.add(key + " is not valid: '" + raw + "'");
            return null;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.config.validation;

import java.util.Map;

public class ConfigValidationDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: typed, fail-fast configuration ===");
        System.out.println("valid  -&gt; " + CheckoutConfig.bind(Map.of(
                "checkout.max-items", "100",
                "checkout.payment-timeout", "PT2S",
                "checkout.payment-url", "https://payments.internal")));
        try {
            CheckoutConfig.bind(Map.of(
                    "checkout.max-items", "1000",
                    "checkout.payment-timeout", "2 seconds",
                    "checkout.payment-url", "http://payments.internal"));
        } catch (CheckoutConfig.InvalidConfigException e) {
            System.out.println("broken -&gt; application failed to start: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: typed, fail-fast configuration ===
valid  -&gt; CheckoutConfig[maxItems=100, paymentTimeout=PT2S, paymentUrl=https://payments.internal]
broken -&gt; application failed to start: invalid configuration:
  - checkout.payment-timeout is not valid: '2 seconds'
  - checkout.max-items must be 1..500 (was 1000)
  - checkout.payment-url must use https (was http://payments.internal)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: collect errors instead of throwing on the first; skip range checks when parsing already failed (no noise); typed values (<code>Duration</code>, <code>URI</code>) remove unit confusion; Spring Boot gives you this with <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> + <code>@Validated</code>.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A production database password was found in a public fork of an internal repository.</li> <li>T: Rotate credentials and make sure secrets could never live in Git again.</li> <li>A: Moved all secrets to Vault with Kubernetes auth and dynamic DB credentials, replaced values with references, added secret scanning to CI, and purged history.</li> <li>R: Zero secrets in repos (enforced by CI); credential rotation went from a quarterly manual task to automatic hourly leases.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is CQRS?</p> <p>Separating the model that changes data (commands) from the model that reads data (queries).</p> <p>Q2. What's the difference between a command and a query?</p> <p>A command changes state and returns little or nothing; a query returns data and changes nothing.</p> <p>Q3. Does CQRS require two databases?</p> <p>No. It can be separate classes on one database; separate read stores are an option for scale.</p> <p>Q4. What is eventual consistency?</p> <p>After a write, read models are updated shortly afterwards, so reads may briefly return old data.</p> <p>Q5. Is CQRS the same as event sourcing?</p> <p>No. They're often used together, but CQRS works with a normal database on the write side.</p> <p>Q1. How are read models kept up to date?</p> <p>A projector consumes events (from Kafka, via the outbox or CDC) and updates the read store idempotently.</p> <p>Q2. How do you handle a user who doesn't see their own change?</p> <p>Read-your-writes techniques: return the new data from the command, poll until the read model's version catches up, optimistic UI, or read from the write side briefly.</p> <p>Q3. How do you add a new read model later?</p> <p>Create a new projector and replay historical events (event store, compacted topic, or a bootstrap from the write DB), then switch reads to it.</p> <p>Q4. Where are business rules validated?</p> <p>Only on the write side, against authoritative state.</p> <p>Q5. When is CQRS a bad idea?</p> <p>Simple CRUD domains, small teams, or when users can't tolerate any staleness and there's no read/write asymmetry.</p> <p>Q1. "Design the product listing for an e-commerce site with 50M products and 100k QPS reads."</p> <p>Write side per domain (catalog, pricing, inventory) → outbox → Kafka → projector into a search cluster with denormalized documents; CDN/edge caching for hot listings; projection lag SLO; checkout re-validates against the write side.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you rebuild a projection without downtime?"</p> <p>Blue/green: build <code>v2</code> from the start of the log while <code>v1</code> serves; when <code>v2</code> catches up to live, switch the query side; then retire <code>v1</code>.</p> <p>Q3. "Projections need data from multiple aggregates. Ordering issues?"</p> <p>Events for different aggregates may arrive out of order across partitions. Design projections to tolerate it (upserts with versions, buffering, or reprocessing on missing references).</p> <p>Q4. "How does SAP S/4HANA's approach relate to CQRS?"</p> <p>S/4HANA moved many aggregates and indexes into in-memory columnar tables and computes analytics on the fly, which reduces the need for separate read models. CQRS remains useful across service boundaries.</p> <p>Q5. "What metrics prove CQRS is working?"</p> <p>Read p99 latency, write throughput unaffected by read load, projection lag distribution, rebuild duration, and error/dead-letter rates in projectors.</p> <p>Problem: Months after launch, product asks for a "top sellers" screen. Build a new projection by replaying stored <code>OrderPlaced</code> events, then keep it live. It must ignore redelivered events.</p> Solution TopSellersProjection.javaRebuildDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.cqrs.rebuild;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Coding challenge: product asks for a new "top sellers this week" screen months after launch.
 * Because the read models are derived data, we build a new projection and replay the stored
 * event history into it. No migration of the write model is needed.
 */
public final class TopSellersProjection {

    public record OrderPlaced(long sequence, String sku, int qty) {
    }

    private final Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; unitsBySku = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    private long lastSequence; // checkpoint: where to resume after a restart

    public void apply(OrderPlaced e) {
        if (e.sequence() &lt;= lastSequence) {
            return; // already applied (replays and redeliveries are safe)
        }
        unitsBySku.merge(e.sku(), e.qty(), Integer::sum);
        lastSequence = e.sequence();
    }

    public List&lt;String&gt; top(int n) {
        return unitsBySku.entrySet().stream()
                .sorted(Map.Entry.&lt;String, Integer&gt;comparingByValue().reversed().thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
                .limit(n)
                .map(e -&gt; e.getKey() + "=" + e.getValue())
                .toList();
    }

    public long checkpoint() {
        return lastSequence;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.cqrs.rebuild;

import com.handbook.microservices.cqrs.rebuild.TopSellersProjection.OrderPlaced;

import java.util.List;

public class RebuildDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;OrderPlaced&gt; history = List.of(
                new OrderPlaced(1, "MANGO-1KG", 2), new OrderPlaced(2, "RICE-5KG", 1),
                new OrderPlaced(3, "MANGO-1KG", 3), new OrderPlaced(4, "MILK-1L", 6),
                new OrderPlaced(5, "RICE-5KG", 1), new OrderPlaced(6, "MILK-1L", 2));

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: add a new read model by replaying history ===");
        var topSellers = new TopSellersProjection();
        history.forEach(topSellers::apply);
        System.out.println("Rebuilt from " + history.size() + " stored events -&gt; top 2: " + topSellers.top(2)
                + ", checkpoint=" + topSellers.checkpoint());

        System.out.println("Live events continue; event 6 is redelivered by the broker:");
        topSellers.apply(new OrderPlaced(6, "MILK-1L", 2));
        topSellers.apply(new OrderPlaced(7, "MANGO-1KG", 4));
        System.out.println("top 2: " + topSellers.top(2) + ", checkpoint=" + topSellers.checkpoint());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: add a new read model by replaying history ===
Rebuilt from 6 stored events -&gt; top 2: [MILK-1L=8, MANGO-1KG=5], checkpoint=6
Live events continue; event 6 is redelivered by the broker:
top 2: [MANGO-1KG=9, MILK-1L=8], checkpoint=7
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the checkpoint (last applied sequence) makes replays and redeliveries safe, and lets a restarted projector resume. Persist it atomically with the view's data. With Kafka, use offsets per partition.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our order database spent 70% of its CPU on reporting queries from the customer "My orders" page and the support tool.</li> <li>T: Reduce load on the transactional database without degrading the pages.</li> <li>A: Introduced CQRS for order history: events from the outbox into a denormalized read table in a separate PostgreSQL instance, with idempotent projectors and lag monitoring; checkout stayed on the write side.</li> <li>R: Primary DB CPU dropped from 85% to 35% at peak; "My orders" p99 went from 1.2 s to 90 ms.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the database-per-service pattern?</p> <p>Each microservice has its own private database; other services access that data only through its API or events.</p> <p>Q2. Why not share one database?</p> <p>Shared schemas couple services: one team's change can break others, and services can't be deployed or scaled independently.</p> <p>Q3. How do you get data from another service?</p> <p>Call its API, or subscribe to its events and keep the needed data locally.</p> <p>Q4. How do you do transactions across services?</p> <p>With a saga (local transactions + compensations), not a distributed transaction.</p> <p>Q5. What is polyglot persistence?</p> <p>Using different database types for different services, each chosen for its workload.</p> <p>Q1. How do you build a page that needs data from 3 services?</p> <p>API composition (parallel, batched calls) for low volume; a CQRS read model fed by events for high volume or strict latency.</p> <p>Q2. What's the N+1 problem in API composition and how do you fix it?</p> <p>One call per item; fix with batch endpoints (<code>GET /customers?ids=...</code>), caching, or a read model.</p> <p>Q3. Is schema-per-service on one database server acceptable?</p> <p>Often, as a pragmatic step: enforce with separate DB users/grants. You lose independent scaling and failure isolation.</p> <p>Q4. How do you keep a local replica correct?</p> <p>Consume the owner's events idempotently, apply them with version checks, and never write to the replica except from events.</p> <p>Q5. How is reporting done?</p> <p>Stream data (CDC/events) into a data warehouse; don't query production service databases.</p> <p>Q1. "Plan the split of a 2 TB shared Oracle database used by 8 services."</p> <p>Table-access inventory, assign owners, introduce APIs/events for foreign access, enforce with grants, split schemas, CDC to sync during transition, move one bounded context at a time, with reconciliation and rollback at each step.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you enforce a cross-service invariant like a global credit limit?"</p> <p>Give one service ownership of the invariant (credit service) and route all reservations through it, often as a saga step; or redesign boundaries so the invariant lives in one service.</p> <p>Q3. "Two services need the same customer data. Replicate or call?"</p> <p>Call when freshness is critical and volume is low; replicate (events) when availability, latency or volume matter and slight staleness is fine.</p> <p>Q4. "What are the operational costs, and how do you contain them?"</p> <p>More databases to patch, back up, monitor and secure. Use managed services, platform templates/operators, standard backup policies, and limit the number of store types.</p> <p>Q5. "How does this relate to SAP's or a large ERP's single database?"</p> <p>ERPs favor one consistent database for tightly integrated processes (strong consistency, easy reporting). Microservices trade that for team autonomy; extensions around an ERP (side-by-side) typically own their data and integrate via APIs/events.</p> <p>Problem: Rewrite the order-history composition so it makes one call to the customer service no matter how many orders there are, and degrades gracefully if a customer is missing.</p> Solution BatchedComposer.javaBatchedComposerDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.batching;

import com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.grocery.CustomerService;
import com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.grocery.OrderService;
import com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.grocery.OrderService.OrderRow;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.TreeSet;

/** Coding challenge: the same composition with ONE batch call instead of one call per order (N+1). */
public final class BatchedComposer {

    private final OrderService orders;
    private final CustomerService customers;

    public BatchedComposer(OrderService orders, CustomerService customers) {
        this.orders = orders;
        this.customers = customers;
    }

    public List&lt;String&gt; history() {
        List&lt;OrderRow&gt; rows = orders.recentOrders();
        Set&lt;String&gt; ids = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;();
        rows.forEach(r -&gt; ids.add(r.customerId())); // collect distinct IDs first
        Map&lt;String, String&gt; names = customers.namesBatch(ids); // one round trip
        return rows.stream()
                .map(o -&gt; o.id() + " " + names.getOrDefault(o.customerId(), "(unknown customer)") + " ₹" + o.totalPaise() / 100)
                .toList();
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.batching;

import com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.grocery.CustomerService;
import com.handbook.microservices.dbperservice.grocery.OrderService;

public class BatchedComposerDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var customers = new CustomerService();
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: API composition without N+1 calls ===");
        new BatchedComposer(new OrderService(), customers).history().forEach(line -&gt; System.out.println("  " + line));
        System.out.println("customer-service API calls: " + customers.apiCalls());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: API composition without N+1 calls ===
  ORD-1 Asha Rao ₹798
  ORD-2 Vikram Singh ₹649
  ORD-3 Asha Rao ₹129
  ORD-4 Vikram Singh ₹399
customer-service API calls: 1
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: collect distinct IDs, call a batch endpoint once (cap the batch size, e.g. 100 IDs), map results in memory; missing entries get a placeholder instead of failing the page. The same idea is GraphQL's DataLoader.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Six services shared one PostgreSQL database; every release needed a cross-team schema review and two incidents were caused by "harmless" migrations.</li> <li>T: Let teams release independently without breaking each other.</li> <li>A: Mapped table access, assigned owners, replaced 23 cross-service queries with batch APIs and events, then moved to schema-per-service with locked-down grants, and finally separate instances for the two busiest services.</li> <li>R: Release frequency went from fortnightly to daily; schema-related incidents went to zero over the next two quarters.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What are microservices?</p> <p>An architecture where an application is built as a set of small, independently deployable services, each owning a business capability and its data, communicating over the network (HTTP/gRPC, messaging).</p> <p>Q2. How do you decide service boundaries?</p> <p>By business capability or DDD subdomain/bounded context, not by technical layer. Each service should own its data and handle most of its use cases on its own.</p> <p>Q3. Monolith vs. microservices?</p> <p>Monolith: one deployable, simple to develop and test, harder to scale teams. Microservices: independent deployment and scaling, at the cost of distributed-system complexity.</p> <p>Q4. What is a bounded context?</p> <p>A boundary within which a domain model and its terms have one consistent meaning ("Trip" in Dispatch vs. Billing).</p> <p>Q5. Should microservices share a database?</p> <p>No. Each service owns its data; others access it through APIs or events.</p> <p>Q1. What's a distributed monolith?</p> <p>Services that must be deployed together or share a database: all the costs of microservices, none of the independence.</p> <p>Q2. How do services get data they don't own?</p> <p>Synchronous API calls for fresh decisions; events to keep local read copies for display and queries.</p> <p>Q3. What's wrong with "entity services"?</p> <p>CRUD services per noun make features span many services, causing chatty calls and coordinated deployments.</p> <p>Q4. How do you handle a use case that writes to two services?</p> <p>Reconsider the boundary first; otherwise use a saga with compensations or make one part asynchronous via events.</p> <p>Q5. Core vs. supporting vs. generic subdomains?</p> <p>Core: competitive advantage, build it well. Supporting: needed, build simply. Generic: buy or use SaaS (auth, email).</p> <p>Q1. "Decompose a food-delivery platform." (classic)</p> <p>Capabilities: Customer, Restaurant/Menu, Ordering, Payments, Delivery (rider assignment, tracking), Notifications, Pricing/Promotions, Search. Each owns its data; order placement runs a saga (reserve → pay → assign rider); events feed search and analytics. Discuss team ownership, scaling hotspots (tracking, search), and starting as a modular monolith.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you validate boundaries before building?"</p> <p>Event storming, mapping use cases to data writes (like Example 3), checking change history (files that change together), and team topology. Prototype as modules and enforce boundaries with ArchUnit or Spring Modulith before splitting deployables.</p> <p>Q3. "When should you NOT use microservices?"</p> <p>Small teams, unclear domains, early-stage products, no mature CI/CD and observability. Start with a well-structured monolith.</p> <p>Q4. "How do you migrate a large monolith?"</p> <p>Strangler fig, extracting capabilities with clear seams first, an anti-corruption layer around legacy models, CDC for data during transition, and decommissioning milestones. Measure deploy frequency and lead time to show progress.</p> <p>Q5. "How does Conway's Law affect your design?"</p> <p>The architecture mirrors team communication. Align services with teams (inverse Conway maneuver); avoid services that need several teams to change together.</p> <p>Problem: Propose a service split for a food-delivery app (restaurants, menus, dishes, customers, orders, riders, assignments, rider locations, payments) such that no use case writes across services. Verify it with the <code>BoundaryChecker</code>.</p> Solution FoodDeliverySplitDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.decomposition.fooddelivery;

import com.handbook.microservices.decomposition.healthcare.BoundaryChecker;
import com.handbook.microservices.decomposition.healthcare.BoundaryChecker.UseCase;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;

/** Challenge solution: a food-delivery split where no use case writes across services. */
public class FoodDeliverySplitDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;UseCase&gt; useCases = List.of(
                new UseCase("Update menu", Set.of("menus", "dishes"), Set.of("restaurants")),
                new UseCase("Place order", Set.of("orders", "order_lines"), Set.of("dishes", "customers")),
                new UseCase("Assign rider", Set.of("assignments"), Set.of("riders", "orders")),
                new UseCase("Rider location ping", Set.of("rider_locations"), Set.of()),
                new UseCase("Capture payment", Set.of("payments"), Set.of("orders")));

        Map&lt;String, String&gt; split = Map.ofEntries(
                Map.entry("restaurants", "Restaurant"), Map.entry("menus", "Restaurant"), Map.entry("dishes", "Restaurant"),
                Map.entry("customers", "Customer"),
                Map.entry("orders", "Ordering"), Map.entry("order_lines", "Ordering"),
                Map.entry("riders", "Delivery"), Map.entry("assignments", "Delivery"), Map.entry("rider_locations", "Delivery"),
                Map.entry("payments", "Payments"));

        BoundaryChecker.check(split, useCases).forEach((uc, v) -&gt; System.out.printf("%-20s %s%n", uc, v));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Update menu          ok   (inside [Restaurant])
Place order          ok   (reads from [Customer, Restaurant]: API calls or a read model)
Assign rider         ok   (reads from [Ordering]: API calls or a read model)
Rider location ping  ok   (inside [Delivery])
Capture payment      ok   (reads from [Ordering]: API calls or a read model)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: every write stays inside one capability; cross-service reads are fine (Ordering reads menus through an API or a cached read model; Delivery and Payments learn about orders through <code>OrderPlaced</code> events). Placing an order end to end (reserve, pay, assign) still spans services, so it becomes a Saga.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our 12 entity-based services always deployed together; releases took a day.</li> <li>T: Restore independent delivery without a rewrite.</li> <li>A: Mapped use cases to data writes, redrew boundaries around four capabilities, merged services that always changed together, and replaced synchronous chains with events.</li> <li>R: Deploy frequency went from weekly to about 30 per day; cross-team deployment coordination dropped to near zero.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is distributed tracing?</p> <p>Following a single request across multiple services using a shared trace ID, recording a span for each operation.</p> <p>Q2. What is a span?</p> <p>A timed unit of work (e.g. an HTTP call or DB query) with a name, start time, duration, attributes and a parent span.</p> <p>Q3. How is the trace ID passed between services?</p> <p>In headers, typically the W3C <code>traceparent</code> header (or older B3 headers).</p> <p>Q4. Name some tracing tools.</p> <p>OpenTelemetry (standard), Jaeger, Zipkin, Grafana Tempo; Spring Boot uses Micrometer Tracing.</p> <p>Q5. Trace ID vs. correlation ID?</p> <p>A correlation ID only groups logs; a trace ID plus span IDs builds a timed tree of operations.</p> <p>Q1. Explain the <code>traceparent</code> header.</p> <p><code>version-traceid-parentid-flags</code>: 00, a 32-hex trace ID, the 16-hex span ID of the caller, and flags (01 = sampled).</p> <p>Q2. How do traces cross Kafka?</p> <p>The producer injects context into message headers; the consumer extracts it and creates a child span or a span link.</p> <p>Q3. Why do traces break in async code?</p> <p>Context is stored in thread-locals; work on other threads loses it unless executors propagate it.</p> <p>Q4. What is sampling and why is it needed?</p> <p>Recording only a fraction of traces to control overhead and storage; head-based (at the start) or tail-based (after completion).</p> <p>Q5. How do you connect logs to traces?</p> <p>Put trace/span IDs into the logging MDC and log pattern; backends link from a trace to its logs.</p> <p>Q1. "Design observability for 300 microservices."</p> <p>OTel everywhere (agents + SDK for custom spans), W3C propagation, collectors per cluster with tail sampling, a trace backend with 7–14 days retention, metrics with exemplars, structured logs with trace IDs, service maps and SLOs per service.</p> <p>Q2. "Head vs. tail sampling trade-offs?"</p> <p>Head: cheap and simple, but may drop the rare error traces. Tail: keeps interesting traces, but the collector must buffer whole traces (memory, routing by trace ID).</p> <p>Q3. "How do you measure the overhead of tracing?"</p> <p>Load-test with and without instrumentation; watch CPU, allocation and p99; tune sampling, batch export and attribute counts.</p> <p>Q4. "A trace shows a gap of 300 ms between two spans. What could it be?"</p> <p>Uninstrumented work (local computation, a library call), queueing in a thread pool or connection pool, GC pauses, network/proxy time, or consumer lag for async hops.</p> <p>Q5. "How would you trace a request through a legacy system that can't be instrumented?"</p> <p>Use a sidecar or proxy to create spans around it, or log the incoming trace ID and correlate; propagate the trace ID through its message fields if possible.</p> <p>Problem: Sample about 10% of traces, but every service must make the same decision for a given trace, so sampled traces are complete. Downstream services should honour the caller's decision.</p> Solution RatioSampler.javaSamplingDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.tracing.sampling;

import com.handbook.microservices.tracing.core.TraceContext;

/**
 * Coding challenge: keep ~10% of traces, and make every service agree on WHICH traces, so sampled
 * traces are complete. The root decides from the trace ID; downstream services honour the sampled
 * flag in traceparent (OpenTelemetry's ParentBased(TraceIdRatioBased) sampler).
 */
public final class RatioSampler {

    private final long threshold;

    public RatioSampler(double ratio) {
        this.threshold = (long) (ratio * Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    /** Root decision: a deterministic function of the trace ID (its last 16 hex chars). */
    public boolean shouldSampleRoot(String traceId) {
        long lower = Long.parseUnsignedLong(traceId.substring(16), 16) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1; // non-negative 63 bits
        return lower &lt; threshold;
    }

    /** Downstream decision: follow the parent. */
    public boolean shouldSample(TraceContext parent) {
        return parent.sampled();
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.tracing.sampling;

import com.handbook.microservices.tracing.core.TraceContext;

import java.util.Random;

public class SamplingDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RatioSampler sampler = new RatioSampler(0.10);
        Random ids = new Random(2026);
        int sampled = 0;
        int disagreements = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10_000; i++) {
            TraceContext root = TraceContext.newRoot(ids);
            boolean rootDecision = sampler.shouldSampleRoot(root.traceId());
            sampled += rootDecision ? 1 : 0;
            TraceContext sent = new TraceContext(root.traceId(), root.spanId(), rootDecision);
            boolean downstream = sampler.shouldSample(TraceContext.parse(sent.toTraceparent()));
            boolean recomputed = sampler.shouldSampleRoot(root.traceId()); // another service, same rule
            if (downstream != rootDecision || recomputed != rootDecision) {
                disagreements++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: consistent 10% trace sampling ===");
        System.out.println("traces sampled: " + sampled + " of 10,000");
        System.out.println("services disagreeing on a trace: " + disagreements + " (every sampled trace is complete)");
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: consistent 10% trace sampling ===
traces sampled: 994 of 10,000
services disagreeing on a trace: 0 (every sampled trace is complete)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the decision is a pure function of the trace ID (random bits), so any service computes the same answer; downstream services just follow the <code>sampled</code> flag (parent-based). This mirrors OpenTelemetry's <code>ParentBased(TraceIdRatioBased(0.1))</code>.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our p99 checkout latency doubled after a release, and each team's dashboard showed their service as healthy.</li> <li>T: Find the cause within the incident window.</li> <li>A: We had just rolled out OpenTelemetry; filtering traces over 2 s showed every slow trace contained a new synchronous call from pricing to a promotions service with no cache.</li> <li>R: Root cause in 20 minutes instead of a day; we added caching and a timeout, and made tracing part of our definition of done for new services.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is event sourcing?</p> <p>Storing every change as an immutable event and deriving current state by replaying events, instead of storing and overwriting the current state.</p> <p>Q2. Give a real-life example.</p> <p>A bank passbook or accounting ledger; Git commits.</p> <p>Q3. What is a snapshot?</p> <p>A saved copy of state at a version, so you don't have to replay all events from the beginning.</p> <p>Q4. Can you delete or edit an event?</p> <p>No. You append a corrective event (e.g. <code>PaymentReversed</code>).</p> <p>Q5. What's the relation to CQRS?</p> <p>Event sourcing gives you events; CQRS projections turn them into query-friendly read models. They're often used together.</p> <p>Q1. How is concurrency handled?</p> <p>Optimistic concurrency: append with the expected stream version; if another writer appended first, reload and retry.</p> <p>Q2. How do you query "all accounts with balance &gt; X"?</p> <p>Not from the event store; build a projection (read model) and query it.</p> <p>Q3. How do you change an event's schema?</p> <p>Version the event and add an upcaster that converts old versions when reading, or use tolerant readers for additive changes.</p> <p>Q4. Domain events vs. integration events?</p> <p>Domain events are internal and fine-grained; integration events are stable, public contracts published to other services.</p> <p>Q5. What makes replay slow, and how do you fix it?</p> <p>Long streams; fix with snapshots, and for projections, parallel replay per partition.</p> <p>Q1. "How do you handle GDPR erasure with an immutable log?"</p> <p>Crypto-shredding: encrypt PII per subject and delete the key; or keep PII out of events (reference IDs) and store it in a deletable store.</p> <p>Q2. "Would you use Kafka as the event store?"</p> <p>Usually not as the only store: no efficient per-aggregate reads or expected-version appends. Use a DB/EventStoreDB as the store and Kafka for distribution.</p> <p>Q3. "Design a ledger for a wallet with 10M users and audit requirements."</p> <p>Per-wallet streams, a unique <code>(wallet, version)</code> constraint, commands with balance checks, snapshots, outbox to Kafka, projections for balances and statements, reversals as events, crypto-shredding, nightly reconciliation of projections against streams.</p> <p>Q4. "A bug produced wrong events last week. What do you do?"</p> <p>Never edit history: append compensating events per affected stream (a scripted migration), fix the code, and rebuild affected projections. Document it for audit.</p> <p>Q5. "When would you advise against event sourcing?"</p> <p>CRUD-dominant domains, teams new to DDD, no audit/temporal needs, or tight deadlines. Consider an outbox + audit log instead.</p> <p>Problem: Version 1 of <code>PriceSet</code> stored the price as a rupee string (<code>"399.00"</code>). Version 2 stores integer paise and a currency. Old events can't be changed. Write an upcaster so the application only ever sees v2.</p> Solution Upcaster.javaUpcastingDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.eventsourcing.upcasting;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Coding challenge: stored events are immutable, but schemas evolve. Version 1 of PriceSet stored
 * the price as a rupee string ("399.00"); version 2 stores integer paise and a currency.
 * An upcaster converts old events on read, so the domain code only ever sees the latest version.
 */
public final class Upcaster {

    private Upcaster() {
    }

    public static Map&lt;String, Object&gt; upcast(Map&lt;String, Object&gt; stored) {
        Map&lt;String, Object&gt; e = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;(stored);
        if ("PriceSet".equals(e.get("type")) &amp;&amp; Integer.valueOf(1).equals(e.get("version"))) {
            long paise = new BigDecimal((String) e.remove("price")).movePointRight(2).longValueExact();
            e.put("version", 2);
            e.put("amountPaise", paise);
            e.put("currency", "INR"); // v1 only ever existed in the Indian store
        }
        return e;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.eventsourcing.upcasting;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class UpcastingDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;Map&lt;String, Object&gt;&gt; stored = List.of(
                event("PriceSet", 1, "sku", "MANGO-1KG", "price", "399.00"),
                event("PriceSet", 1, "sku", "RICE-5KG", "price", "649.50"),
                event("PriceSet", 2, "sku", "MANGO-1KG", "amountPaise", 34_900L, "currency", "INR"));

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: upcasting old event versions on read ===");
        for (Map&lt;String, Object&gt; e : stored) {
            System.out.println("stored  " + e);
            System.out.println("  read  " + Upcaster.upcast(e));
        }
    }

    private static Map&lt;String, Object&gt; event(String type, int version, Object... kv) {
        Map&lt;String, Object&gt; e = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();
        e.put("type", type);
        e.put("version", version);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; kv.length; i += 2) {
            e.put((String) kv[i], kv[i + 1]);
        }
        return e;
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: upcasting old event versions on read ===
stored  {type=PriceSet, version=1, sku=MANGO-1KG, price=399.00}
  read  {type=PriceSet, version=2, sku=MANGO-1KG, amountPaise=39900, currency=INR}
stored  {type=PriceSet, version=1, sku=RICE-5KG, price=649.50}
  read  {type=PriceSet, version=2, sku=RICE-5KG, amountPaise=64950, currency=INR}
stored  {type=PriceSet, version=2, sku=MANGO-1KG, amountPaise=34900, currency=INR}
  read  {type=PriceSet, version=2, sku=MANGO-1KG, amountPaise=34900, currency=INR}
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>BigDecimal.movePointRight(2).longValueExact()</code> avoids floating-point errors and fails loudly on unexpected precision; upcasters chain (v1→v2→v3); test every historical version in CI.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Auditors asked us to explain every balance change on 30,000 merchant accounts over 18 months; our CRUD system only had partial audit logs.</li> <li>T: Make balances fully explainable going forward.</li> <li>A: Moved the merchant ledger to event sourcing (PostgreSQL event table, snapshots, outbox to Kafka) and built statement and balance projections; ran both systems in parallel for two months with nightly reconciliation.</li> <li>R: The next audit was answered from the event log in two days instead of six weeks; reconciliation found and fixed 3 long-standing balance bugs.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What does idempotent mean?</p> <p>Doing an operation once or many times has the same effect. <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code> and <code>DELETE</code> are idempotent in HTTP; <code>POST</code> usually isn't.</p> <p>Q2. Why do duplicate messages happen?</p> <p>Brokers deliver at-least-once: consumer crashes, rebalances, producer retries and network timeouts all cause redelivery.</p> <p>Q3. How do you make a consumer idempotent?</p> <p>Store processed message IDs (unique constraint) in the same transaction as the business change, and skip IDs already seen.</p> <p>Q4. What is an idempotency key?</p> <p>A unique value the client sends with a request (e.g. <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header) so the server can recognise retries and return the original result.</p> <p>Q5. Is <code>balance = balance + 100</code> idempotent?</p> <p>No. Applying it twice adds 200. <code>status = 'SHIPPED'</code> is idempotent.</p> <p>Q1. Why must the dedupe record be in the same transaction?</p> <p>Otherwise a crash between recording and applying (or vice versa) loses the effect or applies it twice.</p> <p>Q2. What does Kafka's exactly-once semantics cover?</p> <p>Atomic consume-transform-produce within Kafka (transactions + idempotent producer). It doesn't make external side effects (DB writes outside Kafka, HTTP calls) exactly-once.</p> <p>Q3. How long do you keep processed IDs?</p> <p>At least as long as duplicates can arrive: broker retention plus any replay window. Purge older ones.</p> <p>Q4. How do you handle concurrent duplicates?</p> <p>Rely on an atomic database constraint or <code>SET NX</code>, not on check-then-act code.</p> <p>Q5. How do you test idempotency?</p> <p>Deliver each message twice (and concurrently) in tests, and assert the state is the same as with one delivery.</p> <p>Q1. "Design idempotency for a payments API used by mobile apps."</p> <p>Client-generated UUID keys; server table <code>(client_id, key)</code> unique with status and stored response; <code>IN_PROGRESS</code> row inserted first (concurrent retry → 409); request fingerprint check (422 on mismatch); 24–72 h expiry; downstream bank calls use the payment ID as their idempotency key.</p> <p>Q2. "Exactly-once end to end: is it possible?"</p> <p>Not as delivery; yes as effect: idempotent producers/outbox + at-least-once broker + idempotent consumers + idempotent external calls.</p> <p>Q3. "Your dedupe table has 5 billion rows. Options?"</p> <p>Partition by time and drop old partitions; shorter retention with version checks for older replays; key by business key instead of message ID; or move to a TTL store for low-risk effects.</p> <p>Q4. "Events can arrive out of order and duplicated. How do you design the consumer?"</p> <p>Version per aggregate, apply only newer versions (stale ones are skipped, duplicates are naturally ignored), buffer or reprocess on gaps if order matters.</p> <p>Q5. "Which operations would you make naturally idempotent?"</p> <p>State transitions with guards (<code>UPDATE ... SET status='SHIPPED' WHERE status='PACKED'</code>), upserts by business key, set-membership operations, and absolute values instead of deltas where the domain allows.</p> <p>Problem: The same refund message is processed by 8 threads at the same moment. Show that exactly one applies it, and explain why an in-memory "processed?" check wouldn't be enough.</p> Solution ConcurrentDuplicatesDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.idempotency.concurrent;

import com.handbook.microservices.idempotency.wallet.WalletCreditConsumer;
import com.handbook.microservices.support.Db;

import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/**
 * Coding challenge: the same message is processed by 8 consumers AT THE SAME TIME (e.g. after a
 * rebalance two instances both hold it). A check-then-act "if not processed then process" in
 * application code would let several through; the database unique constraint lets exactly one win.
 */
public class ConcurrentDuplicatesDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        DataSource db = Db.inMemory("wallet_race", WalletCreditConsumer.SCHEMA);
        var consumer = new WalletCreditConsumer(db);
        int racers = 8;
        CountDownLatch start = new CountDownLatch(1);
        AtomicInteger applied = new AtomicInteger();
        AtomicInteger skipped = new AtomicInteger();
        List&lt;Thread&gt; threads = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; racers; i++) {
            threads.add(Thread.ofPlatform().start(() -&gt; {
                try {
                    start.await();
                    if (consumer.onRefundIssued("msg-900", "CUST-77", 50_000)) {
                        applied.incrementAndGet();
                    } else {
                        skipped.incrementAndGet();
                    }
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException(e);
                }
            }));
        }
        start.countDown();
        for (Thread t : threads) {
            t.join();
        }
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: 8 concurrent deliveries of msg-900 (₹500 refund) ===");
        System.out.println("applied: " + applied.get() + ", skipped as duplicate: " + skipped.get());
        System.out.println("wallet balance: ₹" + Db.count(db, "SELECT balance FROM wallets WHERE customer = 'CUST-77'") / 100);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: 8 concurrent deliveries of msg-900 (₹500 refund) ===
applied: 1, skipped as duplicate: 7
wallet balance: ₹500
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: the unique key makes the database the arbiter: the first insert wins, and the others block on the row lock, then fail with SQLState <code>23505</code> and roll back. A check-then-act in Java would let several threads see "not processed" before any of them records it.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Customers occasionally received two loyalty-point credits after our Kafka consumers rebalanced during deploys.</li> <li>T: Eliminate duplicate effects without changing the broker setup.</li> <li>A: Added producer-assigned event IDs via the outbox, a <code>processed_messages</code> table written in the same transaction as point credits, and a test harness that redelivers every message twice in CI.</li> <li>R: Duplicate credits went to zero; we then safely enabled more aggressive retries, cutting failed-credit tickets by 70%.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is rate limiting?</p> <p>Limiting how many requests a client can make in a time period, to protect the service from overload and abuse.</p> <p>Q2. Which HTTP status code is returned?</p> <p>429 Too Many Requests, ideally with a <code>Retry-After</code> header.</p> <p>Q3. Name rate-limiting algorithms.</p> <p>Fixed window, sliding-window log, sliding-window counter, token bucket, leaky bucket.</p> <p>Q4. Where is rate limiting usually implemented?</p> <p>At the API gateway (Spring Cloud Gateway, Kong, AWS API Gateway), sometimes also inside services.</p> <p>Q5. What is the problem with a fixed window?</p> <p>Bursts at boundaries: a client can send the full limit at the end of one window and again at the start of the next, so 2× in a short time.</p> <p>Q1. How does a token bucket work?</p> <p>A bucket holds up to C tokens and refills at R per second; each request takes a token or is rejected. It allows bursts up to C and an average rate of R.</p> <p>Q2. Token bucket vs. leaky bucket?</p> <p>The token bucket allows bursts; the leaky bucket smooths output to a constant rate (requests queue and leak out).</p> <p>Q3. How do you rate limit across 10 instances?</p> <p>A shared store (Redis) with atomic operations (<code>INCR</code>+<code>EXPIRE</code>, or a Lua script for a token bucket or sliding window).</p> <p>Q4. What headers should a rate-limited API return?</p> <p><code>Retry-After</code>, <code>X-RateLimit-Limit</code>, <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code>, <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code> (or the IETF <code>RateLimit</code> headers).</p> <p>Q5. Resilience4j <code>@RateLimiter</code> limits what?</p> <p>Calls from your service (e.g. to respect a partner's quota), not incoming traffic.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a distributed rate limiter for 1M requests/second."</p> <p>Gateway-local token buckets for the bulk, synchronized with a Redis Cluster (sharded by key) via atomic Lua scripts; config service for per-plan limits; fail open with local limits; hot-key mitigation; metrics and alerts. Discuss accuracy vs. latency trade-offs.</p> <p>Q2. "Sliding window counter: how does it estimate?"</p> <p><code>estimate = previousWindowCount × (overlap fraction) + currentWindowCount</code>. Two counters per key, nearly as accurate as a log (coding challenge).</p> <p>Q3. "Multi-tenant SaaS: one tenant's batch job overloads shared services. Fix?"</p> <p>Per-tenant quotas at the gateway, per-tenant concurrency limits and fair queuing in services, separate pools or queues for batch traffic, and plan-based limits.</p> <p>Q4. "Rate limiting vs. load shedding?"</p> <p>Rate limiting enforces per-client policy even when the server is healthy; load shedding drops low-priority work when the server is overloaded, regardless of client.</p> <p>Q5. "Redis for rate limiting is down. What happens?"</p> <p>Decide explicitly: fail open (availability, risk of abuse) with a local fallback limit, or fail closed for high-risk endpoints (OTP, payments). Alert and degrade gracefully.</p> <p>Problem: Implement a sliding-window counter with O(1) memory per key, and show that it avoids the fixed window's boundary burst.</p> Solution SlidingWindowCounter.javaSlidingCounterDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.ratelimit.slidingcounter;

import java.time.Clock;

/**
 * Coding challenge: the sliding-window counter. Keeps just two numbers (current and previous fixed
 * window) and weights the previous window by how much of it still overlaps the sliding window.
 * Nearly as accurate as a log, with O(1) memory per key (Cloudflare's approach).
 */
public final class SlidingWindowCounter {

    private final long limit;
    private final long windowMillis;
    private final Clock clock;
    private long currentWindow = -1;
    private long currentCount;
    private long previousCount;

    public SlidingWindowCounter(long limit, long windowMillis, Clock clock) {
        this.limit = limit;
        this.windowMillis = windowMillis;
        this.clock = clock;
    }

    public synchronized boolean tryAcquire() {
        long now = clock.millis();
        long window = now / windowMillis;
        if (window != currentWindow) {
            previousCount = window == currentWindow + 1 ? currentCount : 0;
            currentCount = 0;
            currentWindow = window;
        }
        double elapsedFraction = (double) (now % windowMillis) / windowMillis;
        double estimate = previousCount * (1 - elapsedFraction) + currentCount;
        if (estimate &lt; limit) {
            currentCount++;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.ratelimit.slidingcounter;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.FakeClock;

public class SlidingCounterDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: fixed window vs. sliding window counter (limit 10/min) ===");
        FakeClock clock = FakeClock.at("2026-10-13T10:00:50Z");
        var sliding = new SlidingWindowCounter(10, 60_000, clock);
        long[] fixedCounts = new long[2];

        int slidingAllowed = 0;
        int fixedAllowed = 0;
        for (int burst = 0; burst &lt; 2; burst++) { // 10 requests at 10:00:50, 10 more at 10:01:05
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
                if (sliding.tryAcquire()) {
                    slidingAllowed++;
                }
                if (fixedCounts[burst]++ &lt; 10) {
                    fixedAllowed++;
                }
            }
            clock.advanceMillis(15_000);
        }
        System.out.println("20 requests within 15 seconds, straddling the minute boundary:");
        System.out.println("Fixed window allowed  : " + fixedAllowed + "  &lt;- 2x the limit in 15 s");
        System.out.println("Sliding window allowed: " + slidingAllowed);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: fixed window vs. sliding window counter (limit 10/min) ===
20 requests within 15 seconds, straddling the minute boundary:
Fixed window allowed  : 20  &lt;- 2x the limit in 15 s
Sliding window allowed: 11
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: at 10:01:05, 5 s into the new window, the previous window still counts 55/60 ≈ 92%, so only about one more request fits; the fixed window resets to zero and allows all 10. Memory is two counters per key; the error comes from assuming the previous window's requests were evenly spread.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: One partner's misconfigured integration sent 3,000 rps to our public API and degraded it for all 400 partners.</li> <li>T: Isolate partners from each other without blocking legitimate traffic.</li> <li>A: Implemented plan-based token buckets per API key at the gateway (Redis-backed), <code>Retry-After</code> headers, a usage dashboard per partner, and alerts on sustained 429 rates.</li> <li>R: The next misbehaving integration was capped within a second; other partners saw no impact, and we used the same limits to launch paid tiers.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. Why are timeouts important?</p> <p>Without them, a slow or hung dependency blocks threads forever, and the caller eventually runs out of threads.</p> <p>Q2. What is exponential backoff?</p> <p>Waiting longer between each retry (e.g. 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms) so a struggling service gets time to recover.</p> <p>Q3. What is jitter?</p> <p>Randomness added to the backoff so many clients don't retry at the same moment.</p> <p>Q4. What is a bulkhead?</p> <p>Limiting concurrent calls (or using separate thread pools) per dependency, so one failing dependency can't consume all resources.</p> <p>Q5. Which errors should be retried?</p> <p>Transient ones: timeouts, connection errors, 502/503/504, 429. Not 400/401/404 or business errors.</p> <p>Q1. Is it safe to retry a POST?</p> <p>Only if the operation is idempotent, for example with an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> the server deduplicates. Otherwise a retry after a timeout can create duplicates.</p> <p>Q2. Semaphore vs. thread-pool bulkhead?</p> <p>Semaphore: limits concurrency on the caller's thread (cheap). Thread pool: runs calls on a dedicated pool, isolating blocking code completely, but costs threads and a context switch.</p> <p>Q3. How do you choose a timeout value?</p> <p>From the dependency's latency distribution (a bit above p99) and the caller's overall budget. Measure, don't guess.</p> <p>Q4. How do retries and circuit breakers interact?</p> <p>Retries handle short blips; the breaker stops retries from hammering a dependency that is clearly down. Don't retry <code>CallNotPermittedException</code>.</p> <p>Q5. What does <code>orTimeout</code> on a <code>CompletableFuture</code> not do?</p> <p>It doesn't cancel or interrupt the underlying work; it only completes the future exceptionally.</p> <p>Q1. "Explain retry storms and how to prevent them."</p> <p>Many clients retrying at once multiply load on a degraded service, keeping it down. Prevent with jitter, capped attempts, retrying at one layer, retry budgets, circuit breakers, and server-side load shedding (429 with <code>Retry-After</code>).</p> <p>Q2. "What is deadline propagation?"</p> <p>Passing the remaining time budget to downstream calls (gRPC does this natively) so every hop knows when to stop. It avoids wasted work for requests the user has abandoned.</p> <p>Q3. "Size a bulkhead for a dependency doing 200 rps at 50 ms p99."</p> <p>Little's law: 200 × 0.05 = 10 concurrent calls at p99; add headroom (e.g. 20). During degradation (500 ms) demand jumps to 100; the bulkhead caps it at 20 and rejects the rest quickly.</p> <p>Q4. "What are hedged requests and when would you use them?"</p> <p>Sending a duplicate request to another replica if the first exceeds ~p95, using whichever returns first. It cuts tail latency for idempotent reads; cap the hedging rate to avoid doubling load.</p> <p>Q5. "Timeouts are set everywhere but latency incidents still cascade. Why?"</p> <p>Timeouts may be longer than callers' timeouts (wasted work), retries multiply load, shared pools lack bulkheads, or there is no load shedding at the server. Audit timeouts across the chain.</p> <p>Problem: Implement and compare backoff strategies: plain exponential, full jitter, and AWS's decorrelated jitter. Then show why jitter matters when 1,000 clients fail at the same moment.</p> Solution BackoffSchedule.javaBackoffDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.resilience.backoff;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;

/** Coding challenge: compare backoff strategies and see why jitter spreads out retry storms. */
public final class BackoffSchedule {

    private BackoffSchedule() {
    }

    public static List&lt;Long&gt; exponential(long base, long cap, int retries) {
        List&lt;Long&gt; waits = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= retries; i++) {
            waits.add(Math.min(cap, base * (1L &lt;&lt; i)));
        }
        return waits;
    }

    public static List&lt;Long&gt; fullJitter(long base, long cap, int retries, Random random) {
        return exponential(base, cap, retries).stream()
                .map(ceiling -&gt; (long) (random.nextDouble() * ceiling))
                .toList();
    }

    /** AWS "decorrelated jitter": sleep = min(cap, random_between(base, previous * 3)). */
    public static List&lt;Long&gt; decorrelatedJitter(long base, long cap, int retries, Random random) {
        List&lt;Long&gt; waits = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        long previous = base;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; retries; i++) {
            long upper = previous * 3;
            previous = Math.min(cap, base + (long) (random.nextDouble() * (upper - base)));
            waits.add(previous);
        }
        return waits;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.resilience.backoff;

import java.util.Random;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class BackoffDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Challenge: backoff strategies (base 100 ms, cap 5 s, 5 retries) ===");
        System.out.println("Exponential         : " + BackoffSchedule.exponential(100, 5_000, 5));
        System.out.println("Full jitter         : " + BackoffSchedule.fullJitter(100, 5_000, 5, new Random(7)));
        System.out.println("Decorrelated jitter : " + BackoffSchedule.decorrelatedJitter(100, 5_000, 5, new Random(7)));

        System.out.println("-- 1,000 clients fail at the same moment; when does their 1st retry land? --");
        Random random = new Random(11);
        long sameMs = IntStream.range(0, 1_000)
                .mapToLong(i -&gt; BackoffSchedule.exponential(100, 5_000, 1).get(0)).distinct().count();
        long jitteredMs = IntStream.range(0, 1_000)
                .mapToLong(i -&gt; BackoffSchedule.fullJitter(100, 5_000, 1, random).get(0)).distinct().count();
        System.out.println("Without jitter: all 1,000 retries in " + sameMs + " distinct millisecond (a thundering herd)");
        System.out.println("With jitter   : retries spread over " + jitteredMs + " distinct milliseconds");
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: backoff strategies (base 100 ms, cap 5 s, 5 retries) ===
Exponential         : [200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200]
Full jitter         : [146, 299, 278, 1435, 2266]
Decorrelated jitter : [246, 577, 668, 1808, 3870]
-- 1,000 clients fail at the same moment; when does their 1st retry land? --
Without jitter: all 1,000 retries in 1 distinct millisecond (a thundering herd)
With jitter   : retries spread over 200 distinct milliseconds
