🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a saga?</p> <p>A sequence of local transactions across services; if one fails, compensating transactions undo the earlier ones.</p> <p>Q2. Why not use a normal database transaction?</p> <p>Each microservice has its own database; a single transaction can't span them, and distributed transactions (2PC) are slow and reduce availability.</p> <p>Q3. Orchestration vs. choreography?</p> <p>Orchestration: a central coordinator tells services what to do. Choreography: services listen to events and react, with no central coordinator.</p> <p>Q4. What is a compensating transaction? Give an example.</p> <p>A business action that semantically undoes a previous step: refund a payment, release reserved stock, cancel a booking.</p> <p>Q5. Is a saga ACID?</p> <p>No: it's ACD without isolation, and eventually consistent.</p> <p>Q1. When would you choose orchestration?</p> <p>For complex flows with many steps, conditional logic or timeouts, where seeing the flow in one place matters.</p> <p>Q2. What happens if a compensation fails?</p> <p>Retry it (it must be idempotent); after repeated failures, alert and send to manual handling. Compensations must eventually succeed.</p> <p>Q3. What is a pivot transaction?</p> <p>The go/no-go step. Steps before it are compensatable; steps after it are retriable and must complete.</p> <p>Q4. How do sagas publish events reliably?</p> <p>With the transactional outbox: the local state change and the event are written in one local transaction, then relayed to the broker.</p> <p>Q5. Which tools help implement sagas in Java?</p> <p>Temporal, Camunda/Zeebe, Axon, Eventuate Tram, or Spring Kafka with a hand-written state machine.</p> <p>Q1. "Explain isolation anomalies in sagas and countermeasures."</p> <p>Lost updates, dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, because intermediate states are visible. Countermeasures: semantic locks (<code>PENDING</code>), commutative updates, version checks (reread value), and ordering steps by risk.</p> <p>Q2. "Design the order saga for a food delivery app, including timeouts."</p> <p>States: <code>PENDING → RESTAURANT_ACCEPTED → PAID → COURIER_ASSIGNED → CONFIRMED</code>; compensations: void payment authorization, cancel at restaurant; timeouts: restaurant must accept in 3 min, courier in 10 min (else reassign or cancel and refund); durable timers; outbox; idempotent handlers.</p> <p>Q3. "2PC vs. saga: when would you still use 2PC?"</p> <p>Within one organization, few XA-capable resources, short transactions, strong consistency required, and lower availability acceptable (e.g. DB + legacy queue in a monolith). Not across microservices.</p> <p>Q4. "How do you test sagas?"</p> <p>Unit test the orchestrator with fake participants for every failure point; contract tests for messages; chaos tests that crash the orchestrator mid-flow; and verify idempotency by redelivering every message.</p> <p>Q5. "How would you migrate a monolith's @Transactional checkout to a saga?"</p> <p>Identify steps and compensations, introduce <code>PENDING</code> states and the outbox while still in the monolith, extract one participant at a time behind its API, then move coordination to an orchestrator or workflow engine.</p> <p>Problem: The orchestrator pod crashes in the middle of a saga. Persist a saga log so a new pod can find unfinished sagas on startup and compensate the steps that completed.</p> Solution DurableSaga.javaDurableSagaDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.saga.recovery;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Coding challenge: an orchestrator that survives a crash. Before and after each step it appends to
 * a durable saga log (a database table in real life). On restart, it reads the log: an unfinished
 * saga is rolled back by compensating every step that completed.
 */
public final class DurableSaga {

    public record Step(String name, Runnable action, Runnable compensation) {
    }

    /** Stands in for a saga_log table: saga id → ordered entries. */
    public static final class SagaLog {
        private final Map&lt;String, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; entries = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();

        void append(String sagaId, String entry) {
            entries.computeIfAbsent(sagaId, k -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(entry);
        }

        public List&lt;String&gt; read(String sagaId) {
            return List.copyOf(entries.getOrDefault(sagaId, List.of()));
        }
    }

    private final SagaLog log;

    public DurableSaga(SagaLog log) {
        this.log = log;
    }

    public void run(String sagaId, List&lt;Step&gt; steps) {
        log.append(sagaId, "STARTED");
        for (Step step : steps) {
            log.append(sagaId, "BEGIN " + step.name());
            step.action().run();
            log.append(sagaId, "DONE " + step.name());
        }
        log.append(sagaId, "FINISHED");
    }

    /** Called on startup for every saga that has no FINISHED or ROLLED_BACK entry. */
    public void recover(String sagaId, List&lt;Step&gt; steps) {
        List&lt;String&gt; entries = log.read(sagaId);
        if (entries.contains("FINISHED") || entries.contains("ROLLED_BACK")) {
            return;
        }
        List&lt;Step&gt; completed = steps.stream().filter(s -&gt; entries.contains("DONE " + s.name())).toList();
        for (int i = completed.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {
            Step step = completed.get(i);
            step.compensation().run(); // compensations must be idempotent: we may crash here too
            log.append(sagaId, "UNDONE " + step.name());
        }
        log.append(sagaId, "ROLLED_BACK");
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.saga.recovery;

import com.handbook.microservices.saga.recovery.DurableSaga.SagaLog;
import com.handbook.microservices.saga.recovery.DurableSaga.Step;

import java.util.List;

public class DurableSagaDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SagaLog log = new SagaLog();
        List&lt;Step&gt; steps = List.of(
                new Step("reserve stock", () -&gt; say("inventory: reserved"), () -&gt; say("inventory: released")),
                new Step("charge payment", () -&gt; say("payment: charged"), () -&gt; say("payment: refunded")),
                new Step("schedule delivery", () -&gt; {
                    throw new Error("orchestrator pod killed (OOM)"); // simulates a crash mid-saga
                }, () -&gt; { }));

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: recovering a saga after the orchestrator crashes ===");
        try {
            new DurableSaga(log).run("ORD-7", steps);
        } catch (Error crash) {
            System.out.println("CRASH: " + crash.getMessage());
        }
        System.out.println("Saga log after crash : " + log.read("ORD-7"));

        System.out.println("-- new pod starts and scans unfinished sagas --");
        new DurableSaga(log).recover("ORD-7", steps);
        System.out.println("Saga log after restart: " + log.read("ORD-7"));
    }

    private static void say(String s) {
        System.out.println("  " + s);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: recovering a saga after the orchestrator crashes ===
  inventory: reserved
  payment: charged
CRASH: orchestrator pod killed (OOM)
Saga log after crash : [STARTED, BEGIN reserve stock, DONE reserve stock, BEGIN charge payment, DONE charge payment, BEGIN schedule delivery]
-- new pod starts and scans unfinished sagas --
  payment: refunded
  inventory: released
Saga log after restart: [STARTED, BEGIN reserve stock, DONE reserve stock, BEGIN charge payment, DONE charge payment, BEGIN schedule delivery, UNDONE charge payment, UNDONE reserve stock, ROLLED_BACK]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: write the log before and after each step. A step with <code>BEGIN</code> but no <code>DONE</code> is ambiguous (it may or may not have happened), which is why its compensation must also be safe to run when the action never happened. Real systems persist this in a <code>saga_instance</code> table or let Temporal do it.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: After splitting checkout into services, about 0.4% of orders ended up charged but not fulfilled; support refunded them by hand.</li> <li>T: Make checkout consistent without reintroducing a shared database.</li> <li>A: Designed an orchestrated saga with explicit compensations and a pivot at payment capture; added the outbox, idempotency keys and per-step timeouts; built a dashboard of stuck sagas.</li> <li>R: Orphaned charges went to zero; the support team's manual refund queue disappeared.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is service discovery?</p> <p>A way for services to find each other's network locations at runtime using a registry, instead of hard-coded addresses.</p> <p>Q2. Name a service registry.</p> <p>Netflix Eureka, Consul, ZooKeeper, etcd; Kubernetes has discovery built in (Services + DNS).</p> <p>Q3. What is a heartbeat?</p> <p>A periodic "I'm alive" message from an instance to the registry that renews its lease. Missed heartbeats mean the instance is removed.</p> <p>Q4. What does <code>@LoadBalanced</code> do in Spring?</p> <p>Makes a <code>RestTemplate</code>/<code>RestClient</code>/<code>WebClient</code> resolve service names (e.g. <code>http://order-service</code>) through discovery and balance across instances.</p> <p>Q5. Client-side vs. server-side discovery?</p> <p>Client-side: the caller queries the registry and picks an instance. Server-side: the caller calls a load balancer or DNS name that picks for it.</p> <p>Q1. Do you need Eureka on Kubernetes?</p> <p>Usually no. Kubernetes Services, DNS and readiness probes cover discovery; Spring Cloud Kubernetes can bridge if needed.</p> <p>Q2. An instance crashes. How long until callers stop using it?</p> <p>Up to the lease expiry (plus client cache refresh time). Clients should retry another instance on connection errors to cover that window.</p> <p>Q3. What's graceful shutdown in this context?</p> <p>Deregister (or fail readiness), wait for load balancers to notice, finish in-flight requests, then exit. Spring Boot has <code>server.shutdown=graceful</code>.</p> <p>Q4. Readiness vs. liveness?</p> <p>Readiness: can I take traffic now? (removed from the Service if not). Liveness: am I stuck? (container restarted if not).</p> <p>Q5. Why might DNS-based discovery be problematic?</p> <p>Caching: clients and JVMs cache DNS records (<code>networkaddress.cache.ttl</code>), so changes propagate slowly; and DNS has no health or load awareness.</p> <p>Q1. "Is a service registry CP or AP? Which would you choose?"</p> <p>Discovery should usually be AP: serving slightly stale endpoints is better than serving none. Eureka is AP with self-preservation; ZooKeeper is CP. Clients compensate for staleness with retries and outlier detection.</p> <p>Q2. "Design discovery for 2,000 services across 3 regions."</p> <p>Per-region registries or meshes with a control plane pushing endpoints (xDS); locality-aware routing (zone → region → global failover); clients cache; registry scales horizontally; health is local; cross-region calls are explicit.</p> <p>Q3. "Instances flap in and out of the registry every few minutes. Investigate."</p> <p>Check GC pauses or CPU throttling delaying heartbeats, health checks hitting slow dependencies, too-short leases, network packet loss. Fix by tuning leases, making health checks local, and giving heartbeats their own thread.</p> <p>Q4. "How do you do zero-downtime deploys with discovery?"</p> <p>Rolling updates: new instance registers and becomes ready → old instance fails readiness → drains → stops. Pre-stop hooks with a sleep cover propagation delay.</p> <p>Q5. "What's outlier detection and why is it needed on top of health checks?"</p> <p>Passive ejection of instances that return many 5xx or time out, based on real traffic. Health checks can pass while the instance fails real requests.</p> <p>Problem: Eureka's self-preservation: if more than 50% of leases expire at once, the registry is probably partitioned from healthy instances. Stop evicting and keep serving the last known instances until heartbeats return.</p> Solution SelfPreservingRegistry.javaSelfPreservationDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.discovery.selfpreservation;

import com.handbook.microservices.discovery.registry.ServiceRegistry;

import java.time.Clock;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Coding challenge (Eureka's "self-preservation"): if a large share of leases expire at once, the
 * registry is probably cut off from healthy instances by a network partition. Evicting them all would
 * leave callers with nothing, so it stops evicting until heartbeats recover.
 */
public final class SelfPreservingRegistry extends ServiceRegistry {

    private final double maxEvictionRatio;
    private boolean preserving;

    public SelfPreservingRegistry(Clock clock, Duration leaseTtl, double maxEvictionRatio) {
        super(clock, leaseTtl);
        this.maxEvictionRatio = maxEvictionRatio;
    }

    @Override
    public List&lt;String&gt; evictExpired() {
        int expired = expiredIds().size();
        int total = registeredCount();
        preserving = total &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; (double) expired / total &gt; maxEvictionRatio;
        if (preserving) {
            System.out.printf("    self-preservation ON: %d of %d leases expired at once, evicting nobody%n",
                    expired, total);
            return List.of();
        }
        return super.evictExpired();
    }

    @Override
    public List&lt;Instance&gt; lookup(String service) {
        return preserving ? lookupIncludingExpired(service) : super.lookup(service); // stale beats empty
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.discovery.selfpreservation;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.FakeClock;

import java.time.Duration;

public class SelfPreservationDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FakeClock clock = FakeClock.at("2026-10-13T10:00:00Z");
        var registry = new SelfPreservingRegistry(clock, Duration.ofSeconds(30), 0.5);
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) {
            registry.register("cart-service", "cart-" + i, "10.0.0." + i, "zone-a");
        }

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: self-preservation during a network partition ===");
        clock.advance(Duration.ofSeconds(40));
        registry.heartbeat("cart-1");
        registry.heartbeat("cart-2");
        registry.heartbeat("cart-3"); // only cart-4 really crashed
        System.out.println("1 of 4 expired  -&gt; evicted " + registry.evictExpired());

        clock.advance(Duration.ofSeconds(40)); // partition: no heartbeats reach the registry
        System.out.println("3 of 3 expired  -&gt; evicted " + registry.evictExpired());
        System.out.println("Callers still get " + registry.lookup("cart-service").size()
                + " instances (possibly stale, but better than none; clients retry on failure)");

        registry.heartbeat("cart-1");
        registry.heartbeat("cart-2");
        registry.heartbeat("cart-3");
        System.out.println("Partition heals -&gt; evicted " + registry.evictExpired()
                + ", lookup returns " + registry.lookup("cart-service").size() + " instances");
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: self-preservation during a network partition ===
1 of 4 expired  -&gt; evicted [cart-4]
    self-preservation ON: 3 of 3 leases expired at once, evicting nobody
3 of 3 expired  -&gt; evicted []
Callers still get 3 instances (possibly stale, but better than none; clients retry on failure)
Partition heals -&gt; evicted [], lookup returns 3 instances
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: a single crash (1 of 4) evicts normally; mass expiry signals a partition, not mass death; stale endpoints plus client retries beat an empty list. This is the AP trade-off in code.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Every deploy caused 20–40 seconds of 502 errors on our order APIs.</li> <li>T: Make deploys invisible to customers.</li> <li>A: Traced it to instances being killed before deregistering; added graceful shutdown (fail readiness → 15 s drain → stop), client retries on connection errors, and shorter client cache refresh.</li> <li>R: Deploy-related errors went to zero; we went from 2 deploy windows a week to deploying on demand.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is a sidecar?</p> <p>A helper container that runs next to the main application container in the same pod, adding features (proxying, logging, security) without changing the app.</p> <p>Q2. What is a service mesh?</p> <p>An infrastructure layer of sidecar proxies plus a control plane that handles service-to-service communication: mTLS, routing, retries, telemetry.</p> <p>Q3. Name a service mesh and its proxy.</p> <p>Istio (Envoy proxy), Linkerd (linkerd2-proxy), Consul Connect.</p> <p>Q4. What is mTLS?</p> <p>Mutual TLS: both client and server present certificates, so each side verifies the other's identity, and traffic is encrypted.</p> <p>Q5. Gateway vs. service mesh?</p> <p>The gateway handles traffic from clients into the system; the mesh handles traffic between services.</p> <p>Q1. What are the data plane and control plane?</p> <p>The data plane is the proxies handling traffic; the control plane configures them (routes, policies, certificates).</p> <p>Q2. How does a mesh do canary releases?</p> <p>Weighted routing between service subsets (e.g. 90/10), adjusted gradually, often automated with metrics analysis.</p> <p>Q3. Does a mesh give you distributed tracing automatically?</p> <p>It creates spans per hop, but apps must forward trace headers from inbound to outbound requests, or traces break into pieces.</p> <p>Q4. Downsides of sidecars?</p> <p>Per-pod CPU/memory, extra latency, operational complexity, lifecycle ordering issues.</p> <p>Q5. What's the Ambassador pattern?</p> <p>A sidecar that handles outbound connections for an app (routing, retries, TLS), useful for legacy apps you can't modify.</p> <p>Q1. "Would you introduce a mesh? Make the case for or against."</p> <p>For: polyglot services, zero-trust/compliance, need for uniform telemetry and traffic control, a platform team to run it. Against: small, homogeneous estates; no platform capacity; latency-critical paths where libraries suffice.</p> <p>Q2. "Explain how workload identity works in a mesh."</p> <p>The control plane (SPIFFE/SPIRE or Istiod) issues short-lived X.509 certificates bound to the Kubernetes service account; proxies use them for mTLS; authorization policies refer to these identities.</p> <p>Q3. "Sidecar vs. ambient mesh?"</p> <p>Sidecar: per-pod proxies, full L7 features everywhere, higher resource cost. Ambient: per-node L4 (mTLS, telemetry) plus optional per-namespace L7 waypoints; lower overhead, simpler upgrades.</p> <p>Q4. "Outlier detection vs. circuit breaker?"</p> <p>Outlier detection ejects individual misbehaving hosts from the pool; circuit breakers stop calls to a whole dependency and offer fallbacks. The mesh does the former well; apps do the latter.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you roll out mesh config changes safely?"</p> <p>GitOps, validation (istioctl analyze), canary namespaces, staged rollout, monitoring error rates, and quick revert, just like application deploys.</p> <p>Problem: Implement Envoy-style outlier detection: after 3 consecutive 5xx from a host, eject it for 30 s, but never eject more than 50% of hosts (or a bad dependency could eject everyone).</p> Solution OutlierDetector.javaOutlierDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.sidecar.outlier;

import java.time.Clock;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Coding challenge: Envoy-style outlier detection. After N consecutive 5xx from one host, eject it
 * from the load-balancing pool for a while, but never eject more than a maximum share of hosts.
 */
public final class OutlierDetector {

    private final List&lt;String&gt; hosts;
    private final int consecutive5xx;
    private final Duration ejectionTime;
    private final int maxEjectionPercent;
    private final Clock clock;
    private final Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; failures = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    private final Map&lt;String, Instant&gt; ejectedUntil = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

    public OutlierDetector(List&lt;String&gt; hosts, int consecutive5xx, Duration ejectionTime,
                           int maxEjectionPercent, Clock clock) {
        this.hosts = List.copyOf(hosts);
        this.consecutive5xx = consecutive5xx;
        this.ejectionTime = ejectionTime;
        this.maxEjectionPercent = maxEjectionPercent;
        this.clock = clock;
    }

    public List&lt;String&gt; healthyHosts() {
        Instant now = clock.instant();
        ejectedUntil.values().removeIf(until -&gt; !now.isBefore(until));
        return hosts.stream().filter(h -&gt; !ejectedUntil.containsKey(h)).toList();
    }

    public void record(String host, int status) {
        if (status &lt; 500) {
            failures.remove(host);
            return;
        }
        int n = failures.merge(host, 1, Integer::sum);
        boolean underCap = (ejectedUntil.size() + 1) * 100 / hosts.size() &lt;= maxEjectionPercent;
        if (n &gt;= consecutive5xx &amp;&amp; underCap &amp;&amp; !ejectedUntil.containsKey(host)) {
            ejectedUntil.put(host, clock.instant().plus(ejectionTime));
            failures.remove(host);
            System.out.println("    ejected " + host + " for " + ejectionTime.toSeconds() + "s");
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.sidecar.outlier;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.FakeClock;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;

public class OutlierDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FakeClock clock = FakeClock.at("2026-10-13T12:00:00Z");
        var detector = new OutlierDetector(List.of("pod-a", "pod-b", "pod-c", "pod-d"), 3,
                Duration.ofSeconds(30), 50, clock);

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: outlier detection (3 consecutive 5xx, max 50% ejected) ===");
        System.out.println("healthy: " + detector.healthyHosts());
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
            detector.record("pod-b", 503); // pod-b has a corrupted cache
        }
        System.out.println("healthy: " + detector.healthyHosts());
        for (String pod : List.of("pod-c", "pod-d")) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
                detector.record(pod, 503); // a bad dependency makes more pods fail
            }
        }
        System.out.println("healthy: " + detector.healthyHosts() + "  (pod-d kept: ejecting it would exceed 50%)");
        clock.advance(Duration.ofSeconds(30));
        System.out.println("30 s later healthy: " + detector.healthyHosts());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: outlier detection (3 consecutive 5xx, max 50% ejected) ===
healthy: [pod-a, pod-b, pod-c, pod-d]
    ejected pod-b for 30s
healthy: [pod-a, pod-c, pod-d]
    ejected pod-c for 30s
healthy: [pod-a, pod-d]  (pod-d kept: ejecting it would exceed 50%)
30 s later healthy: [pod-a, pod-b, pod-c, pod-d]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: consecutive failures (not a rate) keep it simple; the max ejection percent protects against a shared-dependency failure ejecting all hosts; ejected hosts return automatically after the ejection time (Envoy also increases ejection time for repeat offenders).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A security audit found that 40% of our internal service traffic was unencrypted and any pod could call payment APIs.</li> <li>T: Achieve encrypted, authorized service-to-service traffic within one quarter without changing 60 services.</li> <li>A: Rolled out Linkerd namespace by namespace in permissive mode, then strict mTLS; added default-deny authorization policies generated from observed traffic; dashboards from mesh metrics.</li> <li>R: 100% of east-west traffic encrypted and authorized; the audit finding was closed; p99 latency increased by under 1 ms.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the strangler fig pattern?</p> <p>Migrating a legacy system incrementally by routing one feature at a time to new services, until the old system can be removed.</p> <p>Q2. Why is it named after a fig?</p> <p>The strangler fig grows around a host tree and eventually replaces it, just as the new system grows around the old.</p> <p>Q3. What component makes it possible?</p> <p>A facade/router (often an API gateway) in front of the legacy system that decides where each request goes.</p> <p>Q4. Why not rewrite everything at once?</p> <p>Big-bang rewrites are risky, deliver no value until the end, and often fail; incremental migration is reversible and delivers continuously.</p> <p>Q5. What do you migrate first?</p> <p>A valuable but loosely coupled capability, to prove the approach with low risk.</p> <p>Q1. How do you handle data during migration?</p> <p>Sync data between legacy and new stores (CDC/events), switch ownership per capability, and run reconciliation until the legacy side stops using it.</p> <p>Q2. What's a parallel run?</p> <p>Running old and new logic on the same input, returning the old result and comparing, to find differences before switching.</p> <p>Q3. How do you roll back a migrated route?</p> <p>Change the routing rule (or flip the feature flag) back to legacy, which requires no deploy.</p> <p>Q4. What is branch by abstraction?</p> <p>Introducing an abstraction inside the monolith so old and new implementations can coexist and be switched behind it.</p> <p>Q5. How do you avoid "90% migrated forever"?</p> <p>Track retirement milestones, stop feature work in legacy areas, budget for the long tail, and delete legacy code after each migration.</p> <p>Q1. "Plan the migration of a 15-year-old ERP-integrated monolith to services."</p> <p>Facade first; capability map with coupling and value scores; ACL toward the ERP; CDC-based data sync; order of extraction; gradual rollouts with kill switches; parallel runs for financial logic; reconciliation; retirement plan; team topology.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you migrate a capability whose data is joined by 20 legacy queries?"</p> <p>Keep legacy as reader via a synced copy (the new service writes, CDC syncs back) while migrating readers to the new API one by one; remove sync at the end.</p> <p>Q3. "What metrics show migration progress and health?"</p> <p>Share of traffic on new services, legacy LOC/tables removed, mismatch rate in parallel runs, incident rate per migrated capability, lead time for changes in migrated vs. legacy areas.</p> <p>Q4. "Shadow traffic for writes: is it possible?"</p> <p>Only with side effects stubbed or isolated (separate sandbox DB, no external calls), and compare resulting state. Usually limit shadowing to reads and pure computations.</p> <p>Q5. "How do SAP customers apply this when moving to S/4HANA or the cloud?"</p> <p>Keep the core clean and move custom extensions to side-by-side services gradually, using APIs and events; route and migrate extensions one at a time instead of rewriting all custom code at once.</p> <p>Problem: Route requests to the new service but monitor its error rate over the last 10 calls. If it exceeds 20%, automatically roll back to legacy (and stay there until a human re-enables it). Users must always get an answer.</p> Solution GuardedRouter.javaGuardedRouterDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.strangler.autorollback;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

/**
 * Coding challenge: route traffic to the new service, but watch its error rate over the last N
 * calls. If it exceeds the threshold, switch back to legacy automatically and stay there until
 * a human re-enables the new path.
 */
public final class GuardedRouter {

    private final int window;
    private final int maxErrorPercent;
    private final Deque&lt;Boolean&gt; recent = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
    private boolean rolledBack;

    public GuardedRouter(int window, int maxErrorPercent) {
        this.window = window;
        this.maxErrorPercent = maxErrorPercent;
    }

    public synchronized String route(Supplier&lt;String&gt; modern, Supplier&lt;String&gt; legacy) {
        if (rolledBack) {
            return legacy.get();
        }
        try {
            String result = modern.get();
            record(false);
            return result;
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            record(true);
            return legacy.get(); // the user still gets an answer
        }
    }

    private void record(boolean error) {
        recent.addLast(error);
        if (recent.size() &gt; window) {
            recent.removeFirst();
        }
        long errors = recent.stream().filter(b -&gt; b).count();
        if (recent.size() == window &amp;&amp; errors * 100 / window &gt; maxErrorPercent) {
            rolledBack = true;
            System.out.println("    AUTO-ROLLBACK: " + errors + " errors in last " + window + " calls");
        }
    }

    public synchronized void reEnable() {
        rolledBack = false;
        recent.clear();
    }

    public synchronized boolean rolledBack() {
        return rolledBack;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.strangler.autorollback;

public class GuardedRouterDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var router = new GuardedRouter(10, 20);
        boolean[] modernBroken = {false};

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: automatic rollback when the new service misbehaves ===");
        StringBuilder served = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 30; i++) {
            if (i == 11) {
                modernBroken[0] = true; // a bad deploy of the new service
            }
            int request = i;
            String who = router.route(() -&gt; {
                if (modernBroken[0] &amp;&amp; request % 2 == 0) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException("500");
                }
                return "N";
            }, () -&gt; "L");
            served.append(who);
            if (i % 10 == 0) {
                System.out.println("requests " + (i - 9) + "-" + i + ": " + served + "  (N=new, L=legacy)");
                served.setLength(0);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Rolled back: " + router.rolledBack() + "; every request still got an answer.");
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: automatic rollback when the new service misbehaves ===
