</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: a failed request falls back to legacy so users don't see errors; rollback is sticky (no flapping) and needs a human to re-enable; in production this is what progressive-delivery tools (Argo Rollouts, Flagger) automate using metrics.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our insurer's quoting monolith had 14-week release cycles; a previous rewrite attempt had been cancelled after 18 months.</li> <li>T: Modernize quoting without stopping the business.</li> <li>A: Introduced a gateway facade, extracted vehicle lookup first, then the premium engine with a 6-week parallel run that found 11 undocumented rules; rolled out by region with a kill switch.</li> <li>R: Quoting moved fully to services in 10 months; release cycle dropped to weekly; zero pricing incidents during migration.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the dual-write problem?</p> <p>Writing to a database and publishing to a broker as two separate operations: if one succeeds and the other fails, the systems become inconsistent.</p> <p>Q2. What is the transactional outbox pattern?</p> <p>Save the event into an outbox table in the same DB transaction as the business data, then a separate relay publishes it to the broker.</p> <p>Q3. Does it guarantee exactly-once delivery?</p> <p>No: at-least-once. Consumers must handle duplicates.</p> <p>Q4. What is Debezium?</p> <p>A change data capture tool that reads the database's transaction log and publishes changes (including outbox rows) to Kafka.</p> <p>Q5. Why not publish to Kafka inside <code>@Transactional</code>?</p> <p>Kafka isn't part of the database transaction; the event could be sent even if the DB commit later fails.</p> <p>Q1. Polling relay vs. CDC?</p> <p>Polling: simple, adds query load and latency. CDC: low latency, no polling, but needs Kafka Connect/Debezium infrastructure.</p> <p>Q2. How do you preserve event order?</p> <p>Relay in ID order and use the aggregate ID as the message key, so all events for one entity land in one partition.</p> <p>Q3. How do multiple relay instances avoid duplicates?</p> <p><code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>, atomic claim updates, or a single active relay via leader election.</p> <p>Q4. How do consumers handle duplicates?</p> <p>Store processed event IDs (unique constraint) in the same transaction as their own update and skip IDs already seen.</p> <p>Q5. What do you monitor?</p> <p>Outbox lag (age of the oldest unpublished row), relay error rate, and table size.</p> <p>Q1. "Design reliable event publishing for 50k writes/second."</p> <p>CDC (Debezium) from the WAL instead of polling, outbox partitioned by time, events keyed by aggregate, consumers idempotent; capacity for connector lag; schema registry for payloads.</p> <p>Q2. "Outbox vs. event sourcing?"</p> <p>The outbox adds events alongside state; event sourcing makes events the state. The outbox is far easier to adopt in an existing CRUD service.</p> <p>Q3. "What if the relay publishes, the broker acks, and the DB update to mark published fails?"</p> <p>The event is republished on the next poll, which is expected under at-least-once. Consumers deduplicate. Never try to make it exactly-once at the producer.</p> <p>Q4. "Should the outbox contain the full entity or a minimal event?"</p> <p>A business event with the fields consumers need (and a version). Thin events ("OrderPlaced id=1") force consumers to call back (coupling and load); full row dumps leak internals. Design them as public contracts.</p> <p>Q5. "How do you migrate a service with dual writes to the outbox safely?"</p> <p>Add the outbox table and write to it in the transaction, run the relay to a shadow topic and compare, switch consumers, remove the direct publish, and run a reconciliation job for the past window.</p> <p>Problem: Run three relay instances concurrently over 500 outbox rows. No row may be published twice and none may be missed. (PostgreSQL would use <code>SKIP LOCKED</code>; implement it with an atomic conditional <code>UPDATE</code>.)</p> Solution ClaimingRelay.javaClaimingRelayDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.outbox.claiming;

import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.LongConsumer;

/**
 * Coding challenge: several relay instances (for availability) must not publish the same row.
 * Each relay atomically claims a batch with a conditional UPDATE, then publishes only its claim.
 * (On PostgreSQL you would use SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED instead.)
 */
public final class ClaimingRelay {

    private final DataSource db;
    private final String relayId;

    public ClaimingRelay(DataSource db, String relayId) {
        this.db = db;
        this.relayId = relayId;
    }

    public int pollOnce(int batchSize, LongConsumer publish) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection c = db.getConnection()) {
            c.setAutoCommit(false);
            List&lt;Long&gt; claimed = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
            try (PreparedStatement select = c.prepareStatement(
                    "SELECT id FROM outbox WHERE claimed_by IS NULL ORDER BY id LIMIT ?");
                 PreparedStatement claim = c.prepareStatement(
                         "UPDATE outbox SET claimed_by = ? WHERE id = ? AND claimed_by IS NULL")) {
                select.setInt(1, batchSize);
                try (ResultSet rs = select.executeQuery()) {
                    while (rs.next()) {
                        claim.setString(1, relayId);
                        claim.setLong(2, rs.getLong(1));
                        if (claim.executeUpdate() == 1) { // 0 rows means another relay won this one
                            claimed.add(rs.getLong(1));
                        }
                    }
                }
                c.commit();
            } catch (SQLException e) {
                c.rollback();
                throw e;
            }
            claimed.forEach(publish::accept);
            return claimed.size();
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.microservices.outbox.claiming;

import com.handbook.microservices.support.Db;

import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class ClaimingRelayDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        DataSource db = Db.inMemory("claiming",
                "CREATE TABLE outbox (id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY, claimed_by VARCHAR)",
                "INSERT INTO outbox (claimed_by) SELECT NULL FROM SYSTEM_RANGE(1, 500)");

        Map&lt;Long, AtomicInteger&gt; publishCount = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();
        CountDownLatch start = new CountDownLatch(1);
        Thread[] relays = new Thread[3];
        for (int r = 0; r &lt; relays.length; r++) {
            ClaimingRelay relay = new ClaimingRelay(db, "relay-" + r);
            relays[r] = Thread.ofPlatform().start(() -&gt; {
                try {
                    start.await();
                    while (relay.pollOnce(20, id -&gt; publishCount.computeIfAbsent(id, k -&gt; new AtomicInteger())
                            .incrementAndGet()) &gt; 0) {
                        // keep polling until nothing is left
                    }
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException(e);
                }
            });
        }
        start.countDown();
        for (Thread t : relays) {
            t.join();
        }

        System.out.println("=== Challenge: 3 relay instances, 500 outbox rows ===");
        System.out.println("Rows published      : " + publishCount.size());
        System.out.println("Published twice     : " + publishCount.values().stream().filter(c -&gt; c.get() &gt; 1).count());
        System.out.println("Every row claimed   : " + (Db.count(db, "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM outbox WHERE claimed_by IS NULL") == 0));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>=== Challenge: 3 relay instances, 500 outbox rows ===
Rows published      : 500
Published twice     : 0
Every row claimed   : true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>UPDATE ... WHERE claimed_by IS NULL</code> is atomic per row, so only one relay's update returns 1. Also add a claim timeout (if a relay dies after claiming, the rows must be re-claimable), and remember that cross-relay ordering per aggregate needs partitioning rows by key.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our notification service occasionally missed "order shipped" events; customers called support asking where their parcels were.</li> <li>T: Guarantee every shipment produced a notification.</li> <li>A: Found dual writes in 6 services; introduced a shared outbox library with a polling relay, lag metrics, and consumer dedupe; ran a reconciliation to backfill.</li> <li>R: Missed notifications went from about 300/week to zero; outbox lag p99 stayed under 800 ms.</li> </ul>

