🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Adapter pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern that converts one interface into another that the client expects, letting incompatible classes work together. Like a travel plug adapter.</p> <p>Q2. Object adapter vs. class adapter?</p> <p>An object adapter holds the adaptee as a field (composition). A class adapter extends the adaptee (inheritance). Java prefers the object adapter: it works with <code>final</code> classes and with any subclass of the adaptee.</p> <p>Q3. Give JDK examples.</p> <p><code>Arrays.asList()</code>, <code>InputStreamReader</code> (bytes to characters), <code>Collections.enumeration()</code>, and <code>Executors.callable(Runnable)</code>.</p> <p>Q4. When would you use Adapter?</p> <p>When integrating a third-party library or legacy code whose interface doesn't match yours, and you can't change it.</p> <p>Q5. Adapter vs. Facade?</p> <p>Adapter makes one existing interface fit an expected interface. Facade provides a new, simpler interface to a whole subsystem.</p> <p>Q1. What else besides method names does an adapter translate?</p> <p>Data formats (XML, maps to records), units (°F to °C, rupees to paise), identifiers, error codes and exceptions, and sometimes call sequences (two vendor calls for one target call, as in Example 1).</p> <p>Q2. How do you test an adapter?</p> <p>With a fake adaptee (assert the exact values sent, as in <code>AdapterTest</code>) and contract tests against recorded real responses. Cover unknown codes and malformed data.</p> <p>Q3. How does Spring MVC use Adapter?</p> <p><code>DispatcherServlet</code> works with many handler types. A <code>HandlerAdapter</code> for each type (<code>RequestMappingHandlerAdapter</code> for <code>@Controller</code> methods, and others) adapts them all to one <code>handle(request, response, handler)</code> call.</p> <p>Q4. What is a two-way adapter?</p> <p>One class that implements both the old and new interfaces, so both kinds of client can use it during a gradual migration.</p> <p>Q5. Where should retries and timeouts live?</p> <p>Around the call to the external system: either inside the adapter or as a decorator/proxy wrapping it. Keep them out of business code, and configure them per vendor.</p> <p>Q1. "We integrate 40 shipping carriers. Design the integration layer."</p> <p>A <code>CarrierPort</code> interface (rate, label, track, cancel) with one adapter per carrier, packaged as plug-ins and selected from a registry. Add a canonical data model, contract tests per carrier, per-carrier resilience (timeouts, circuit breakers, bulkheads), mapping tables as config, webhook adapters for carriers that push updates, and observability by carrier. Consider generating adapters for carriers that share a standard.</p> <p>Q2. "How do you migrate from a legacy ERP to a new one without a big bang?"</p> <p>An anti-corruption layer of adapters behind a stable domain interface; strangler fig routing by entity or tenant; dual writes or CDC during the transition with reconciliation; flip reads per module with feature flags; retire the legacy adapter at the end.</p> <p>Q3. "A vendor changes an error code without notice and checkout breaks. How do you design against that?"</p> <p>Unknown codes map to an explicit "unknown" outcome that alerts, never to success. Contract tests run daily against the vendor sandbox. Metrics per code detect a new code within minutes, and a circuit breaker can fail over to a second provider.</p> <p>Q4. "Is an adapter a performance problem?"</p> <p>The wrapper itself is negligible (often inlined by the JIT). Costs come from what it does: parsing, extra network calls, object mapping. Measure, cache static mappings, and avoid adapters that make N+1 calls to the adaptee.</p> <p>Q5. "Adapter vs. Anti-Corruption Layer vs. API Gateway?"</p> <p>Adapter: a class-level translation. ACL: a set of adapters and translators that protects a bounded context's model. API Gateway: a network-level entry point that routes, authenticates and rate-limits. It may also adapt protocols, but its main job is edge concerns.</p> <p>Problem: A legacy invoicing library returns <code>java.util.Enumeration</code>, but your code uses <code>Iterator</code> and for-each loops. Write <code>EnumerationIterator&lt;T&gt;</code> so legacy results work in a for-each loop. What should <code>remove()</code> do?</p> Solution EnumerationIterator.javaLegacyDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.adapter.legacy;

import java.util.Enumeration;
import java.util.Iterator;

/** Challenge solution: let old Enumeration-based APIs work with modern Iterator code. */
public class EnumerationIterator&lt;T&gt; implements Iterator&lt;T&gt; {

    private final Enumeration&lt;T&gt; enumeration;

    public EnumerationIterator(Enumeration&lt;T&gt; enumeration) {
        this.enumeration = enumeration;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean hasNext() {
        return enumeration.hasMoreElements();
    }

    @Override
    public T next() {
        return enumeration.nextElement();
    }

    @Override
    public void remove() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Enumeration does not support remove()");
    }

    /** Bonus: use it in a for-each loop. */
    public static &lt;T&gt; Iterable&lt;T&gt; iterable(Enumeration&lt;T&gt; e) {
        return () -&gt; new EnumerationIterator&lt;&gt;(e);
    }
}
