🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Bridge pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern that separates an abstraction from its implementation so both can vary independently. The abstraction holds a reference to the implementor; that reference is the "bridge".</p> <p>Q2. What problem does it solve?</p> <p>Class explosion from combining two dimensions with inheritance (M × N classes become M + N).</p> <p>Q3. Give an example.</p> <p>Shapes (Circle, Square) × renderers (SVG, ASCII), remotes × devices, reports × formats, and JDBC (your code uses the JDBC API; vendor drivers implement it).</p> <p>Q4. Bridge vs. Adapter?</p> <p>Bridge is designed up front to let two sides vary; Adapter is applied afterwards to make existing incompatible classes work together.</p> <p>Q5. Which principle does Bridge follow?</p> <p>Composition over inheritance, plus Open/Closed (add an implementor without touching abstractions) and Single Responsibility.</p> <p>Q1. Bridge vs. Strategy?</p> <p>Similar structure; different intent. Strategy swaps an algorithm inside one class. Bridge splits two hierarchies that both get subclassed and evolve independently.</p> <p>Q2. How do you choose the implementor at runtime?</p> <p>Pass it into the abstraction's constructor (DI), or look it up from a map by a request parameter (<code>format=csv</code>).</p> <p>Q3. How do you design the implementor interface?</p> <p>Keep it to primitive operations every implementation can support; build richer behavior in refined abstractions; add capability queries for optional features.</p> <p>Q4. How do you test a Bridge?</p> <p>Abstractions with a fake implementor (or a lambda); implementors with a shared contract test that every implementation must pass.</p> <p>Q5. How is JDBC a Bridge?</p> <p>The abstraction is the JDBC API (<code>Connection</code>, <code>Statement</code>, <code>ResultSet</code>) your code uses; the implementors are vendor drivers. Your DAO code works with any database driver, and drivers evolve without changing your code.</p> <p>Q1. "Design a notification platform: 10 message types, 6 channels, more of both every year."</p> <p>Bridge: <code>Notification</code> (type: content, priority, templates) × <code>Channel</code> (SMS, email, push, WhatsApp; send, capabilities). Add decorators on channels (retries, rate limits, metrics), fallback chains (push fails, so SMS), user preferences, idempotency keys, and per-channel queues for isolation.</p> <p>Q2. "How would you design a storage abstraction over S3, GCS and local disk?"</p> <p>The abstraction (<code>BlobStore</code> with higher-level operations like <code>putWithChecksum</code>, <code>copy</code>, <code>listByPrefix</code>) uses a small implementor SPI (put, get, list, delete, with capability flags such as multipart support). Contract tests per backend; consistency differences documented; cost and latency metrics per backend.</p> <p>Q3. "The implementor interface needs a new method. How do you evolve it across 12 implementations?"</p> <p>Add it as a default method (fallback built from existing primitives, or a capability flag that reports "unsupported"); roll out implementations gradually; then make it required. Version the SPI if third parties implement it.</p> <p>Q4. "When is Bridge over-engineering?"</p> <p>When there's only one real dimension or one implementation, or when the second dimension is speculative. Start with Strategy or a single class and refactor to a Bridge when the second axis is real.</p> <p>Q5. "Where else does this appear at system level?"</p> <p>Driver models (JDBC, ODBC), logging facades (SLF4J + back-ends), cross-platform UI frameworks (one widget API, native renderers), observability (OpenTelemetry API + exporters).</p> <p>Problem: You have <code>Circle</code> and <code>Square</code>, and two renderers: SVG and ASCII. Design it so a new shape or a new renderer is a single new class, and show all four combinations.</p> Solution Renderer.javaShapes.javaShapesDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.bridge.shapes;

