    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (Renderer r : List.of(new SvgRenderer(), new AsciiRenderer())) {
            System.out.println(new Circle(r, 3).draw() + "  " + new Square(r, 4).draw());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>&lt;circle r="3"/&gt;  &lt;rect width="4" height="4"/&gt;
( ooo )  [####]
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: shapes know what they are; renderers know how to draw primitives. 2 shapes + 2 renderers = 4 classes for 4 combinations; a <code>Triangle</code> or a <code>CanvasRenderer</code> is one more class each. (Adding a shape also means adding a primitive to <code>Renderer</code>, which is the trade-off of a Bridge whose implementor has one method per shape. Mention it.)</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our notifications code had grown to 54 classes (types × channels) with copy-pasted retry logic.</li> <li>T: Add WhatsApp without 9 more classes, and fix retries once.</li> <li>A: Refactored to a Bridge (notification types × channels), moved retries into a channel decorator, and added a contract test every channel must pass.</li> <li>R: 15 classes instead of 54; WhatsApp shipped as one class in 4 days; retry-related incidents dropped to zero.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Composite pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern that composes objects into tree structures and lets clients treat individual objects and groups uniformly through a common interface.</p> <p>Q2. Name the participants.</p> <p>Component (common interface), Leaf (no children), Composite (holds children, delegates to them), and Client.</p> <p>Q3. Give real examples.</p> <p>File system (files and folders), UI (panels contain buttons and panels), org charts, product bundles, menus with sub-menus.</p> <p>Q4. How does a composite compute something like total price?</p> <p>It iterates its children, calls the same method on each (leaves return their value; composites recurse), and combines the results.</p> <p>Q5. Which principle does it support?</p> <p>Open/Closed (new node types without changing clients) and treating objects polymorphically.</p> <p>Q1. Should <code>add()</code> be in the Component interface?</p> <p>It's a trade-off: on the component gives transparency (uniform API) but leaves must throw; on the composite only gives type safety. Modern Java usually prefers safety, with sealed types.</p> <p>Q2. How do you prevent cycles?</p> <p>Check on insert whether the new child already contains the parent (Example 3), or track a visited set during traversal.</p> <p>Q3. How would you iterate a composite?</p> <p>Recursively for small trees; an explicit stack/queue for deep or untrusted trees (DFS/BFS); or a Visitor for many operations.</p> <p>Q4. How do you make roll-ups fast on large trees?</p> <p>Cache aggregates per node and invalidate up the parent chain on changes, or pre-compute in storage.</p> <p>Q5. Composite vs. Decorator?</p> <p>Both use recursive composition, but a decorator wraps one object to add behavior, while a composite holds many children to represent a whole.</p> <p>Q1. "Design org-chart storage for a company with 500k employees and frequent re-orgs."</p> <p>Compare adjacency list + recursive CTE (simple writes), closure table (fast subtree and ancestor queries; re-org updates many rows), materialized path (cheap reads, path rewrites on moves). Add cached roll-ups (headcount, cost) updated by events, effective dating (who reported to whom on a date), and access control on subtrees.</p> <p>Q2. "Permission checks take 40 ms because of nested roles. Fix it."</p> <p>Pre-compute effective permissions per role (flatten the tree), cache per user, invalidate on role or assignment changes via events; check with a set lookup (O(1)). Keep the tree for administration and audit only.</p> <p>Q3. "How do you render a 1-million-node tree in a UI?"</p> <p>Don't build it all: lazy-load children on expand, paginate large child lists, use virtualized rendering, and compute aggregates server-side.</p> <p>Q4. "When does Composite become an anti-pattern?"</p> <p>When the structure is really a graph (multiple parents), when leaves need very different interfaces (a forced common interface), or when trees get so deep that recursion and roll-ups hurt performance.</p> <p>Q5. "Sealed interfaces + pattern matching vs. Visitor for operations on a composite?"</p> <p>Since Java 21, pattern matching over a sealed hierarchy gives exhaustive, compile-checked type dispatch without accept/visit boilerplate. Use it for operations outside the model; keep core behavior as methods on the nodes. Classic Visitor still helps when operations need double dispatch or when the hierarchy isn't sealed.</p> <p>Problem: Model a file system with files and folders. <code>sizeBytes()</code> on a folder returns the total size of everything inside it. Print the tree with sizes, children sorted by name, indented by depth.</p> Solution Node.javaFileSystemDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.composite.filesystem;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

/** Challenge solution: files and folders with size and a tree printout. */
public abstract sealed class Node permits Node.File, Node.Folder {

    protected final String name;

    protected Node(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public abstract long sizeBytes();

    public abstract void print(String indent, StringBuilder out);

    public static final class File extends Node {
        private final long size;

        public File(String name, long size) {
            super(name);
            this.size = size;
        }

        public long sizeBytes() { return size; }

        public void print(String indent, StringBuilder out) {
            out.append(indent).append(name).append(" (").append(size).append(" B)\n");
        }
    }

    public static final class Folder extends Node {
        private final List&lt;Node&gt; children = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

        public Folder(String name, Node... children) {
            super(name);
            this.children.addAll(List.of(children));
        }

        public long sizeBytes() {
            return children.stream().mapToLong(Node::sizeBytes).sum();
        }

        public void print(String indent, StringBuilder out) {
            out.append(indent).append(name).append("/ (").append(sizeBytes()).append(" B)\n");
            children.stream().sorted(Comparator.comparing(n -&gt; n.name))
                    .forEach(c -&gt; c.print(indent + "  ", out));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.composite.filesystem;

import com.handbook.structural.composite.filesystem.Node.File;
import com.handbook.structural.composite.filesystem.Node.Folder;

public class FileSystemDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Folder root = new Folder("project",
                new File("pom.xml", 2_048),
                new Folder("src",
                        new File("App.java", 4_096),
                        new Folder("util", new File("Strings.java", 1_024))),
                new File("README.md", 512));
        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
        root.print("", out);
        System.out.print(out);
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>project/ (7680 B)
  README.md (512 B)
  pom.xml (2048 B)
  src/ (5120 B)
    App.java (4096 B)
    util/ (1024 B)
      Strings.java (1024 B)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: sealed abstract class with two final subclasses; folder size is the sum of children (recursive); sorting is done at print time so the model keeps insertion order; for real disks you'd cache folder sizes and handle symlink cycles.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A customer locked themselves out after building a circular custom role.</li> <li>T: Restore access immediately and make it impossible to recur.</li> <li>A: Hot-fixed the resolver to use an iterative traversal with a visited set, added cycle validation in the role editor, and flattened effective permissions into a per-user cache.</li> <li>R: Access restored in 40 minutes; permission checks went from 12 ms to under 1 ms as a side effect.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Decorator pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern that adds responsibilities to an object dynamically by wrapping it in an object of the same interface. Decorators can be stacked.</p> <p>Q2. Give a Java example.</p> <p>Java I/O: <code>new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream("a.txt")))</code>. Each wrapper adds a feature (buffering, decoding) to the stream it wraps.</p> <p>Q3. Why not just use inheritance?</p> <p>Inheritance is static and leads to a subclass for every combination. Decorators are composed at runtime, in any combination, with one class per feature.</p> <p>Q4. Decorator vs. Proxy?</p> <p>Same structure; different intent. Decorator adds behavior; Proxy controls access (lazy loading, security, remote calls, caching).</p> <p>Q5. Which principle does Decorator follow?</p> <p>Open/Closed (extend without modifying) and Single Responsibility (each decorator does one thing).</p> <p>Q1. Does the order of decorators matter?</p> <p>Yes: compress before encrypting, logging inside or outside retry, discounts before tax. Order changes behavior and results.</p> <p>Q2. How does Spring use decorators?</p> <p>AOP proxies wrap beans for <code>@Transactional</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code>, <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Retryable</code>. Also <code>BeanPostProcessor</code>s that wrap beans, and <code>HttpServletRequestWrapper</code> in filters.</p> <p>Q3. How do you write a decorator for an interface with 20 methods?</p> <p>Extend an abstract forwarding base class that delegates all 20 by default; override only what you change.</p> <p>Q4. How do you test decorators?</p> <p>Each one in isolation with a fake inner component (assert it adds its behavior and forwards correctly), plus one test for the composed stack's order.</p> <p>Q5. Write a functional decorator.</p> <p><code>Function&lt;String,String&gt; pipeline = String::trim; pipeline = pipeline.andThen(String::toLowerCase);</code>, or default methods returning wrapping lambdas (Example 3).</p> <p>Q1. "Design a resilient client for a flaky downstream service."</p> <p>Decorators: timeout per attempt, retry with exponential backoff and jitter for idempotent calls, circuit breaker, bulkhead to cap concurrency, fallback (cached response or degraded mode), metrics at each layer. State the order and why, and mention retry budgets to prevent retry storms.</p> <p>Q2. "Multiple layers retry and we had an outage. Explain and fix."</p> <p>Retries multiply across layers (5 × 3 × 3 = 45). Keep one retry layer, add backoff and jitter, a retry budget (for example 10% extra load), circuit breakers, and propagate deadlines so inner layers don't retry past the caller's timeout.</p> <p>Q3. "Decorator vs. AOP: when would you choose explicit decorators?"</p> <p>When composition is domain-specific or per tenant (pricing rules, merchant-specific payment stacks) and should be visible in code and tests. AOP suits uniform, infrastructure-level concerns across many beans.</p> <p>Q4. "How would you add per-tenant encryption to an existing storage service without touching its code?"</p> <p>An <code>EncryptingStore</code> decorator that looks up the tenant's key (envelope encryption: data key encrypted by a tenant KMS key), wraps the existing store, adds key-version metadata for rotation, and is composed in configuration.</p> <p>Q5. "What are the performance implications of deep decorator stacks?"</p> <p>Mostly negligible (JIT inlining). Watch megamorphic call sites, extra allocations per call (e.g. wrapper objects in streams), and buffer copies in I/O stacks. Measure with JFR; collapse layers only if profiling shows it matters.</p> <p>Problem: The product catalog database is slow. Add a read-through cache in front of any <code>ProductCatalog</code> without changing the catalog or its callers. Count hits and misses, and support evicting a SKU when its price changes.</p> Solution CachingCatalog.javaProductCatalog.javaCachingDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.decorator.caching;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/** Challenge solution: transparent read-through cache in front of any catalog. */
public class CachingCatalog implements ProductCatalog {

    private final ProductCatalog inner;
    private final Map&lt;String, String&gt; cache = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();
    private final AtomicInteger hits = new AtomicInteger();
    private final AtomicInteger misses = new AtomicInteger();

    public CachingCatalog(ProductCatalog inner) {
        this.inner = inner;
    }

    @Override
    public String describe(String sku) {
        String cached = cache.get(sku);
        if (cached != null) {
            hits.incrementAndGet();
            return cached;
        }
        misses.incrementAndGet();
        return cache.computeIfAbsent(sku, inner::describe);
    }

    public void evict(String sku) {
        cache.remove(sku);
    }

    public String stats() {
        return "hits=" + hits + " misses=" + misses;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.decorator.caching;

/** Challenge: component interface. */
public interface ProductCatalog {

    String describe(String sku);
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.decorator.caching;

import java.util.List;

public class CachingDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ProductCatalog slowDb = sku -&gt; {
            System.out.println("  (db query for " + sku + ")");
            return sku + ": Salted Butter 500g, INR 285";
        };
        CachingCatalog catalog = new CachingCatalog(slowDb);

        for (String sku : List.of("BUTTER-500", "BUTTER-500", "BUTTER-500")) {
            System.out.println(catalog.describe(sku));
        }
        catalog.evict("BUTTER-500"); // price changed
        System.out.println(catalog.describe("BUTTER-500"));
        System.out.println(catalog.stats());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>  (db query for BUTTER-500)
BUTTER-500: Salted Butter 500g, INR 285
BUTTER-500: Salted Butter 500g, INR 285
BUTTER-500: Salted Butter 500g, INR 285
  (db query for BUTTER-500)
BUTTER-500: Salted Butter 500g, INR 285
hits=2 misses=2
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: same interface, so callers don't change; <code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent</code> makes concurrent misses for the same SKU load once; eviction keeps the cache correct after price changes. Production follow-ups: size limit and TTL (use Caffeine), and don't cache <code>null</code>s or errors.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: A retry storm across three layers turned a slow database into a two-hour outage.</li> <li>T: Make the client stack safe by design.</li> <li>A: Mapped every decorator layer, removed two retry layers, added backoff with jitter, a retry budget and a circuit breaker inside the remaining retry; documented the stack order in an ADR and in tests.</li> <li>R: The next database slowdown caused 4 minutes of degraded responses instead of an outage.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Facade pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern that provides a simple, unified interface to a complex subsystem, so clients don't deal with its many classes directly.</p> <p>Q2. Give an example.</p> <p>A <code>CheckoutFacade.placeOrder()</code> hiding inventory, payment, delivery and notification services; in Java, SLF4J and Spring's <code>JdbcTemplate</code>.</p> <p>Q3. Does a facade prevent access to subsystems?</p> <p>No. It simplifies common use; advanced clients can still use the subsystems directly.</p> <p>Q4. Facade vs. Adapter?</p> <p>Facade simplifies many classes behind a new interface. Adapter converts one class's interface to the one a client expects.</p> <p>Q5. Which principle does Facade support?</p> <p>Law of Demeter (talk to few friends), loose coupling, and Single Responsibility (the workflow lives in one place).</p> <p>Q1. Is a Spring <code>@Service</code> a facade?</p> <p>Often, yes: a service method that orchestrates several repositories and clients for one use case is a facade, often with <code>@Transactional</code> around it.</p> <p>Q2. How do you stop a facade becoming a god class?</p> <p>One facade per use case group or bounded context; keep business rules in domain objects; split when it grows unrelated methods.</p> <p>Q3. How do you test a facade?</p> <p>Inject fakes for subsystems; test the workflow and failure paths (payment fails, so stock is released), not the subsystems themselves.</p> <p>Q4. Facade vs. Mediator?</p> <p>A facade is one-way: clients call it, subsystems don't know it. A mediator coordinates two-way communication between colleagues that know the mediator.</p> <p>Q5. Can a facade call services in parallel?</p> <p>Yes, if the calls are independent: <code>CompletableFuture.allOf(...)</code> or structured concurrency with virtual threads. Total latency becomes the slowest call rather than the sum.</p> <p>Q1. "Design the backend for a mobile app home screen that needs data from 8 services."</p> <p>A BFF (backend-for-frontend) facade: one endpoint per screen, parallel fan-out with per-call timeouts, partial responses (show what you have; a missing "recommendations" section is fine), caching, and response shaping for mobile (small payloads). Discuss versioning for old app versions.</p> <p>Q2. "Your checkout facade calls 4 services over the network. How do you keep data consistent?"</p> <p>No distributed transaction: use a saga (orchestrated by the facade/orchestrator) with compensations, idempotency keys, an outbox for reliable events, reservation TTLs as a safety net, and reconciliation jobs.</p> <p>Q3. "When is a facade an anti-pattern in microservices?"</p> <p>When it becomes a distributed monolith chokepoint that every change must go through, or when it holds business logic that belongs in the domain services. Keep facades thin: orchestration and shaping, not core rules.</p> <p>Q4. "How do facades relate to Hexagonal/Clean architecture?"</p> <p>Application services (use cases) are facades over the domain and ports. Inbound adapters (REST controllers, message listeners) call them; they coordinate domain objects and outbound ports.</p> <p>Q5. "Latency budget: p99 must be under 300 ms, and the facade calls 5 services. Approach?"</p> <p>Run independent calls in parallel, give each a timeout within the budget, use hedged requests for tail latency, cache stable data (entitlements, CDN maps), and degrade gracefully (no subtitles is better than no playback).</p> <p>Problem (ride-hailing): Booking a ride needs a fare (with 1.5× surge at peak), a payment hold for that fare, and a driver match. If no driver is found, the hold must be released. Give the app a single <code>book(...)</code> call.</p> Solution RideBookingFacade.javaRideDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.facade.ride;

/** Challenge solution: one call to book a ride, hiding pricing, matching and payment holds. */
public class RideBookingFacade {

    static class SurgePricing {
        int fareInr(int km, boolean peak) {
            int base = 50 + 14 * km;
            return peak ? base * 3 / 2 : base;
        }
    }

    static class DriverMatcher {
        String nearestDriver(String pickup) {
            return pickup.equals("Airport T2") ? null : "Driver Ramesh (KA-01-AB-1234), 4 min away";
        }
    }

    static class PaymentHold {
        String hold(String rider, int amount) {
            return "HOLD-" + rider + "-" + amount;
        }

        void releaseHold(String holdId) {
            System.out.println("  released " + holdId);
        }
    }

    private final SurgePricing pricing = new SurgePricing();
    private final DriverMatcher matcher = new DriverMatcher();
    private final PaymentHold payments = new PaymentHold();

    public String book(String rider, String pickup, int km, boolean peak) {
        int fare = pricing.fareInr(km, peak);
        String hold = payments.hold(rider, fare);
        String driver = matcher.nearestDriver(pickup);
        if (driver == null) {
            payments.releaseHold(hold);
            return "No drivers near " + pickup + ", you were not charged";
        }
        return "Booked: " + driver + ", fare INR " + fare + (peak ? " (1.5x surge)" : "");
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.facade.ride;

public class RideDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RideBookingFacade rides = new RideBookingFacade();
        System.out.println(rides.book("R-9", "MG Road", 8, false));
        System.out.println(rides.book("R-9", "MG Road", 8, true));
        System.out.println(rides.book("R-9", "Airport T2", 35, false));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Booked: Driver Ramesh (KA-01-AB-1234), 4 min away, fare INR 162
Booked: Driver Ramesh (KA-01-AB-1234), 4 min away, fare INR 243 (1.5x surge)
  released HOLD-R-9-540
No drivers near Airport T2, you were not charged
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: one task-level method; the compensation (release the hold) lives in the facade so no client can forget it; subsystems stay independent and reusable. Discussion point: matching before holding avoids the compensation entirely, so ask which order the business prefers (holding first guarantees payment before a driver commits).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Three client apps implemented checkout separately; stock stayed reserved after failed payments during a sale.</li> <li>T: Fix the bug everywhere and stop it from recurring.</li> <li>A: Built a server-side checkout facade with compensation, migrated the apps to one endpoint behind a feature flag, and added reservation TTLs.</li> <li>R: "Phantom out-of-stock" incidents went to zero; checkout changes now ship once instead of three times.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Flyweight pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern that reduces memory by sharing common parts of many similar objects instead of storing them in every object.</p> <p>Q2. What are intrinsic and extrinsic state?</p> <p>Intrinsic is shared and immutable, stored in the flyweight (icon image). Extrinsic is unique per use, stored outside or passed in (position).</p> <p>Q3. Give a Java example.</p> <p><code>Integer.valueOf()</code> caches -128 to 127; the String pool; enum constants.</p> <p>Q4. Why is <code>Integer a = 127, b = 127; a == b</code> true but 128 false?</p> <p>Autoboxing uses <code>Integer.valueOf</code>, which returns cached flyweights for -128 to 127; 128 creates new objects. Use <code>equals()</code> for wrappers.</p> <p>Q5. Why must flyweights be immutable?</p> <p>They're shared: a change through one reference would affect every user.</p> <p>Q1. How do you implement a thread-safe flyweight factory?</p> <p><code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent(key, Factory::create)</code>, so each key is created once even under concurrency; flyweights themselves are immutable.</p> <p>Q2. When is Flyweight not worth it?</p> <p>With few objects, little shared state, or when the shared part is small compared to the per-object overhead. Measure first.</p> <p>Q3. Flyweight vs. Singleton?</p> <p>Singleton is one instance of a class; flyweights are one instance per distinct intrinsic state, many classes of usage sharing a few objects.</p> <p>Q4. Flyweight vs. Object Pool?</p> <p>Pools lend mutable objects exclusively and take them back; flyweights are immutable and used by everyone at once.</p> <p>Q5. How do records help?</p> <p>Records give value-based <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> and immutability, so interning is just <code>pool.computeIfAbsent(candidate, k -&gt; k)</code>.</p> <p>Q1. "Our service's heap is 70% duplicate strings. What do you do?"</p> <p>Confirm with MAT's duplicate-strings report. Options: G1 string deduplication, interning a bounded set of known values (enums, codes, country names), dictionary-encoding data loaded from files or databases, and fixing the source (deserializers creating new strings for repeated values).</p> <p>Q2. "Render 1 million points on a map in a browser or a Java client."</p> <p>Flyweight for shared styles and icons; structure-of-arrays for coordinates (<code>float[]</code>); level-of-detail clustering; render on the GPU. Past a certain scale, data-oriented design beats object-per-point.</p> <p>Q3. "How does a columnar database use the Flyweight idea?"</p> <p>Dictionary encoding: a column with repeated values stores each distinct value once and small integer codes per row; the same intrinsic/extrinsic split, applied to storage.</p> <p>Q4. "Design an interning service for user-generated tags under adversarial load."</p> <p>Validate and normalize input; intern only allow-listed or popular tags; a bounded cache with weak values for the rest; monitor pool size; rate-limit tag creation per user.</p> <p>Q5. "Where else do you see Flyweight in system design?"</p> <p>CDNs (one cached asset served to millions), container image layers (shared base layers), copy-on-write memory pages after <code>fork()</code>, database normalization.</p> <p>Problem (social media): Posts contain hashtags like <code>#Java</code>, <code>#java</code>, <code>#JAVA</code>. Implement <code>Hashtag.of(raw)</code> that normalizes (trim, lowercase), validates, and returns one shared instance per tag, safe under concurrency.</p> Solution Hashtag.javaHashtagDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.flyweight.social;

import java.util.Locale;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

/** Challenge solution: interned, case-insensitive hashtags, safe under concurrency. */
public final class Hashtag {

    private static final ConcurrentMap&lt;String, Hashtag&gt; CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();

    private final String normalized;

    private Hashtag(String normalized) {
        this.normalized = normalized;
    }

    public static Hashtag of(String raw) {
        String key = raw.strip().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
        if (!key.matches("#[a-z0-9_]{1,50}")) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not a hashtag: " + raw);
        }
        return CACHE.computeIfAbsent(key, Hashtag::new);
    }

    public static int cached() {
        return CACHE.size();
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return normalized;
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.flyweight.social;

import java.util.List;

public class HashtagDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;String&gt; postsTags = List.of("#Java", "#java", " #JAVA ", "#SpringBoot", "#springboot", "#Java21");
        List&lt;Hashtag&gt; tags = postsTags.stream().map(Hashtag::of).toList();
        System.out.println("Tags used      : " + tags);
        System.out.println("Unique objects : " + Hashtag.cached());
        System.out.println("#Java == #JAVA ? " + (Hashtag.of("#Java") == Hashtag.of("#JAVA")));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>Tags used      : [#java, #java, #java, #springboot, #springboot, #java21]
Unique objects : 3
#Java == #JAVA ? true
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: private constructor so every instance comes from <code>of()</code>; normalization before lookup so variants share one object; <code>Locale.ROOT</code> so lowercasing doesn't depend on the server's language (Turkish "I"); validation bounds the key space; and in production, a size-limited cache instead of an unbounded map (see the war story).</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Our pricing service needed 28 GB of heap and had long GC pauses.</li> <li>T: Cut memory without a rewrite.</li> <li>A: Heap analysis showed catalog text duplicated per store listing; split listings into shared <code>ProductInfo</code> flyweights and per-store records, loaded through a bounded cache.</li> <li>R: Heap dropped to 6 GB; p99 GC pause went from 1.8 s to 120 ms; we downsized the instances and saved about 40% on that service's compute bill.</li> </ul>

🟢 Beginner / Service Companies🟡 Mid-Level🔴 Senior / FAANG / SAP💻 Coding Challenge <p>Q1. What is the Proxy pattern?</p> <p>A structural pattern where a surrogate object with the same interface controls access to a real object, for lazy creation, security, remote access, caching or logging.</p> <p>Q2. Types of proxies?</p> <p>Virtual (lazy), protection (security), remote (another process), caching, logging/smart reference.</p> <p>Q3. Proxy vs. Decorator?</p> <p>Same structure. Proxy controls access (may not call the real object at all, often creates it); Decorator adds behavior and is composed by the client.</p> <p>Q4. Give real examples.</p> <p>Spring <code>@Transactional</code> proxies, Hibernate lazy loading, gRPC/Feign client stubs, and Java's <code>java.lang.reflect.Proxy</code>.</p> <p>Q5. What is a dynamic proxy in Java?</p> <p>A proxy class generated at runtime by <code>Proxy.newProxyInstance</code> for one or more interfaces, with all calls routed to an <code>InvocationHandler</code>.</p> <p>Q1. How does Spring implement <code>@Transactional</code>?</p> <p>It wraps the bean in a proxy (JDK or CGLIB). The proxy starts a transaction, calls the real method, and commits or rolls back (on runtime exceptions by default).</p> <p>Q2. Why does <code>@Transactional</code> not work on a method called from the same class?</p> <p>Self-invocation calls <code>this.method()</code> on the real object, bypassing the proxy. Move the method to another bean or call through the proxy.</p> <p>Q3. JDK dynamic proxy vs. CGLIB?</p> <p>JDK proxies need interfaces; CGLIB creates a subclass, so it works without interfaces but not for <code>final</code> classes or methods.</p> <p>Q4. What causes <code>LazyInitializationException</code>?</p> <p>Accessing a Hibernate lazy proxy after the session is closed. Fetch needed data in the transaction (join fetch, entity graphs, DTOs).</p> <p>Q5. What should a remote proxy handle besides marshalling?</p> <p>Timeouts, retries (idempotent calls only), error translation to domain exceptions, auth headers, and tracing context propagation.</p> <p>Q1. "Design client libraries for 30 internal microservices."</p> <p>Generate remote proxies from contracts (OpenAPI/Protobuf); a shared runtime layer for timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, auth, tracing, metrics; batch endpoints to avoid chattiness; versioning; and testing with contract tests and generated fakes.</p> <p>Q2. "A service mesh sidecar vs. in-process proxies (libraries): trade-offs?"</p> <p>Sidecar (Envoy): language-agnostic, uniform mTLS, retries and telemetry, changed without redeploying apps; but extra hop latency, resource cost, and operational complexity. Library: lower latency and richer semantics, but must be implemented per language and upgraded per service.</p> <p>Q3. "Lazy loading caused an N+1 storm in production. How do you find and fix it, and prevent it?"</p> <p>Find via SQL logs/APM (one query per row) or Hibernate statistics. Fix with join fetch / entity graphs / batch fetching (<code>@BatchSize</code>) or DTO projections. Prevent with tests that assert query counts and with lazy-by-default mappings plus explicit fetch plans.</p> <p>Q4. "Design authorization for a multi-tenant SaaS API."</p> <p>A policy enforcement point at the edge (gateway: authentication, coarse scopes) and in-process protection proxies or AOP (<code>@PreAuthorize</code>) for fine-grained checks, with a central policy decision point (OPA, Zanzibar-style). Always enforce tenant isolation in the data layer too (row-level security), not only in proxies.</p> <p>Q5. "When would you avoid proxies?"</p> <p>When the surprise cost is high: hot paths where the indirection matters, code that depends on object identity, <code>final</code>-heavy designs (records, sealed classes can't be CGLIB-proxied), and when explicit code (a decorator or a direct call) would be clearer.</p> <p>Problem: Write one generic proxy that logs every method call, its arguments and its result (or exception) for any interface, without writing a class per interface. Make sure callers still see the original exception type.</p> Solution: JDK dynamic proxy LoggingProxy.javaDynamicProxyDemo.java <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.proxy.dynamic;

import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

/** Challenge solution: one generic proxy that logs every call on ANY interface. */
public final class LoggingProxy {

    private LoggingProxy() {
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static &lt;T&gt; T wrap(T target, Class&lt;T&gt; iface, Consumer&lt;String&gt; log) {
        return (T) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
                iface.getClassLoader(),
                new Class&lt;?&gt;[] {iface},
                (proxy, method, args) -&gt; {
                    String call = iface.getSimpleName() + "." + method.getName()
                            + (args == null ? "()" : Arrays.toString(args).replace('[', '(').replace(']', ')'));
                    try {
                        Object result = method.invoke(target, args);
                        log.accept(call + " -&gt; " + result);
                        return result;
                    } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
                        log.accept(call + " threw " + e.getCause());
                        throw e.getCause(); // (1)!
                    }
                });
    }

    public interface Greeter {
        String greet(String name);

        List&lt;String&gt; languages();
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li><code>Method.invoke</code> wraps exceptions in <code>InvocationTargetException</code>. Rethrowing <code>getCause()</code> gives callers the original exception (<code>SecurityException</code>), so the proxy stays transparent. Forgetting this is the classic dynamic-proxy bug.</li> </ol> <pre><code>package com.handbook.structural.proxy.dynamic;

import com.handbook.structural.proxy.banking.AccountService;
import com.handbook.structural.proxy.banking.CoreAccountService;
import com.handbook.structural.proxy.dynamic.LoggingProxy.Greeter;

import java.util.List;

public class DynamicProxyDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountService accounts = LoggingProxy.wrap(new CoreAccountService(), AccountService.class,
                line -&gt; System.out.println("[audit] " + line));
        accounts.balance("SB-1001");
        accounts.withdraw("SB-1001", 1_000);

        Greeter greeter = LoggingProxy.wrap(new Greeter() {
            public String greet(String name) { return "Namaste, " + name; }
            public List&lt;String&gt; languages() { return List.of("hi", "en"); }
        }, Greeter.class, line -&gt; System.out.println("[audit] " + line));
        greeter.greet("Asha");
        greeter.languages();
        System.out.println("Proxy class: " + (greeter.getClass().getSimpleName().startsWith("$Proxy") ? "$ProxyN (generated at runtime)" : greeter.getClass()));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>[audit] AccountService.balance(SB-1001) -&gt; 250000
[audit] AccountService.withdraw(SB-1001, 1000) -&gt; withdrew INR 1000, new balance INR 249000
[audit] Greeter.greet(Asha) -&gt; Namaste, Asha
[audit] Greeter.languages() -&gt; [hi, en]
Proxy class: $ProxyN (generated at runtime)
</code></pre> <p>Key points to say: <code>Proxy.newProxyInstance(classLoader, interfaces, handler)</code>; one handler serves every interface; exceptions are unwrapped; works only with interfaces (for classes you'd use ByteBuddy or CGLIB). This is how Spring AOP, Mockito and Feign work underneath.</p> <p>Behavioural tie-in (STAR) for experienced candidates</p> <ul> <li>S: Wallet transfers occasionally lost money; logs showed debits without matching credits.</li> <li>T: Find the root cause and guarantee atomic transfers.</li> <li>A: Found self-invocation bypassing <code>@Transactional</code> proxies; restructured into one transactional use case and a separate ledger bean; added rollback integration tests and an ArchUnit rule against <code>@Transactional</code> on private and self-called methods.</li> <li>R: Zero ledger mismatches since; the rule caught two similar bugs in other services within a month.</li> </ul>
